      "prompt": "The numerical coefficient of the highest-degree term when a polynomial is written in standard form (descending powers).",
      "choices": [
        "standard deviation",
        "degree",
        "monomial",
        "leading coefficient"
      ],
      "correctText": "leading coefficient",
      "topic": "Polynomial Vocabulary",
      "explanation": "Leading coefficient is the front number in standard form. Worked examples: in 3x^2-5x+1, the leading coefficient is 3; in -4x^3+x-7, it is -4. NY A-APR.1 references this. The sign of the leading coefficient for a quadratic determines whether the parabola opens up (positive) or down (negative). Regents items use this to interpret quadratic graphs and to test standard-form recognition.",
