      "correctText": "principal root",
      "topic": "Rational Exponents",
      "explanation": "Principal root is the standard positive choice. Worked example: sqrt(36) = 6 (principal), not -6 — even though (-6)^2 = 36. NY N-RN.2 follows this convention. Regents items use sqrt without ambiguity to mean the principal root. The equation x^2 = 36 has two solutions (plus and minus 6), but the function sqrt(36) returns only 6. Cube roots (and odd indices) have no ambiguity — cbrt(8) = 2 only.",
