      "correctText": "extraneous solution",
      "topic": "Regents Error Analysis",
      "explanation": "Extraneous solutions arise from non-reversible operations. Worked example: solving sqrt(x)=x-2 by squaring gives x=x^2-4x+4, so x^2-5x+4=0, factoring (x-1)(x-4)=0, giving x=1 or x=4. Checking: x=1 fails (sqrt(1) not equal -1), so x=1 is extraneous. Only x=4 is valid. NY A-REI.2 explicitly addresses this. Regents radical and rational items require students to check final answers and discard extraneous ones.",
