      "explanation": "Domain restrictions arise from context and from algebra. Worked example: in t=d/r, r cannot equal 0 (division undefined). For a population model P=20+5t, t is greater-than-or-equal 0 since time is non-negative. NY F-IF.5 covers interpreting domain in context. Regents items expect students to state restrictions explicitly: 'x is greater-than-or-equal 0 because x represents the number of students sold tickets, which cannot be negative.'",
