      "correctText": "powers of ten",
      "topic": "Scientific Notation",
      "explanation": "Powers of ten are 10, 100, 1000 ... and 0.1, 0.01, 0.001. Worked example: 10^3 = 1000 (three zeros), 10^-2 = 0.01 (two decimal places). NY 8.EE.1 covers exponent rules with base 10. Each power of ten shifts the decimal one place. Regents items test arithmetic with powers of 10 (multiplying 4 * 10^5 by 3 * 10^2 equals 12 * 10^7 = 1.2 * 10^8). Always renormalize the mantissa to be at least 1 and less than 10.",
