      "correctText": "polynomial zero",
      "topic": "Algebraic Structure",
      "explanation": "A polynomial zero is a value x=r where P(r)=0 (A-APR.3). Tools to find zeros: (1) factoring (try GCF, special products, grouping); (2) quadratic formula for degree 2; (3) Rational Root Theorem to list rational candidates p/q where p|constant and q|leading; (4) synthetic division to confirm and reduce; (5) calculator zero-finder. Example: 2x³-5x²+x+2; RRT candidates ±1, ±2, ±1/2; testing x=2: 16-20+2+2=0 ✓; synthetic division gives 2x²-x-1=(2x+1)(x-1), zeros x=-1/2, 1. All zeros: -1/2, 1, 2. On the cumulative Algebra 2 Regents review, polynomial zero-finding is a foundational skill tested in factoring, identifying x-intercepts, and counting real vs complex roots.",
