      "correctText": "slant asymptote",
      "topic": "Asymptotes and Holes",
      "explanation": "A slant (oblique) asymptote arises when deg(numerator) = deg(denominator) + 1 (F-IF.7d). To find it, perform polynomial division: the quotient (ignoring the remainder) is the slant asymptote. Example: f(x)=(x²-3x+2)/(x-1). Long division: x²-3x+2 ÷ (x-1) = x-2 with remainder 0. So f(x)=x-2 with hole at x=1. For (x²+1)/(x), division gives x+1/x; as x→±∞, 1/x→0 and y→x is the slant asymptote. On the Algebra 2 Regents, slant asymptotes appear in graphing problems for cubic-over-quadratic or quadratic-over-linear rational functions; division is the required tool to identify them.",
      "domain": "Review",
