      "prompt": "Line x=a where a rational function's graph approaches ±∞, occurring when the denominator equals zero but the numerator does not.",
      "choices": [
        "expected value",
        "horizontal asymptote",
        "vertical asymptote",
        "hole in graph"
      ],
      "correctText": "vertical asymptote",
      "topic": "Asymptotes and Holes",
      "explanation": "A vertical asymptote occurs at x=a when (x-a) is a factor of the denominator but not the numerator (F-IF.7d). For f(x)=(x+2)/(x²-1)=(x+2)/((x-1)(x+1)), vertical asymptotes are at x=1 and x=-1. As x approaches an asymptote, |f(x)|→∞. Determine the direction (±∞) by examining one-sided behavior: for f(x)=1/(x-2), as x→2⁺, f→+∞; as x→2⁻, f→-∞. On the Algebra 2 Regents, vertical asymptotes are a frequent topic in graphing rational functions and in identifying domain restrictions. Always factor before identifying asymptotes—a denominator zero that cancels with a numerator zero produces a hole, not an asymptote.",
