      "correctText": "horizontal asymptote",
      "topic": "Asymptotes and Holes",
      "explanation": "A horizontal asymptote describes f(x) as x→±∞ (F-IF.7d). Three cases for f(x)=p(x)/q(x): (1) deg p < deg q: horizontal asymptote y=0. (2) deg p = deg q: y=(leading coefficient of p)/(leading coefficient of q). Example: (3x²+1)/(2x²-x) has asymptote y=3/2. (3) deg p > deg q: no horizontal asymptote (there's a slant or curve asymptote instead). On the Algebra 2 Regents, students determine horizontal asymptotes from degree comparisons and use them to sketch end behavior of rational functions. A function may cross its horizontal asymptote at intermediate x-values, unlike vertical asymptotes.",
