      "correctText": "removable discontinuity",
      "topic": "Asymptotes and Holes",
      "explanation": "A removable discontinuity is a hole that could be 'patched' by defining the function at that point to match the limit (F-IF.7d). For f(x)=(x²-1)/(x-1)=x+1 with x≠1, the limit as x→1 is 2, so defining f(1)=2 removes the discontinuity. Non-removable discontinuities are vertical asymptotes (jump to ∞) and jumps (left and right limits disagree). On the Algebra 2 Regents, the concept of removability links to factoring: if (x-a) cancels, the discontinuity is removable; if (x-a) survives in the denominator, the discontinuity is non-removable (a vertical asymptote). This idea preludes calculus's formal limit definition.",
