      "explanation": "Function composition feeds g's output into f as input (F-BF.1). If f(x)=2x+1 and g(x)=x², then (f∘g)(x)=f(x²)=2x²+1, but (g∘f)(x)=g(2x+1)=(2x+1)²=4x²+4x+1. Composition is not commutative. The domain of f∘g requires both that x is in the domain of g and g(x) is in the domain of f. Example: if g(x)=√x and f(x)=1/x, then (f∘g)(x)=1/√x with domain x>0. On the Algebra 2 Regents, composition shows up in symbolic evaluation, real-world chains (cost→tax→tip), and verifying inverses (f(f⁻¹(x))=x).",
