      "explanation": "Average rate of change is the slope of the secant line from (a,f(a)) to (b,f(b)), computed as [f(b)-f(a)]/(b-a) (F-IF.6). For f(x)=x² on [1,4]: [f(4)-f(1)]/(4-1)=(16-1)/3=5. This is the Algebra 2 generalization of slope: for linear functions it equals the (constant) slope; for non-linear functions it varies by interval. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students compare average rates of change across function families to argue linear vs exponential behavior, or to interpret average growth/decay in real contexts. Be careful: 'average rate of change' is not the instantaneous rate (that's calculus); it is a two-point computation.",
