      "explanation": "Inverse composition gives the identity: f(f⁻¹(x))=x and f⁻¹(f(x))=x when each is defined (F-BF.4). This is the formal definition that establishes inverses on the Regents. Example: for f(x)=3x-7, f⁻¹(x)=(x+7)/3; check f(f⁻¹(x))=3·(x+7)/3-7=x+7-7=x. Inverse composition is a quick verification: students who derive an inverse can confirm by composing in either order and checking that the result simplifies to x. Failing this check means an algebra error in the inversion process. For exponential and logarithmic pairs: 2^(log₂ x)=x for x>0, and log₂(2^x)=x for all x.",
