      "correctText": "binomial coefficient",
      "topic": "Counting",
      "explanation": "A binomial coefficient C(n,k)=ⁿCₖ=n!/(k!(n-k)!) (S-CP.9). Appears in the Binomial Theorem: (a+b)ⁿ=Σ(k=0 to n) ⁿCₖ a^(n-k) b^k. Example: (a+b)⁴=a⁴+4a³b+6a²b²+4ab³+b⁴ with coefficients 1, 4, 6, 4, 1 (row 4 of Pascal's triangle). The coefficient of x⁵ in (1+x)¹⁰ is ¹⁰C₅=252. Pascal's triangle generates binomial coefficients row by row, each entry the sum of the two above. Symmetric: C(n,k)=C(n,n-k). On the Algebra 2 Regents, binomial coefficients appear in expanding (a+b)ⁿ for small n, in probability distributions (binomial: P(X=k)=C(n,k)·p^k·(1-p)^(n-k)), and counting subsets.",
