      "correctText": "factorial",
      "topic": "Counting",
      "explanation": "Factorial n!=n(n-1)(n-2)···1 counts the number of orderings of n distinct objects (S-CP.9). 0!=1 by convention (empty product). Examples: 0!=1, 1!=1, 2!=2, 3!=6, 4!=24, 5!=120, 6!=720, 10!=3,628,800. Calculator key: x! or ! (in MATH menu). Grows extremely fast: 20!≈2.43×10¹⁸. Stirling's approximation n!≈√(2πn)(n/e)ⁿ. Useful identities: n!=n·(n-1)!; 0!=1 makes formulas work. On the Algebra 2 Regents, factorials appear in permutation and combination formulas, and in series like the Maclaurin expansions referenced in enrichment. The number of arrangements of all letters in a word with k repeats is n!/(repeated counts).",
