      "correctText": "permutation",
      "topic": "Counting",
      "explanation": "A permutation is an ordered arrangement: ⁿPᵣ=n!/(n-r)! (S-CP.9). Example: number of ways to arrange 3 of 5 books on a shelf: ₅P₃=5!/2!=60. The full arrangement of n items: n!. For unrestricted ordering of all items, ⁿPₙ=n!. With repetition allowed: nʳ (each of r positions independently filled from n). For circular permutations: (n-1)! to account for rotational symmetry. On the Algebra 2 Regents, permutations apply when order matters: race finishes (gold, silver, bronze), seating arrangements, license plates, lock combinations. Distinguishing 'order matters' (permutation) from 'order doesn't matter' (combination) is the first step in counting problems.",
