      "correctText": "counting principle",
      "topic": "Counting",
      "explanation": "The Fundamental Counting Principle (multiplication principle): if step 1 has m outcomes and step 2 has n outcomes (independent of step 1), then there are m·n total outcomes (S-CP.9). Generalizes to k steps: total = m₁·m₂·...·mₖ. Example: choosing an outfit with 5 shirts and 4 pants: 5·4=20 outfits. License plate with 3 letters then 4 digits: 26³·10⁴=175,760,000. Both permutations (ⁿPᵣ=n·(n-1)·...) and combinations (ⁿCᵣ=ⁿPᵣ/r!) build on this principle. On the Algebra 2 Regents, the counting principle is the foundation for nearly all enumeration problems; students apply it before resorting to permutation/combination formulas in complex cases.",
