      "correctText": "trigonometric equation",
      "topic": "Equations",
      "explanation": "A trigonometric equation has unknown angles inside trig functions (F-TF.7). Most have infinitely many solutions due to periodicity, but problems usually specify an interval (e.g., 0≤x<2π). Standard solution: (1) isolate the trig function, (2) find reference angle, (3) use unit circle to locate all solutions in given interval. Example: 2sin x=1 → sin x=1/2 → reference angle π/6 → solutions in [0,2π) are π/6 and 5π/6 (both quadrants where sin is positive). For unrestricted intervals: general solution sin x=1/2 → x=π/6+2πk or x=5π/6+2πk for integer k. On the Algebra 2 Regents, every trig equation specifies a domain interval.",
