      "correctText": "general solution",
      "topic": "Equations",
      "explanation": "A general solution lists all angles satisfying a trig equation across all of ℝ, using +2πk (or +πk for tangent) for integer k (F-TF.7). Example: cos x=1/2 → x=π/3+2πk or x=-π/3+2πk for integer k. Compactly: x=±π/3+2πk. For tan x=1 (period π): x=π/4+πk. For sin x=0: x=πk. On the Algebra 2 Regents, general solutions are sometimes required but more often students provide solutions in a specific interval like [0,2π). The general solution form ensures completeness by accounting for periodicity—a frequent source of point loss when students forget to add +2πk or omit other quadrant solutions.",
