      "correctText": "inverse trig function",
      "topic": "Equations",
      "explanation": "Inverse trig functions return angles: sin⁻¹(x)=arcsin(x) is the angle θ in [-π/2, π/2] with sin θ=x (F-TF.8). Domain [-1,1]; range [-π/2, π/2]. cos⁻¹(x): domain [-1,1], range [0,π]. tan⁻¹(x): domain ℝ, range (-π/2, π/2). The range restrictions are necessary because trig functions aren't one-to-one over their full domains. Examples: sin⁻¹(1/2)=π/6; cos⁻¹(-1)=π; tan⁻¹(1)=π/4. Calculator inverses respect these principal values: sin⁻¹(0.7)≈0.775 rad. On the Algebra 2 Regents, inverse trig functions arise in solving trig equations and in modeling: given a triangle side ratio, find the angle.",
