      "correctText": "pH calculation",
      "topic": "Exponential and Practice",
      "explanation": "pH=-log₁₀[H⁺] measures acidity on a logarithmic scale (F-BF.5 application). [H⁺] is hydrogen ion concentration in mol/L. pH=7 is neutral (pure water, [H⁺]=10⁻⁷). pH<7 acidic; pH>7 basic. Each pH unit is a factor of 10: lemon juice pH 2 has 100x more H⁺ than tomato juice pH 4. From pH=5.3: [H⁺]=10^(-5.3)≈5.0×10⁻⁶ M. From [H⁺]=2.5×10⁻⁹: pH=-log(2.5×10⁻⁹)≈8.6. On the Algebra 2 Regents, pH is a canonical real-world application of logarithms, illustrating compression of orders-of-magnitude data and giving practice with negative-exponent scientific notation. Similar log scales: Richter (earthquake), decibel (sound).",
