      "explanation": "Radioactive decay follows N(t)=N₀(1/2)^(t/h) or equivalently N(t)=N₀·e^(-kt) where k=ln(2)/h (F-IF.8b). Half-life h is the time for the substance to reduce by half. Famous examples: Carbon-14 (5730 yr, used in archaeological dating), Iodine-131 (8.02 days, medical thermography), Uranium-238 (4.5 billion yr, age of Earth). To date a sample with 30% of original Carbon-14 remaining: 0.30=(1/2)^(t/5730) → t=5730·log(0.3)/log(0.5)≈9953 years. On the Algebra 2 Regents, radioactive decay problems test exponential function setup, calculator use, and logarithmic solution of time variables.",
