      "explanation": "A logarithm is the inverse of exponentiation: log_b(x)=y means b^y=x (F-BF.5). Example: log₂(8)=3 because 2³=8. log₁₀(1000)=3 because 10³=1000. The logarithm function y=log_b(x) is defined only for x>0 and b>0, b≠1. Three laws: log_b(MN)=log_b(M)+log_b(N), log_b(M/N)=log_b(M)-log_b(N), log_b(M^p)=p·log_b(M). The change-of-base formula: log_b(x)=log(x)/log(b)=ln(x)/ln(b). On the Algebra 2 Regents, logarithms appear in solving exponential equations, computing pH and decibels, and inverting exponential functions. The notation log without a subscript usually means log₁₀ (common log); ln means log_e (natural log).",
