      "explanation": "A sequence is an ordered list of numbers, often called terms, indexed by positive integers (F-IF.3, F-BF.2). Sequences can be finite (3, 5, 7, 9, 11) or infinite (1, 1/2, 1/4, 1/8, ...). The nth term is denoted aₙ. Sequences may be specified explicitly (aₙ=2n+1 gives 3, 5, 7, 9, ...) or recursively (a₁=3, aₙ=aₙ₋₁+2). Functions in disguise: a sequence is a function whose domain is the positive integers. On the Algebra 2 Regents, common types: arithmetic (constant difference), geometric (constant ratio), and Fibonacci-style recursive. Distinguishing types and writing explicit/recursive formulas are tested skills.",
