      "topic": "Function Behavior",
      "explanation": "A rational function is a quotient of polynomials (F-IF.7d). Domain excludes zeros of Q(x). Key features include: vertical asymptotes (where Q(x)=0 and P(x)≠0), horizontal or slant asymptotes (controlled by degrees), holes (where common factors cancel), x-intercepts (where P(x)=0 with Q(x)≠0), and y-intercept (value at x=0, if defined). Example: f(x)=(x-1)/(x²-1)=(x-1)/((x-1)(x+1))=1/(x+1) for x≠1; vertical asymptote x=-1, hole at x=1. On the Algebra 2 Regents, rational functions are a major Unit 3 topic with graph-matching and feature-identification items.",
