      "correctText": "one-to-one function",
      "topic": "Function Vocabulary",
      "explanation": "A one-to-one function pairs distinct inputs with distinct outputs, so no horizontal line crosses its graph more than once (F-BF.4). One-to-one functions are exactly those that have inverse functions that are themselves functions. f(x)=x³ is one-to-one (passes horizontal line test); f(x)=x² is not, because f(2)=f(-2)=4. To make x² invertible we restrict its domain to x≥0, giving the inverse √x. On the Regents, one-to-one questions appear in inverse-function items (find f⁻¹, state any domain restriction required) and in interpretation tasks where students must explain why a function cannot be inverted globally.",
