      "correctText": "confidence interval",
      "topic": "Inference Ideas",
      "explanation": "A confidence interval gives a range of plausible values for a population parameter, with a stated confidence level (S-IC.4). For a population proportion: p̂ ± z·√(p̂(1-p̂)/n), often using z=1.96 for 95%. Example: a sample of 400 finds 55% support; 95% CI is 0.55±1.96·√(0.55·0.45/400)≈(0.501, 0.599). Interpretation: 'We are 95% confident the true population proportion is between 50.1% and 59.9%.' Wider CI = more confidence but less precision; narrower CI = less confidence or larger sample. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students compute, interpret, and compare CIs. The confidence level is a long-run frequency: 95% of CIs constructed this way will contain the true parameter.",
