      "correctText": "common ratio",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "The common ratio r of a geometric sequence equals aₙ₊₁/aₙ for all n (F-LE.2). Example: in 16, 8, 4, 2, 1, ..., r=8/16=4/8=1/2. r>1 gives exponential growth; 0<r<1 gives decay; r<0 gives alternating signs; r=1 makes all terms equal (constant); r=0 makes terms after the first all zero. The sign of r affects oscillation: r=-2 gives 1, -2, 4, -8, 16, .... On the Algebra 2 Regents, students compute r from given terms (divide consecutive terms), interpret r in context (growth factor, decay factor), and use r in explicit/recursive formulas and series sums. r corresponds to (1+growth rate) in percent-change models.",
