      "correctText": "half-life model",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "Half-life model: N(t)=N₀(1/2)^(t/h), where N₀ is initial amount, h is half-life (F-IF.8b). Example: Carbon-14 has half-life 5730 years; a sample starting at 100g has after 11,460 years: 100·(1/2)^(11460/5730)=100·(1/2)²=25g. To find age from remaining fraction: 0.25=(1/2)^(t/5730) → t/5730=2 → t=11,460. Equivalent forms use e: N(t)=N₀·e^(-kt) where k=ln(2)/h. On the Algebra 2 Regents, half-life appears in radioactive decay, drug elimination, and cooling problems. The model is exponential decay with a specific 'half' reference point. Doubling time analog: N(t)=N₀·2^(t/d) for growth with doubling time d.",
