      "correctText": "geometric sequence",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "A geometric sequence has a constant common ratio r between consecutive terms (F-LE.2). Explicit: aₙ=a₁·r^(n-1). Recursive: aₙ=r·aₙ₋₁. Example: 3, 6, 12, 24, ...; a₁=3, r=2; a₁₀=3·2⁹=1536. Geometric sequences correspond to exponential functions: aₙ=a₁·r^(n-1) is an exponential with base r. The graph as (n, aₙ) is exponential growth (r>1) or decay (0<r<1) or alternating (r<0). On the Algebra 2 Regents, geometric sequences are tested via finding specific terms, identifying a₁ and r, and computing geometric series sums. Recognize from constant ratios: aₙ₊₁/aₙ = r for all n.",
