      "correctText": "infinite geometric series",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "An infinite geometric series converges when |r|<1: S=a₁/(1-r) (A-SSE.4). Example: 1+1/2+1/4+1/8+... with a₁=1, r=1/2: S=1/(1-1/2)=2. Geometrically intuitive: each term is half the previous, and the partial sums approach 2 but never exceed it. For |r|≥1, the series diverges (sum grows without bound or oscillates). Famous result: 0.999...=Σ9/10ⁿ=0.9/(1-0.1)=1, showing 0.999... and 1 are equal. On the Algebra 2 Regents, infinite geometric series appear in repeating decimal problems (convert 0.272727... to fraction: a₁=0.27, r=0.01, S=0.27/0.99=27/99=3/11) and in continuous-payment financial models.",
