      "correctText": "finite geometric series",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "A finite geometric series is the sum of n terms of a geometric sequence: Sₙ=a₁(1-rⁿ)/(1-r) for r≠1 (A-SSE.4). Example: 2+6+18+54+162 has a₁=2, r=3, n=5; S=2(1-3⁵)/(1-3)=2(-242)/(-2)=242. Formula derivation: Sₙ=a₁+a₁r+a₁r²+...+a₁rⁿ⁻¹. Multiply by r: rSₙ=a₁r+a₁r²+...+a₁rⁿ. Subtract: Sₙ(1-r)=a₁(1-rⁿ). On the Algebra 2 Regents, geometric series appear in compound-interest balances over multiple deposits, drug dosing schedules, and pattern problems. The formula simplifies to nuanced variants like Sₙ=a₁(rⁿ-1)/(r-1) for r>1.",
