      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "Exponential regression fits y=a·b^x (or equivalently y=a·e^(kx)) to bivariate data (S-ID.6, F-LE.5). On the calculator: enter data in lists L1, L2; STAT → CALC → ExpReg L1, L2; calculator returns a and b plus r and r² (correlation and coefficient of determination). Example: bacteria counts at hours 0,1,2,3,4 are 100,180,324,583,1050; ExpReg returns y≈100·(1.80)^x with r²≈0.9999, suggesting an excellent exponential fit. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students often perform regression and then interpret: a=initial value, b=growth factor (b>1 growth, 0<b<1 decay), percent change = 100(b-1)%. Compare exponential to other model types using r² and residual plots.",
