      "correctText": "doubling time",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "Doubling time is the period for an exponentially growing quantity to double (F-IF.8b). For continuous growth A=Pe^(rt), set A=2P: 2=e^(rt) → ln 2=rt → t=ln(2)/r. Example: 7% continuous growth gives doubling time ln(2)/0.07≈9.9 years. Rule of 70 (or 72): doubling time ≈ 70/(100r) for small r. Compound version: 2=(1+r)^t → t=log(2)/log(1+r). On the Algebra 2 Regents, doubling time appears in population, investment, and growth modeling. Pairs with half-life for decay: t_half=ln(2)/k. Recognizing the inverse relationship between growth rate and doubling time is a frequent Regents item.",
