      "explanation": "A tide model represents water height as a sinusoid: H(t)=a·cos(b·t)+d (F-TF.5). Typical period ≈ 12.4 hours (semidiurnal tide, two highs and two lows per day). Example: low tide 1 ft, high tide 7 ft, high at t=0 (noon): midline d=4, amplitude a=3, b=2π/12.4≈0.507. H(t)=3·cos(0.507t)+4. When does H=5? 5=3cos(0.507t)+4 → cos(0.507t)=1/3 → 0.507t=±1.231+2πk → t=2.43+12.4k or 9.97+12.4k. On the Algebra 2 Regents, tide problems are classic sinusoidal modeling items requiring identification of amplitude, period, midline, and phase shift, plus inverse-cosine solution of when height equals a target.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
