      "topic": "Parameters",
      "explanation": "The amplitude |a| in y=a·sin(bx)+d gives the maximum vertical deviation from the midline (F-TF.5). Max=d+|a|; min=d-|a|; amplitude=(max-min)/2. In tide modeling: amplitude is half the difference between high and low tide. In daylight modeling: amplitude is half the difference between longest and shortest day. Negative a reflects the graph but amplitude is still |a|. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students interpret amplitude in context: 'the tides vary 3 feet above and 3 feet below average,' or 'daylight ranges 4 hours above and 4 hours below the 12-hour average.' Amplitude has the same units as the dependent variable (feet, hours, dollars, etc.).",
