      "correctText": "intercept meaning",
      "topic": "Parameters",
      "explanation": "The y-intercept (0, k) is the value of the dependent variable when x=0; often interpretable as 'initial value' (F-IF.4). In linear y=mx+b: y-intercept = b (initial value). In exponential y=a·bˣ: y-intercept = a (initial value). In quadratic y=ax²+bx+c: y-intercept = c. Examples: in C(t)=50+0.10t for cost as a function of texts, $50 is the base fee. In P(t)=1000(1.02)ᵗ for population, 1000 is the initial population. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students always interpret intercepts in context: at t=0 (start), what's the value? Always state units. The y-intercept may not be in the meaningful domain (e.g., the y-intercept of a model that starts at t=1 may be unrealistic).",
