      "explanation": "The exponential parent function f(x)=b^x (b>1) has domain all reals, range y>0, horizontal asymptote y=0, and y-intercept (0,1) (F-IF.7e). It grows by a constant factor per unit increase in x, distinguishing it from polynomial growth. Common bases: 2 (computer science), e≈2.71828 (natural growth, continuous compounding), and 10 (orders of magnitude). The inverse of y=b^x is y=log_b(x). On the Algebra 2 Regents, exponential models appear in compound-interest items A=P(1+r/n)^(nt), continuous growth A=Pe^(rt), and half-life problems N(t)=N₀(1/2)^(t/h). When 0<b<1 the function decays; when b>1 it grows. Solving b^x=k uses logarithms: x=log_b(k).",
