      "explanation": "The absolute value parent function f(x)=|x| produces a V-shape with vertex (0,0), slopes -1 (left) and +1 (right) (F-IF.7b). Domain is all real numbers; range is y≥0. Absolute value equations like |x-3|=5 split into x-3=5 or x-3=-5, giving x=8 or x=-2. Inequalities like |x|<a become -a<x<a (intersection); |x|>a becomes x<-a or x>a (union). On the Algebra 2 Regents, absolute value functions appear in piecewise representations and transformation items: y=2|x-1|-3 has vertex (1,-3), opens upward with slope ±2. The function is not differentiable at the vertex, a fact that distinguishes it from quadratic minima.",
