      "correctText": "degree",
      "topic": "Polynomial Features",
      "explanation": "The degree of a polynomial is the highest exponent on the variable (A-APR.1). For f(x)=3x⁴-2x+1, degree=4. Degree controls end behavior, the maximum number of real zeros (at most n for degree n), and the maximum number of turning points (at most n-1). Quadratic: degree 2, up to 2 zeros, 1 turning point. Cubic: degree 3, up to 3 zeros, 2 turning points. Quartic: degree 4, up to 4 zeros, 3 turning points. Constant terms have degree 0; the zero polynomial has undefined degree. On the Algebra 2 Regents, degree appears in classifying polynomials, predicting end behavior, and applying the Fundamental Theorem of Algebra: a polynomial of degree n has exactly n complex zeros counting multiplicities.",
