      "topic": "Power + Change",
      "explanation": "Divergence means a sequence or series fails to approach a finite limit (A-SSE.4 connection). Three types of sequence divergence: (1) to +∞, like 1, 2, 3, 4, ...; (2) to -∞, like -1, -2, -3, ...; (3) oscillation, like 1, -1, 1, -1, .... For series: partial sums grow unbounded, like 1+2+3+4+... or oscillate without settling. Geometric series diverge when |r|≥1. On the Algebra 2 Regents, divergence appears in checking |r|<1 for infinite geometric series: if |r|≥1, no finite sum exists. Distinguishing convergence vs divergence by sign of |r|-1 is the standard test for geometric series.",
