      "correctText": "domain of logarithm",
      "topic": "Power + Change",
      "explanation": "The domain of y=log_b(x) is x>0 (F-IF.5). For transformed logs y=log_b(g(x)), the domain consists of all x where g(x)>0. Example: y=log(2x-6) has domain 2x-6>0 → x>3, written (3,∞). For y=log(x²-4): x²-4>0 → x²>4 → x<-2 or x>2, written (-∞,-2)∪(2,∞). On the Algebra 2 Regents, students state domain restrictions of logarithmic functions in interval or set-builder notation, and use these restrictions to reject extraneous solutions of logarithmic equations. Whenever a candidate solution makes any log argument non-positive, it must be rejected as extraneous.",
