      "correctText": "repeated percent change",
      "topic": "Power + Change",
      "explanation": "Repeated percent change applies the same percent factor each period, producing a geometric sequence (F-LE.5). A quantity Q with periodic rate r has Qₙ=Q₀(1+r)ⁿ (growth) or Qₙ=Q₀(1-r)ⁿ (decay). Example: $1000 with 5% annual growth → after 10 years: 1000(1.05)¹⁰≈1628.89. Recognize repeated percent change in problem text: 'increases by 3% each year,' 'depreciates 12% annually,' 'population doubles every decade.' Common ratio = 1+r (growth) or 1-r (decay). On the Algebra 2 Regents, percent change problems test recognition of geometric structure, fluent computation of (1±r)ⁿ, and interpretation of growth factor versus growth rate.",
      "domain": "Review",
