      "correctText": "event complement",
      "topic": "Probability Rules",
      "explanation": "The complement A' (or A^c) of event A consists of all outcomes in the sample space not in A (S-CP.1). P(A')=1-P(A). Useful when computing 'at least' probabilities: P(at least one head in 5 flips)=1-P(no heads)=1-(1/2)⁵=31/32. Complement of 'getting a 6' in a die roll is 'getting 1, 2, 3, 4, or 5,' probability 5/6. On the Algebra 2 Regents, the complement is a key tool when direct counting is complicated: complement of 'at least one' is 'none,' which is often easier to count. De Morgan's laws: (A∪B)'=A'∩B', (A∩B)'=A'∪B'.",
