      "explanation": "The addition rule: P(A∪B)=P(A)+P(B)-P(A∩B) (S-CP.7). Subtracting the intersection corrects for double-counting outcomes in both A and B. For mutually exclusive events (A∩B=∅): P(A∪B)=P(A)+P(B). Example: drawing a card, P(heart or king)=P(heart)+P(king)-P(heart and king)=13/52+4/52-1/52=16/52=4/13. Without subtraction: 17/52 (incorrect, double-counts the king of hearts). On the Algebra 2 Regents, the addition rule is tested in two-event problems with possible overlap; identifying whether events are mutually exclusive determines whether the subtraction is needed.",
