      "explanation": "Conditional probability P(B|A) restricts the sample space to A and asks the proportion of A that is also in B: P(B|A)=P(A∩B)/P(A) (S-CP.3). Example: from a two-way table of car color and type, P(SUV | red)=P(SUV and red)/P(red). Independent events have P(B|A)=P(B); dependent events have P(B|A)≠P(B). Bayes' theorem rearranges: P(A|B)=P(B|A)·P(A)/P(B). On the Algebra 2 Regents, conditional probability problems often use two-way tables (rows = one categorical variable, columns = another). Students compute conditional probabilities by restricting attention to a row or column and dividing. P(B|A)+P(B'|A)=1 (complement within the restricted space).",
