      "explanation": "A rational exponent m/n is defined as ⁿ√(x^m) = (ⁿ√x)^m (N-RN.1). Examples: x^(1/2)=√x, x^(1/3)=³√x, x^(2/3)=³√(x²)=(³√x)². All exponent rules carry over: x^a·x^b=x^(a+b), (x^a)^b=x^(ab), x^a/x^b=x^(a-b). Example: 8^(2/3)=(8^(1/3))²=2²=4. Negative rational exponents reciprocate: x^(-m/n)=1/x^(m/n). On the Algebra 2 Regents, students translate between radical and rational-exponent notation, simplify expressions like (x⁴)^(3/2)=x⁶, and solve equations like x^(2/3)=4 by raising to the reciprocal power: x=4^(3/2)=8.",
