      "explanation": "The principal square root is the non-negative root: √25=5, not -5 (N-RN.2). The equation x²=25 has two solutions x=±5, but √25 (the symbol alone) returns only the principal (non-negative) root. Distinguishing these in writing is critical on the Regents: √x is a function returning the non-negative output, whereas ±√x represents both square roots. For even-index radicals (square root, fourth root), only the principal (non-negative) root is returned. For odd-index radicals (cube root, fifth root), there is a unique real root that matches the sign of the radicand. This convention matters when solving equations involving even powers.",
