      "correctText": "index of radical",
      "topic": "Radical Expressions",
      "explanation": "The index of a radical is the small number above the radical symbol, indicating the root: ²√ (square root, often omitted as just √), ³√ (cube root), ⁴√ (fourth root) (N-RN.2). Even index (2,4,6): radicand must be ≥0 in the real numbers; result is non-negative. Odd index (3,5): radicand can be any real; result has same sign as radicand. The relationship to rational exponents: ⁿ√(x^m)=x^(m/n). Example: ³√(x⁶)=x². On the Algebra 2 Regents, students convert between radical and rational-exponent forms, simplify radical expressions, and apply index awareness to set domain restrictions on radical functions.",
