      "correctText": "conjugate pair",
      "topic": "Radical Expressions",
      "explanation": "Conjugate pairs are expressions of the form a+b√c and a-b√c, whose product (a+b√c)(a-b√c)=a²-b²c is rational (N-RN.3). Use conjugates to rationalize denominators: 1/(2+√3) · (2-√3)/(2-√3)=(2-√3)/(4-3)=2-√3. Complex conjugate pairs (a+bi and a-bi) work similarly: (a+bi)(a-bi)=a²+b². Use to simplify 1/(3-2i): multiply by (3+2i)/(3+2i)=(3+2i)/(9+4)=(3+2i)/13. On the Algebra 2 Regents, rationalizing denominators and simplifying complex-number expressions both use conjugates. The technique reduces a denominator with a radical or imaginary unit to a real, rational number.",
