      "correctText": "simplified rational form",
      "topic": "Rational Expressions",
      "explanation": "A simplified rational expression has no common factors between numerator and denominator, and includes notation of all excluded values from the original (A-APR.7). Example: (x²-9)/(x²-3x)=(x-3)(x+3)/(x(x-3))=(x+3)/x, with x≠0 and x≠3. The x≠3 restriction is essential even though the (x-3) was canceled—the original was undefined there. On the Algebra 2 Regents, leaving out excluded values is a common point-loss error in simplification items. Always check that the simplified expression is equivalent to the original by selecting a non-excluded value and substituting into both. A graph would show a hole at x=3.",
