      "correctText": "excluded value",
      "topic": "Rational Expressions",
      "explanation": "An excluded value is an x-value that makes the denominator zero, undefined for the rational expression (A-APR.7). For (x+3)/(x²-9)=(x+3)/((x-3)(x+3)), excluded values are x=3 and x=-3 (even after canceling). Always identify excluded values from the original (unsimplified) expression because simplifying can hide them. The domain of the rational expression is all reals except the excluded values, written {x|x≠3, x≠-3} or (-∞,-3)∪(-3,3)∪(3,∞). On the Algebra 2 Regents, excluded values appear in domain questions and in identifying whether a denominator zero produces a vertical asymptote (still present after simplifying) or a hole (canceled).",
