      "correctText": "complex fraction",
      "topic": "Rational Expressions",
      "explanation": "A complex fraction has fractions in its numerator, denominator, or both (A-APR.7). Example: (1/x+1/y)/(1/x-1/y). Two simplification methods: (1) combine top and bottom into single fractions, then divide: ((y+x)/xy)/((y-x)/xy)=(y+x)/(y-x); (2) multiply numerator and denominator by the LCD of all internal fractions: multiply by xy/xy to get (y+x)/(y-x). On the Algebra 2 Regents, complex fractions appear in items where students simplify before solving an equation or interpret a rational expression in context (e.g., harmonic mean, parallel resistor formula 1/R=1/R₁+1/R₂).",
