      "correctText": "rounding rule",
      "topic": "Reasonableness",
      "explanation": "A rounding rule keeps the precision of the final answer consistent with the input data (N-Q.3). Common: round to the nearest whole number, tenth, or hundredth based on context. For money: cents (hundredths). For population: whole people. For time: depends on precision of the problem (seconds, hours, days). On the Algebra 2 Regents, the directions usually specify rounding ('to the nearest tenth'); failing to round properly costs credit. Common errors: rounding intermediate results (compounds rounding error), reporting more digits than meaningful (false precision), and rounding too early. Keep extra decimal places during computation; round only at the end.",
