      "correctText": "loan payment model",
      "topic": "Sequences and Series Practice",
      "explanation": "Loan payment model uses PMT=P·r/[1-(1+r)⁻ⁿ] where P is principal, r is rate per period, n total payments (A-SSE.4 application). Example: $20,000 car loan at 4.8% annual (0.4% monthly) for 60 months: r=0.004, PMT=20000·0.004/(1-(1.004)⁻⁶⁰)≈$375.41. Total paid: 60·$375.41=$22,524.60; interest: $2524.60. This derives from setting the present value of an annuity equal to the loan amount. On the Algebra 2 Regents, loan-amortization problems test setting up correct monthly variables (annual rate ÷ 12, monthly periods = years × 12) and computing payment, total cost, or interest paid.",
