      "prompt": "Number r² between 0 and 1 giving the proportion of variation in y explained by the regression.",
      "choices": [
        "coefficient of determination",
        "correlation coefficient",
        "regression equation",
        "logarithm"
      ],
      "correctText": "coefficient of determination",
      "topic": "Technology Output",
      "explanation": "The coefficient of determination r² (R-squared) is the proportion of variability in y explained by the regression model (S-ID.8). r²∈[0,1]. r²=0.85 means 85% of variation in y is explained by x; the remaining 15% is unexplained (residual). Always non-negative regardless of correlation direction. r²=r² (square of correlation for linear regression). Useful for comparing different models on the same data: higher r² generally indicates better fit. But r² alone can be misleading—a high r² with a non-random residual pattern indicates wrong model type. On the Algebra 2 Regents, students compute r², interpret it as a percent, and use it alongside residual plots to assess model adequacy.",
