      "correctText": "trig-equation solution",
      "topic": "Trig and Sequences",
      "explanation": "A trig-equation solution is an angle satisfying a trig equation (F-TF.7). Standard solution method: (1) isolate the trig function; (2) find reference angle; (3) determine sign and quadrant; (4) list solutions in given interval; (5) for general solution, add 2πk for sine/cosine or πk for tangent. Example: 2cos θ+1=0 in [0,2π) → cos θ=-1/2 → reference angle π/3, cos negative in Q2 and Q3 → θ=π-π/3=2π/3 and θ=π+π/3=4π/3. On the cumulative Algebra 2 Regents review, trig equations test fluent unit-circle work, quadrant analysis, and writing all solutions in the specified interval. Missing a solution is a common error.",
