      "correctText": "sinusoidal parameter",
      "topic": "Trig and Sequences",
      "explanation": "A sinusoidal parameter is one of a, b, c, or d in y=a·sin(b(x-c))+d (F-TF.5). a (amplitude): (max-min)/2. b (controls period 2π/b): if period T given, b=2π/T. c (phase shift): aligns a known feature, e.g., a peak's x-coordinate. d (midline): (max+min)/2. From context, identify: max value, min value, period, and the x-coordinate of a peak or midline crossing. Example: Ferris wheel max height 60 ft, min 10 ft, period 4 min, first peak at t=2 min. d=35, a=25, b=2π/4=π/2, c=2 (using sine starting from midline at t=0, the peak is at t=2 because period/4=1 from start, but cosine model is cleaner with c=2). On the cumulative Algebra 2 Regents review, students extract parameters from data to write the sinusoidal equation.",
