      "explanation": "A series sum is the total of terms in a series (A-SSE.4). Arithmetic: Sₙ=n(a₁+aₙ)/2=n[2a₁+(n-1)d]/2. Geometric finite: Sₙ=a₁(1-rⁿ)/(1-r). Geometric infinite (|r|<1): S=a₁/(1-r). Example: sum 2+5+8+...+59 (n=20, a₁=2, a₂₀=59): S=20(2+59)/2=610. Geometric 1+3+9+27+81 (a₁=1, r=3, n=5): S=1(1-243)/(1-3)=121. Infinite geometric 12+6+3+1.5+... (r=0.5): S=12/(1-0.5)=24. On the cumulative Algebra 2 Regents review, students choose the right formula based on series type, identify a₁, r or d, n, and substitute correctly.",
