      "correctText": "Pythagorean identity",
      "topic": "Trig Functions",
      "explanation": "The Pythagorean identity: sin²θ+cos²θ=1 (F-TF.8). Direct consequence of x²+y²=1 with x=cos θ, y=sin θ. Dividing by cos²θ: tan²θ+1=sec²θ. Dividing by sin²θ: 1+cot²θ=csc²θ. These three Pythagorean identities are the most-used trig identities on the Algebra 2 Regents. Applications: (1) If sin θ=3/5 and θ is in Q1, find cos θ: cos²θ=1-9/25=16/25 → cos θ=4/5 (positive in Q1); (2) Simplify (1-cos²x)/sin x = sin²x/sin x = sin x. The identity does not depend on the unit of θ (works in both radians and degrees) because it follows from the geometric Pythagoras theorem.",
