      "explanation": "The reciprocal identities define the three reciprocal trig functions: csc(θ)=1/sin(θ), sec(θ)=1/cos(θ), cot(θ)=1/tan(θ)=cos(θ)/sin(θ) (F-TF.8). Domains exclude zeros of the denominator: csc undefined where sin=0 (multiples of π); sec undefined where cos=0 (odd multiples of π/2); cot undefined where sin=0. Range of csc and sec is (-∞,-1]∪[1,∞) because |sin|, |cos|≤1. Examples: csc(π/2)=1/1=1; sec(π/3)=1/(1/2)=2; cot(π/4)=1/1=1. On the Algebra 2 Regents, reciprocal identities appear in simplifying trig expressions and in solving equations involving sec, csc, or cot. Less common than sin/cos/tan but tested.",
