      "explanation": "A logarithmic graph has shape determined by base b (F-IF.7e). For b>1: domain x>0, range all reals, vertical asymptote x=0, x-intercept (1,0), passing through (b,1). For 0<b<1: same domain and range, but decreasing. The graph approaches -∞ as x→0⁺ (for b>1). Transformations apply as usual: y=log(x-2)+3 shifts the parent log right 2 and up 3, with vertical asymptote x=2. On the Algebra 2 Regents, log graphs appear in inverse-function items (graph of exponential and its log inverse) and in modeling slow-growth phenomena. The log graph grows but does so slowly: doubling x adds only a fixed amount to y, not multiplies.",
