      "explanation": "A complex root has the form a+bi with imaginary part b≠0 (N-CN.7). The Conjugate Pairs Theorem: if a polynomial has real coefficients and a+bi is a root, then a-bi is also a root. Example: if P(x)=x²-4x+13 has root 2+3i, then 2-3i is also a root, and P(x)=(x-(2+3i))(x-(2-3i))=x²-4x+13. Use the quadratic formula to find complex roots when the discriminant is negative: for x²+2x+5=0, x=(-2±√(-16))/2=-1±2i. On the Algebra 2 Regents, complex roots appear in solving quadratics with negative discriminant and in higher-degree polynomials where students count real vs complex zeros and use Vieta's relations.",
