      "topic": "Apoptosis + Checkpoints",
      "explanation": "The Spindle Assembly Checkpoint (SAC) prevents anaphase until every kinetochore is bipolarly attached to microtubules. Unattached kinetochores generate a 'wait' signal — the MCC (Mad2, BubR1, Bub3, Cdc20 complex) — that inhibits the APC/C and prevents securin/cyclin B degradation. Even ONE unattached kinetochore arrests mitosis. Bypassing the SAC causes chromosome missegregation and aneuploidy — virtually universal in solid tumors (Boveri 1914 first hypothesized cancer-aneuploidy link). Drugs: nocodazole and taxanes (paclitaxel) lock the SAC by perturbing MT dynamics → mitotic arrest → apoptosis (anti-cancer mechanism). AP Bio Unit 4 expects mechanistic knowledge of error-detection in mitosis.",
