      "explanation": "Punctuated equilibrium (Eldredge and Gould 1972) proposes that most species spend most of their existence in morphological stasis, punctuated by relatively brief (geologically — 5,000-50,000 yr) bursts of rapid change during speciation (often allopatric, in small peripheral populations). Contrasts with phyletic gradualism (Darwin's view: slow constant change). Fossil evidence: many species show stasis for millions of years, then sudden replacement by daughter species. Both modes occur — punctuated for some lineages, gradual for others. Punctuationism doesn't invoke new mechanisms — just different rates. AP Bio Unit 7 FRQs may ask students to interpret a phylogenetic diagram with branch lengths and identify stasis vs. rapid change.",
