      "explanation": "Genetic drift is random change in allele frequencies due to sampling error in finite populations. Effect inversely proportional to effective population size (Ne): small populations drift fast (alleles fix or are lost quickly); large populations drift slowly. Wright (1931) developed mathematical theory. Drift is non-selective — neutral or even beneficial alleles can be lost. Neutral theory of molecular evolution (Kimura 1968): most substitutions at the molecular level are selectively neutral and reach fixation by drift. Effective population size of humans was ~10,000 for most of history — small enough that drift shaped much variation. AP Bio Unit 7 FRQs ask students to compare drift vs. selection roles in small vs. large populations.",
