      "correctText": "ln absolute value x",
      "topic": "Antiderivatives",
      "explanation": "integral of 1/x dx = ln|x| + C (for x != 0). The absolute value is essential — without it the antiderivative is undefined for x < 0, since ln(negative) is undefined over the reals. Derived from d/dx[ln|x|] = 1/x. Euler (1748 Introductio) used this antiderivative in series expansions; Cauchy (1823) supplied rigor. The case n = -1 in the Power Rule is exceptional precisely because the reverse formula x^(n+1)/(n+1) gives x^0/0, undefined. AP Calculus AB Unit 6 FRQ rubrics (2014 AB 6) deduct points when students omit the absolute value, particularly for definite integrals with negative bounds.",
