      "explanation": "U-substitution reverses the Chain Rule for integration: identify u = g(x), compute du = g'(x) dx, then rewrite integral f(g(x)) g'(x) dx as integral f(u) du. Leibniz (1675-84) anticipated the technique by manipulating differentials; Euler (1748) formalized substitution as a calculation tool; Cauchy (1823) gave rigorous justification using the Chain Rule and FTC. Worked example: integral 2x cos(x^2) dx with u = x^2 gives integral cos u du = sin u + C = sin(x^2) + C. AP Calculus AB Unit 6 FRQs (2017 AB 6, 2019 AB 4) require explicit du substitution and limit conversion for definite integrals.",
