      "correctText": "Increases solubility",
      "topic": "Acid-Base Equilibria",
      "explanation": "Insoluble hydroxides like Mg(OH)₂ (Ksp = 5.6 × 10⁻¹²) and Fe(OH)₃ (Ksp = 2.8 × 10⁻³⁹) become more soluble in acid. Adding H⁺ consumes OH⁻ (forming water), so Le Chatelier shifts the dissolution equilibrium right, dissolving more solid. Similarly, weak-acid anions (CO₃²⁻, S²⁻, PO₄³⁻) make their salts more soluble in acid because protonation of the anion lowers its free concentration. Strong-acid anions (Cl⁻, NO₃⁻, ClO₄⁻) show no pH effect. AP Chemistry Unit 7 routinely tests pH-dependent solubility, which connects directly to Unit 8 acid-base equilibria.",
      "domain": "Review",
