      "topic": "Acid-Base Equilibria",
      "explanation": "Weak acids ionize (dissociate) only partially in solution — typically 1-5% in 0.1 M solutions — establishing an equilibrium HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻. Strong acids ionize completely (>99.9%); weak acids' ionization is limited by their Ka. Acetic acid (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵) at 0.10 M gives x ≈ 1.3 × 10⁻³ M, about 1.3% ionization. This partial ionization is what makes weak-acid solutions buffer-capable when mixed with their conjugate-base salt (Unit 8) — addition of strong acid is absorbed by the conjugate base; addition of strong base is absorbed by the weak acid. AP Chemistry Unit 7 frames this for Unit 8.",
