      "prompt": "Plot of reaction progress vs energy, showing reactants, products, and Ea.",
      "choices": [
        "Activation energy",
        "Potential energy diagram",
        "Negative activation energy",
        "Henderson-Hasselbalch"
      ],
      "correctText": "Potential energy diagram",
      "topic": "Activation Energy",
      "explanation": "A potential-energy (PE) diagram plots energy on the y-axis against reaction coordinate on the x-axis. Key features: reactants (left), transition state(s) (peaks), intermediates (valleys), products (right). ΔH = E_products − E_reactants (exothermic if negative, endothermic if positive). Ea_forward = E_TS − E_reactants; Ea_reverse = E_TS − E_products. Multi-step reactions show multiple humps. Catalysts lower all activation barriers; they do NOT change ΔH. AP Chemistry Unit 5 free-response routinely asks students to draw, label, and interpret PE diagrams — often comparing catalyzed vs uncatalyzed cases.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
