      "id": "ap-chemistry-038",
      "prompt": "Geometry of PCl₅: five bonding pairs, no lone pairs on central P.",
      "choices": [
        "Coffee-cup calorimeter",
        "Bent",
        "VSEPR theory",
        "Trigonal bipyramidal"
      ],
      "correctText": "Trigonal bipyramidal",
      "topic": "VSEPR Geometry",
      "explanation": "Trigonal bipyramidal geometry has five bonding domains in a 'double-pyramid' arrangement: three equatorial positions at 120° (in a plane) and two axial positions at 90° to that plane. PCl₅ is the textbook example. Axial bonds are slightly longer than equatorial because they have three 90° neighbors (vs. two 120° plus two 90° for equatorial). Lone pairs prefer equatorial sites (less repulsion), giving derived shapes: seesaw (1 LP, SF₄), T-shape (2 LP, ClF₃), and linear (3 LP, XeF₂). AP Chemistry Unit 2 expects students to recall both ideal and lone-pair-modified geometries.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
