      "id": "ap-chemistry-040",
      "prompt": "Shape and angle of XeF₄: square planar, this many lone pairs on Xe.",
      "choices": [
        "Two",
        "Negative",
        "Bent",
        "Trigonal bipyramidal"
      ],
      "correctText": "Two",
      "topic": "VSEPR Geometry",
      "explanation": "Xenon tetrafluoride (XeF₄) has six electron domains around xenon: four bonding pairs (Xe-F) plus two lone pairs. The lone pairs occupy trans-axial positions (180° apart, minimizing LP-LP repulsion), leaving the four F atoms in a square planar arrangement at 90° angles. The molecule is therefore square planar and nonpolar (bond dipoles cancel by symmetry). XeF₄ was a landmark synthesis by Neil Bartlett's group in 1962, proving noble gases form compounds. AP Chemistry Unit 2 uses XeF₄ as a key case study for octahedral electron geometry with 2 lone pairs.",
