      "prompt": "Shape of water: two bonding pairs, two lone pairs, ~104.5°.",
      "choices": [
        "Opposite sign",
        "VSEPR theory",
        "Expanded octet",
        "Bent"
      ],
      "correctText": "Bent",
      "topic": "VSEPR Geometry",
      "explanation": "Water (H₂O) has four electron domains around oxygen — two O-H bonds plus two lone pairs — so the electron geometry is tetrahedral but the molecular geometry is bent. The H-O-H angle compresses from 109.5° to ~104.5° because lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs. This bent shape, combined with the polar O-H bonds, gives water its substantial dipole moment (1.85 D), driving its many anomalous properties (high BP, high heat capacity, high surface tension, density max at 4°C). AP Chemistry Unit 2 ties this geometry directly to Unit 3 IMF discussions.",
