      "id": "ap-chemistry-036",
      "prompt": "Theory predicting molecular shape from electron-pair repulsion around a central atom.",
      "choices": [
        "Trigonal bipyramidal",
        "Percent yield",
        "Bent",
        "VSEPR theory"
      ],
      "correctText": "VSEPR theory",
      "topic": "VSEPR Geometry",
      "explanation": "VSEPR — Valence Shell Electron Pair Repulsion — theory was developed by Ronald Gillespie and Ronald Nyholm in 1957. Electron pairs (bonding and lone) around a central atom repel each other and arrange themselves at maximum angular separation. Lone pairs occupy more angular space than bonding pairs (LP-LP > LP-BP > BP-BP repulsion), distorting ideal angles. The number of electron domains around the central atom dictates the electron geometry (2 = linear, 3 = trigonal planar, 4 = tetrahedral, 5 = trigonal bipyramidal, 6 = octahedral). AP Chemistry Unit 2 free-response routinely demands the steric-number rationale.",
