      "correctText": "Audience analysis",
      "topic": "Audience Adaptation",
      "explanation": "Audience analysis is the systematic study of the audience's knowledge, values, disposition, and likely responses - foundational to all rhetorical choice. AP English Language Unit 7 teaches audience analysis as the prerequisite to all other rhetorical decisions: writers who do not know their audience cannot calibrate logos, ethos, or pathos. Edwin Black's 'Second Persona' (1970) extended audience analysis to imagined audiences constructed by the text. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) is a master class in layered audience analysis: clergy, white moderates, Black community, posterity. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards students who tie rhetorical choices to specific audience features rather than treating rhetoric as universal.",
