      "topic": "Audience Analysis",
      "explanation": "An expert audience knows the topic deeply and requires technical precision, specialized vocabulary, and primary sources to be persuaded - generalist appeals fail. AP English Language Unit 1 teaches that expert audiences detect vagueness and reject oversimplification, so writers must demonstrate disciplinary command. Stephen Jay Gould's essays for natural history readers presume Darwin's mechanism; Carson's 'Silent Spring' (1962) sustained a dual address by including chemical detail rigorous enough for entomologists while remaining accessible. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ tests whether students can recognize expert-audience markers like jargon, citation density, and presumed background knowledge.",
