      "correctText": "In-text citation",
      "topic": "Citation Practices",
      "explanation": "In-text citation is brief attribution within the essay's prose - typically (Source A) or 'As Carson notes...' in the synthesis FRQ (Q1) format. AP English Language Unit 8 teaches in-text citation as the integration mechanism: every quotation, paraphrase, and summary needs clear attribution at the point of use, not just in a separate citation list. The AP synthesis FRQ rubric (Row B, evidence and commentary) implicitly demands in-text citation because evidence credit requires the reader to identify which source supplies the cited material. Strong essays cite smoothly within sentence flow: 'Source D's data complicates this claim,' rather than awkward parenthetical interruptions.",
