      "correctText": "Coherence",
      "topic": "Coherence",
      "explanation": "Coherence is the quality of unity within and across paragraphs - the sense that ideas connect, that the writing makes sense as a whole. AP English Language Unit 3 distinguishes coherence (logical and conceptual unity) from cohesion (surface-level connection via pronouns, transitions, and repeated terms). The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric does not list coherence as a separate scoring category, but coherence failures sink evidence/commentary scores (Row B) because incoherent reasoning cannot be tracked. Orwell's 'Politics and the English Language' (1946) demonstrates coherence by tying every example back to the controlling thesis about political prose and clear thought.",
