      "correctText": "Concession",
      "topic": "Counterargument",
      "explanation": "Concession is the rhetorical move of acknowledging the truth or partial validity of an opposing view before proceeding with one's own argument. AP English Language Unit 2 teaches concession as foundational to ethos: writers who concede appear fair and reasonable, building credibility for the broader case. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) concedes the clergymen's good intentions before refuting their substantive positions; Wollstonecraft's 'Vindication' (1792) concedes that women's current condition matches the critics' descriptions before arguing that condition is cultivated, not natural. The AP argument FRQ rubric (Row C, sophistication, 1 point) rewards strategic concession.",
