      "explanation": "Anticipating counterargument is the rhetorical practice of raising objections oneself before opponents do - converting a defensive move (responding to attack) into an offensive one (controlling the framing). AP English Language Unit 2 teaches anticipation as advanced argumentative craft, signaled by phrases like 'one might object,' 'critics will argue,' or 'it could be said.' Sandel's 'Justice' (2009) anticipates libertarian, utilitarian, and Kantian objections in turn. The AP argument FRQ rubric (Row C, sophistication) awards points for arguments that anticipate counterargument, even more when the anticipated objections are genuinely strong rather than caricatured.",
