      "correctText": "Yes-but structure",
      "topic": "Counterargument",
      "explanation": "The yes-but structure is a rhetorical pattern of conceding then refuting - acknowledging the opposing view's partial validity ('yes, this is true...') before introducing the decisive limit ('...but this is more important'). AP English Language Unit 2 teaches yes-but as a fundamental concession/refutation rhythm because it disarms opposition while reasserting one's position. Lincoln's Second Inaugural (1865) deploys yes-but in conceding shared responsibility for war ('Both read the same Bible...') before urging reconciliation. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric rewards yes-but structures that earn sophistication points by showing genuine engagement with opposing views.",
