      "correctText": "Refutation",
      "topic": "Counterargument",
      "explanation": "Refutation is the rhetorical move of disproving or undermining an opposing view - the active critique that often follows concession. AP English Language Unit 2 teaches that effective refutation engages the strongest version of the opposing argument (the steel-man rather than straw-man), then identifies its empirical, logical, or moral failures. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) refutes the charge of extremism by reframing himself in the company of Jesus, Paul, Luther, Lincoln, and Jefferson. The AP argument FRQ rubric (Row B, evidence and commentary) rewards commentary that refutes counterarguments with specific reasoning, not just dismissal.",
