      "correctText": "Strengthens argument",
      "topic": "Counterargument",
      "explanation": "Addressing opposition strengthens one's own argument by demonstrating awareness, fairness, and resilience - the central insight of AP English Language Unit 2's treatment of counterargument. Writers who refuse to engage opposition signal either ignorance or evasion, both fatal to ethos. The AP argument FRQ rubric (Row C, sophistication, 1 point, College Board CED 2024) explicitly rewards 'engaging effectively with broader context' and 'addressing complexities or tensions' - language directly inviting counterargument engagement. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) is canonical because it spends paragraphs taking opposition seriously, transforming the eight clergymen's letter into a foil that magnifies his own argument.",
