      "correctText": "Paradox",
      "topic": "Devices",
      "explanation": "Paradox is an apparent contradiction that reveals deeper truth - a statement that seems self-defeating but illuminates rather than collapses on examination. AP English Language teaches paradox as related to but larger than oxymoron: paradoxes are full statements while oxymorons are compressed phrases. Orwell's 'Politics and the English Language' (1946) traffics in paradox about how clear thought requires clear language and clear language requires clear thought. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards students who identify paradox and trace how it functions - typically forcing readers through apparent contradiction toward more sophisticated understanding, building ethos through demonstrated intellectual depth.",
