      "correctText": "Metaphor",
      "topic": "Devices",
      "explanation": "Metaphor is a comparison without 'like' or 'as' - an implicit identification between two unlike things. AP English Language teaches metaphor as the most powerful trope (Aristotle, 'Poetics,' 4th c. BCE: 'the greatest thing by far is to be a master of metaphor'). King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) builds extended metaphor of America's 'promissory note' to Black citizens; Carson's 'Silent Spring' (1962) builds metaphor of the silenced soundscape. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2, 6-point rubric, College Board CED 2024) rubric rewards specific metaphor analysis tied to the source/target mapping and its rhetorical effect, not generic 'figurative language' labels. Strong metaphor analysis traces what is mapped onto what and why.",
