      "correctText": "Anaphora",
      "topic": "Devices",
      "explanation": "Anaphora is the repetition of the same word or phrase at the beginning of successive clauses, sentences, or paragraphs - one of the most powerful schemes in oral rhetoric. AP English Language treats anaphora as central because it appears constantly in canonical texts: King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) builds 'I have a dream' and 'Let freedom ring' as anaphoric pillars; Lincoln's Gettysburg Address (1863) closes with 'of the people, by the people, for the people.' The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards anaphora analysis tied to emphasis, rhythm, and structural framing rather than mere identification. Anaphora works through cumulative repetition that builds rhetorical momentum.",
