      "explanation": "Archaic diction uses words and expressions no longer in common use - 'thee,' 'thou,' 'whence,' 'consecrate,' 'forefathers,' 'shall' - lending writing a historical, ceremonial, or biblical resonance. AP English Language Unit 4 teaches archaic diction as a deliberate stylistic choice creating gravity, continuity with tradition, or distance from everyday speech. Lincoln's Second Inaugural (1865) uses archaic diction ('Fondly do we hope - fervently do we pray') to invoke biblical authority; King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) channels prophetic archaism. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards specific identification of archaic diction and analysis of its tonal effect, typically elevating the rhetorical occasion to historical or sacred register.",
