      "correctText": "Diction",
      "topic": "Diction",
      "explanation": "Diction is the writer's word choice - the selection of vocabulary that shapes meaning, tone, register, and persona. AP English Language Unit 4 treats diction as one of the two primary stylistic dimensions (alongside syntax) analyzed on the rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2). Skilled rhetorical analysts move past 'the author uses strong word choice' to specific diction-effect claims: which words, which connotations, which register, which emotional charge. Orwell's 'Politics and the English Language' (1946) makes diction the central concern, arguing that pretentious diction conceals political reality. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ rubric (Row B) awards points for diction analysis tied to author purpose, not vague labels.",
