      "explanation": "Concession as ethos strategy acknowledges the truth or partial validity of opposing views - a move that builds credibility by signaling fairness and intellectual honesty. AP English Language Unit 5 makes concession a primary ethos tool because writers who refuse all concession appear blinkered, while strategic concession demonstrates strength: writers who concede secondary points strengthen their case for primary ones. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) concedes the clergymen's good intentions before refuting their substantive positions. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric (Row C, sophistication) rewards strategic concession; pure stubbornness or false balance both cost sophistication points.",
