      "explanation": "An analogy is a comparison used to illustrate or argue a point by mapping the structure of a familiar case onto an unfamiliar one - both an explanatory device (figurative analogy) and a form of evidence (argument from analogy). AP English Language Unit 2 teaches that analogical arguments succeed only when the relevant features of the two cases truly match; superficial likeness yields false analogy fallacy. Swift's 'A Modest Proposal' (1729) deploys analogy between livestock and infants to expose the calculating logic he satirizes. The AP argument FRQ rewards analogical reasoning when the mapping is explicit and the disanalogies acknowledged.",
