      "correctText": "Anecdote",
      "topic": "Evidence Types",
      "explanation": "An anecdote is an eyewitness account or short personal story used as evidence - a primary form of pathos appeal because it makes abstract claims concrete and emotionally vivid. AP English Language Unit 2 teaches anecdote as legitimate evidence when paired with broader support; standalone anecdotes risk hasty generalization. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) deploys the anecdote of explaining segregation to his six-year-old daughter to make abstract injustice viscerally felt. Amy Tan's 'Mother Tongue' (1990) builds entirely on anecdotes about her mother's English. The AP argument FRQ rubric (Row B) accepts anecdotal evidence when commentary connects it to the broader claim.",
