      "correctText": "Statistics",
      "topic": "Evidence Types",
      "explanation": "Statistics are quantitative data supporting a claim - the dominant form of logos in modern argument because numbers carry an aura of objectivity. AP English Language Unit 2 teaches students to evaluate statistical evidence critically: who collected the data, what methodology was used, what counterfactual is implied. Carson's 'Silent Spring' (1962) uses statistical evidence on DDT residues to ground its policy claims; Pollan's 'The Omnivore's Dilemma' (2006) cites caloric and acreage statistics. On the AP synthesis FRQ (Q1), students must use statistical sources alongside other types and avoid the common error of letting numbers stand without commentary - the rubric requires reasoning that links data to claim.",
