      "correctText": "Expert testimony",
      "topic": "Evidence Types",
      "explanation": "Expert testimony is a quotation or citation from an authority figure used as evidence - an appeal to ethos by transferring the cited expert's credibility to the argument. AP English Language Unit 2 teaches students to evaluate expert testimony: is the expert genuinely qualified in the relevant field, is the citation accurate and in context, are competing experts acknowledged. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) cites Aquinas, Augustine, Buber, Tillich, and Niebuhr as theological authorities. The AP synthesis FRQ (Q1) source packets often include expert testimony, and the 6-point rubric rewards students who attribute testimony correctly and integrate it rather than letting quotation stand alone.",
