      "correctText": "Ad hominem",
      "topic": "FRQ Synthesis",
      "explanation": "Ad hominem (Latin: 'to the person') is the logical fallacy of attacking the speaker rather than the argument - a deflection from substance to character. AP English Language distinguishes ad hominem from legitimate ethos challenges: questioning a witness's credibility when credibility is at issue is not fallacious, but dismissing a chemical claim because the speaker is unpopular is. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric (Row C, sophistication) penalizes students who refute opposing views through ad hominem rather than substantive engagement. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) explicitly refuses ad hominem against the clergymen, engaging their arguments instead - a move that strengthens his own ethos.",
