      "explanation": "A political address is a speech delivered by a public official to citizens, often on ceremonial or crisis occasions - Inaugural addresses, State of the Union, Gettysburg Address (Lincoln 1863), Second Inaugural (Lincoln 1865), JFK Inaugural (1961). AP English Language Unit 1 makes political address a foundational genre because it concentrates rhetorical features: tightly composed, written for oral delivery, balancing logos for argument with pathos for unity and ethos for office. Students must master genre conventions before the rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2), which frequently features political addresses from MLK, Lincoln, Reagan, FDR, and others.",
