      "correctText": "Olfactory imagery",
      "topic": "Imagery",
      "explanation": "Olfactory imagery appeals to smell - the most evocative and least common sensory mode, capable of triggering memory and emotion bypassing rational evaluation. AP English Language Unit 4 teaches olfactory imagery as rare but powerful; writers deploy it when they want to bypass intellectual filters and reach embodied memory. Pollan's 'The Omnivore's Dilemma' (2006) uses olfactory imagery to ground arguments about industrial food in visceral disgust and pleasure. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards students who notice olfactory imagery and analyze why a writer reached for smell when other senses were available - typically to intensify memory, build place, or evoke unmediated reaction.",
