      "explanation": "An anecdote as pathos strategy is a short emotion-stirring story used to ground abstract claims in particular human experience. AP English Language Unit 5 teaches anecdote as the workhorse of pathos because readers feel narrative before they evaluate it analytically. King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) deploys the anecdote of explaining segregation to his six-year-old daughter, making abstract injustice viscerally felt. Sojourner Truth's 1851 'Ain't I a Woman?' uses the anecdote of her thirteen children sold into slavery as devastating pathos. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards students who analyze how anecdotes generate emotional response specifically, not just label them as 'emotional appeal.'",
