      "explanation": "Appeal to identity invokes the audience's sense of self - tribal, national, religious, professional, gendered - to align them emotionally with the argument's position. AP English Language Unit 5 teaches identity appeal as one of the most powerful pathos tools because identity threats trigger defensive emotional response. King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) appeals to identity through 'all of God's children,' the founding fathers, and shared American heritage; Frederick Douglass's 1852 speech indicts the gap between American identity claims and Black experience. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric (Row C) rewards essays that engage identity carefully, building common ground rather than triggering defensive identity reaction.",
