      "topic": "Pathos Strategies",
      "explanation": "Fear appeal warns of dire consequences to motivate audience action - one of the most ancient and contested pathos tools, ranging from legitimate alarm to manipulative scaremongering. AP English Language Unit 5 teaches fear appeal as ethically loaded: warnings about real dangers (Carson on pesticide harm, 1962; Postman on television's cultural effects, 1985) serve audience interests, while exaggerated or fabricated fears manipulate. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rubric (Row C, sophistication) penalizes pure fear-mongering as a logos/ethos failure while rewarding evidence-grounded warning. Fear appeal works when the threat is real, the proposed action mitigates it, and the appeal stays proportionate.",
