      "correctText": "Identification",
      "topic": "Persuasive Strategies",
      "explanation": "Identification (Kenneth Burke, 'A Rhetoric of Motives,' 1950) is the strategy of building common ground with the audience - establishing shared identity, values, or interests that allow persuasion to proceed. AP English Language Unit 7 teaches identification as Burke's modern reframing of classical ethos and pathos: persuasion requires the audience to feel the speaker is 'one of us.' King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) identifies with the clergymen as fellow Christians, citing shared scripture; Frederick Douglass's 1852 speech invites white audiences to identify with the moral position the day's hypocrisy denies. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) rewards identification moves that build rather than fracture audience common ground.",
