      "id": "ap-english-language-and-composition-019",
      "prompt": "Purpose calling for action.",
      "choices": [
        "Entertain",
        "Instruct",
        "Exhort",
        "Anaphora"
      ],
      "correctText": "Exhort",
      "topic": "Purpose and Context",
      "explanation": "To exhort is the rhetorical purpose of urging the audience to act or commit, going beyond persuading them to believe to demanding they do something - the principal purpose of speeches like King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) and JFK's Inaugural (1961, \"Ask not what your country can do for you\"). AP English Language Unit 1 places exhortation at the strong end of the persuasion spectrum because it requires not just changed minds but changed behavior. The AP argument FRQ (Q3) often invites exhortation in concluding paragraphs, but the rubric awards sophistication points only when exhortation is grounded in earned ethos and reasoned support, not pure pathos.",
