      "correctText": "Instruct",
      "topic": "Purpose and Context",
      "explanation": "To instruct is the rhetorical purpose of teaching readers how to perform a procedure or understand a process - common in how-to writing, technical manuals, and pedagogical essays. AP Lang Unit 1 treats instruction as a distinct purpose because it shapes structure (sequential steps, clear transitions) and style (imperatives, second-person address, precise diction). Orwell's 'Politics and the English Language' (1946) is partly instructive - it gives six rules for clear prose - while also persuasive about political honesty. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ asks students to identify when texts mix instruction with other purposes, especially in passages addressing readers as apprentices.",
