      "correctText": "Entertain",
      "topic": "Purpose and Context",
      "explanation": "To entertain is the rhetorical purpose of providing pleasure, amusement, or aesthetic enjoyment, often through humor, narrative, or vivid style - common in personal essays, satire, and creative nonfiction. AP English Language Unit 1 reminds students that entertainment rarely operates alone: satire entertains while critiquing (Swift, 'A Modest Proposal,' 1729), and personal essay entertains while making moral claims (Pollan, 'The Omnivore's Dilemma,' 2006; Tan, 'Mother Tongue,' 1990). The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ tests whether students see through surface entertainment to identify embedded purposes - mistaking satire for serious advocacy is a documented rubric pitfall.",
