      "correctText": "Inductive reasoning",
      "topic": "Reasoning Types",
      "explanation": "Inductive reasoning moves from specific observations to general conclusions - the engine of empirical science and most everyday argument. AP English Language Unit 3 distinguishes inductive from deductive reasoning because inductive conclusions are probable, not certain: a finite sample of cases licenses generalizations only with appropriate qualifiers. Carson's 'Silent Spring' (1962) reasons inductively from documented cases of bird die-offs to systemic claims about pesticide harm. The AP synthesis FRQ (Q1) typically requires inductive synthesis - drawing a general thesis from multiple specific sources - and the 6-point rubric rewards qualified generalizations over absolute ones (Row C, sophistication).",
