      "correctText": "Analogical reasoning",
      "topic": "Reasoning Types",
      "explanation": "Analogical reasoning argues by mapping a familiar case onto an unfamiliar one - inferring that what is true of the source case is true of the target case in the relevant respects. AP English Language Unit 3 teaches that analogy is powerful when the mapped features are genuinely parallel and weak when they diverge in ways affecting the conclusion - the false analogy fallacy. Swift's 'A Modest Proposal' (1729) builds satire through analogy between livestock economics and the treatment of Irish infants. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) often tests analogical reasoning, and the 6-point rubric rewards commentary that examines both the mapping and its limits.",
