      "correctText": "Tone",
      "topic": "Rhetorical Choices",
      "explanation": "Tone as rhetorical choice is the author's attitude toward subject matter - the emotional and intellectual register shaped through diction, syntax, imagery, and other stylistic features. AP English Language Unit 7 treats tone as a primary unit of rhetorical analysis because the same content delivered in different tones has different effects: indignant vs. measured, satirical vs. solemn, conciliatory vs. confrontational. Swift's 'A Modest Proposal' (1729) sustains deadpan tone to intensify satirical critique; King's 'Letter from Birmingham Jail' (1963) sustains firm but conciliatory tone. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards tonal analysis with precise adjectives tied to specific textual evidence.",
