      "topic": "Schemes",
      "explanation": "Parallelism is the use of similar grammatical structure for similar ideas - matching nouns to nouns, verbs to verbs, clauses to clauses - producing balance and clarity. AP English Language Unit 6 teaches parallelism as the scaffolding scheme underlying anaphora, epistrophe, and chiasmus: all repetition-based schemes depend on parallel structure. Lincoln's Gettysburg Address (1863) builds parallelism: 'we cannot dedicate - we cannot consecrate - we cannot hallow'; King's 'I Have a Dream' (1963) sustains parallel construction throughout. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards parallelism analysis specific enough to identify the structures matched and the rhetorical effect of matching them - typically emphasis, clarity, or rhythmic momentum.",
