      "correctText": "Onomatopoeia",
      "topic": "Sound Devices",
      "explanation": "Onomatopoeia is the use of words that imitate the sounds they describe - 'buzz,' 'hiss,' 'crash,' 'whisper,' 'pop.' AP English Language Unit 6 teaches onomatopoeia as a sonic device making language physically reproduce the referent, intensifying imagery's vividness. Onomatopoeia is more common in narrative and poetry than analytical essay but appears in descriptive passages. The AP rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) rubric rewards students who identify onomatopoeia and analyze its rhetorical effect - typically grounding abstract claims in sensory immediacy, building pathos through embodied response. Nature writers and memoirists deploy onomatopoeia to draw readers into scenes through sound.",
