      "explanation": "Syntax is the arrangement of words in sentences - sentence structure, word order, length, and complexity - the second primary stylistic dimension alongside diction. AP English Language Unit 4 treats syntax as central to the rhetorical analysis FRQ (Q2) because syntactic choices shape pacing, emphasis, and reader experience independently of vocabulary. Sophisticated rhetorical analysts move beyond identifying sentence types to claims about syntactic effect: short sentences for punch, long periodic sentences for suspension, parallel structure for emphasis. Orwell's 'Politics and the English Language' (1946) addresses syntax directly, advocating short Anglo-Saxon constructions over Latinate inversions.",
