      "correctText": "Source grouping",
      "topic": "Synthesis Strategy",
      "explanation": "Source grouping is the strategy of categorizing sources by viewpoint, theme, or rhetorical function before drafting - mapping the AP synthesis FRQ (Q1) packet to find natural argumentative structures. AP English Language Unit 8 teaches source grouping as a prewriting technique: students might group sources by position (pro/con/qualified), by type (data/anecdote/expert), or by argumentative function (establishing problem, proposing solution, anticipating objection). The grouping reveals the synthesis architecture: which sources team up on each major claim, which oppose, which qualify. The AP synthesis FRQ rubric rewards essays whose organization emerges from genuine source relationships rather than source-by-source plodding.",
