      "correctText": "Cross-source analysis",
      "topic": "Synthesis Strategy",
      "explanation": "Cross-source analysis is the practice of comparing two or more sources directly within the synthesis essay - putting them in conversation rather than treating each in isolation. AP English Language Unit 8 teaches cross-source analysis as a sophistication marker: the AP synthesis FRQ (Q1) rubric (Row C, 1 point) rewards essays that engage source relationships, especially when sources disagree or qualify each other. Cross-source analysis demonstrates that the writer has read the packet as a whole, not just mined isolated quotes. Strong AP synthesis essays often include paragraphs that explicitly juxtapose sources: 'While Source A claims X, Source D's data complicates the picture by showing Y.'",
