      "correctText": "Antagonist",
      "topic": "Character",
      "explanation": "Antagonist embodies the opposing force, person, or system that resists the protagonist. In Atwood's The Handmaid's Tale (1985) the Republic of Gilead itself is the antagonist of Offred; in Conrad's Heart of Darkness (1899) Kurtz functions as both antagonist and dark double for Marlow. AP Lit Q2 FRQ analysis (6-point rubric, Row B) rewards students who frame antagonists as instruments of theme — not mere villains. Sophistication (Row C, 1 pt) emerges when students show how the antagonist's role complicates rather than confirms moral certainty.",
