      "explanation": "Character arc is the trajectory of change a character undergoes across a narrative. Janie Crawford in Hurston's Their Eyes Were Watching God (1937) arcs from voicelessness to self-possession through three marriages; Okonkwo in Achebe's Things Fall Apart (1958) arcs from cultural dominance to suicide. AP Lit Q3 literary-argument FRQs (6-point rubric, all rows) reward students who trace specific catalysts of arc and defend an interpretation linking change to thematic meaning. Sophistication (Row C, 1 pt) emerges through complex argument.",
