      "explanation": "Interior monologue is the rendering of a character's thoughts in continuous prose, typically signaled by stylistic shift from external narration. Joyce's Ulysses (1922) develops it across multiple consciousnesses; Woolf's To the Lighthouse (1927) deploys it through free indirect discourse. AP Lit Q2 prose-analysis FRQs (6-point rubric, Row B, 4 pts) reward students who name the technique precisely and explain how syntactic markers — fragments, association, internal address — generate psychological realism and thematic depth. Sophistication (Row C) emerges through analytic precision.",
