      "explanation": "Climax is the peak of conflict where the protagonist's fate pivots. In Sophocles's Oedipus Rex it is Oedipus's recognition of his patricide and incest; in Conrad's Heart of Darkness (1899) it is Kurtz's 'The horror! The horror!' On the AP Lit Q2 prose-analysis FRQ, examiners want students to identify climax not as a moment of action but as a turning point in characterization or theme. The 6-point rubric's sophistication point (Row C) is earned by demonstrating that the climax's craft choices generate complex interpretive meaning.",
