      "explanation": "Sonnet is a 14-line lyric in iambic pentameter, originated by Giacomo da Lentini in 13th-century Sicily, perfected by Petrarch (Canzoniere, 14th c.), and naturalized in English by Wyatt, Surrey, and Shakespeare (Sonnets, 1609). Petrarchan and Shakespearean forms both rotate on a structural turn (volta). Donne's Holy Sonnets (1633), Hopkins's terrible sonnets, and Heaney's Glanmore Sonnets extend the tradition. AP Lit Q1 poetry-analysis FRQs (6-point rubric, Row B, 4 pts) reward students who explain how sonnet form generates meaning.",
