      "explanation": "Iamb is a metrical foot of one unstressed syllable followed by one stressed (u /), as in 'to-DAY.' It is the dominant foot of English verse because English speech rhythm naturally falls into iambic patterns. Shakespeare's blank verse, Donne's Holy Sonnets, Frost's 'Stopping by Woods on a Snowy Evening' (1923), and Heaney's lyrics use iambs. AP Lit Q1 poetry-analysis FRQs (6-point rubric, Row B, 4 pts) reward students who identify iambic patterns and explain how variations (trochaic inversion, spondee substitution) generate emphasis and meaning.",
