      "explanation": "Stream of consciousness is a narrative technique rendering the unstructured flow of a character's mind — sensations, memories, half-formed thoughts. Joyce perfects it in Ulysses (1922, especially Molly Bloom's closing monologue); Woolf deploys it in Mrs Dalloway (1925) and To the Lighthouse (1927); Faulkner uses it across The Sound and the Fury (1929). AP Lit Q2 prose-analysis FRQs (6-point rubric, Row B, 4 pts) reward students who name stream of consciousness precisely and explain how syntactic fragmentation generates psychological realism and thematic depth.",
