      "explanation": "Quadrat sampling estimates population density of sessile or slow-moving species by counting individuals in randomly placed sample plots (quadrats, typically 1 m^2) and extrapolating to total area. Frederick Clements pioneered quadrat methods in plant ecology around 1900. Total population estimate = (count per quadrat) x (total area / quadrat area). Used for trees, wildflowers, intertidal organisms, soil arthropods. Random placement avoids bias; systematic grid placement captures spatial heterogeneity. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 3.3) frequently provide quadrat data and require students to calculate density and extrapolate population — also testing precision via replication and sample-size calculations.",
