      "correctText": "Minimum viable population",
      "topic": "Sampling Populations",
      "explanation": "Minimum Viable Population (MVP) is the smallest number of individuals required for a population to persist with a high probability (e.g., 95% chance of survival over 100+ years) given environmental and genetic stochasticity. Mark Shaffer coined the term in 1981. Below MVP, inbreeding depression, genetic drift, and demographic randomness drive extinction. The classic '50/500 rule' (Ian Franklin 1980; Russell Lande 1988): 50 individuals to avoid short-term inbreeding, 500 to maintain long-term evolutionary potential. Modern Population Viability Analysis (PVA) models give species-specific MVPs. Florida panther MVP analysis prompted 1995 Texas cougar introductions. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 9.7) connect MVP to Endangered Species Act protection.",
