      "explanation": "Chlorination disinfects sewage effluent and drinking water by killing pathogens with chlorine (gaseous Cl2 or sodium hypochlorite NaOCl). Began in US drinking water in Jersey City NJ (1908, John Leal MD), dramatically reducing typhoid and cholera (typhoid death rate dropped 90% in early 20th century). Disinfection byproducts (DBPs) — trihalomethanes (THMs, including chloroform) and haloacetic acids — form when chlorine reacts with organic matter; classified as suspected carcinogens, regulated under Safe Drinking Water Act (1974). Alternative disinfectants: chloramines (longer-lasting residual), ozone (no DBPs but no residual), UV light (no chemicals but no residual). The 1993 Milwaukee Cryptosporidium outbreak (400,000 sickened, 100 deaths) prompted SDWA Surface Water Treatment Rule. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 8.13) test trade-offs.",
