      "explanation": "Sewage sludge (biosolids when treated to Class A standards) is the semi-solid residue from sewage-treatment processes. US generates ~7 million dry tons/year. Disposal options: land application as fertilizer (~50% — requires meeting EPA 503 standards for pathogens, heavy metals, organic contaminants), incineration (~15%), landfilling (~30%), surface impoundment. Class A biosolids (pathogen-free) can be sold to public; Class B (treated but with detectable pathogens) restricted to non-food crops. Concerns: heavy metals (Cd, Pb), pharmaceuticals, PFAS (forever chemicals) accumulating in agricultural soils. Some states (Maine 2022) banning land application due to PFAS contamination. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 8.13) integrate disposal options and emerging contaminants.",
