      "correctText": "Primary treatment",
      "topic": "Sewage Treatment",
      "explanation": "Primary sewage treatment is mechanical/physical: screening removes large debris (sticks, rags, plastic); grit chambers settle sand/gravel; primary clarifiers (sedimentation tanks) settle suspended solids and skim grease/oil. Removes ~50-70% of suspended solids and ~25-40% of BOD (Biochemical Oxygen Demand). Resulting sludge is sent for further treatment or disposal. Primary treatment alone is insufficient — secondary biological treatment is required by the Clean Water Act (1972) for US municipalities. Before secondary treatment was required, many cities discharged primary-treated sewage directly into rivers (Boston Harbor until 1991 court order, $4.5 billion Deer Island upgrade). AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 8.13) test the three-stage sequence.",
