      "topic": "Sewage Treatment",
      "explanation": "Tertiary (advanced) sewage treatment removes specific dissolved pollutants left after secondary treatment — phosphorus (chemical precipitation with alum/iron salts), nitrogen (biological nitrification-denitrification), heavy metals, pharmaceuticals, and pathogens. Required where receiving waters are nutrient-sensitive (Chesapeake Bay watershed, Lake Tahoe). More expensive than secondary — adds ~50-100% to treatment costs. Membrane bioreactors and reverse osmosis can produce water of drinking quality (used in Singapore's NEWater, Orange County CA's Groundwater Replenishment). Removing pharmaceuticals (estrogens, antidepressants, antibiotics) is an active research area — current secondary treatment leaves ~50-90% in effluent. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 8.13) test stage sequence and removal effectiveness.",
