      "correctText": "Secondary succession",
      "topic": "Succession",
      "explanation": "Secondary succession occurs on disturbed land where soil remains intact — after forest fire, clearcut, abandoned farm field, or hurricane. Faster than primary succession (decades vs centuries) because seed bank, root systems, and nutrients persist. Classic example: New England forests regrowing on abandoned 19th-century farms (now ~80% forested again per Thoreau's Walden journals). After the 1988 Yellowstone fires (793,000 acres burned), lodgepole pine regrew from serotinous cones that require fire heat to open — a fire-adapted species. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 2.7) require students to trace stages (annual weeds to perennials to shrubs to early-successional trees like aspen and birch to climax forest like oak-hickory).",
