      "correctText": "Primary succession",
      "topic": "Succession",
      "explanation": "Primary succession begins on lifeless substrate with no pre-existing soil — bare rock from glacial retreat, volcanic lava, or new sand dunes. Pioneer species (lichens, then mosses) slowly weather rock and build initial soil over centuries. Classic example: Mount St. Helens after the 1980 eruption — primary succession on lava flows is just beginning to support grasses 45 years later. Glacier Bay AK (William Cooper's classic 1923 study; Cooper retraced glacial retreat from 1794 onward) shows ~200 years from bare rock to spruce-hemlock climax forest. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 2.7) require students to distinguish from secondary succession and explain the slow timescale of soil formation (~1 cm per 500-1000 yr).",
