      "correctText": "Nitrogen cycle",
      "topic": "Sustainability",
      "explanation": "The nitrogen cycle moves N between atmosphere (78% N2 inert), biosphere (organic compounds), and lithosphere. Five key processes: (1) N-fixation by Rhizobium bacteria in legume root nodules (and lightning ~5%, and the industrial Haber-Bosch process — Fritz Haber Nobel 1918 — which now fixes more N annually than all natural processes combined); (2) nitrification by Nitrosomonas (NH4+ to NO2-) and Nitrobacter (NO2- to NO3-); (3) assimilation by plants (NO3- and NH4+ uptake); (4) ammonification by decomposers; (5) denitrification by anaerobic bacteria (NO3- to N2). Synthetic fertilizers cause eutrophication and the Gulf of Mexico dead zone. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 1.10) require students to name all five processes and identify Rhizobium.",
