      "correctText": "Invasive species",
      "topic": "Sustainability",
      "explanation": "Invasive species are non-native organisms that establish, spread rapidly, and cause economic or ecological harm. Second leading cause of biodiversity loss globally (after habitat loss) per IUCN. ~50,000 non-native species are established in the US, causing ~$120 billion/yr damages (Pimentel 2005). Success factors: lack of co-evolved predators/pathogens/competitors, high reproductive rates, broad tolerance. Pathways: ballast water (zebra mussels 1988 Great Lakes), pet/horticulture trade (Burmese python in Everglades, kudzu vine since 1876 Centennial Exposition), hitchhikers on cargo (emerald ash borer in wood pallets, 2002 Detroit). The 1900 Lacey Act, 1990 Nonindigenous Aquatic Nuisance Prevention and Control Act, and 1992 NISA regulate trade. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 9.7).",
