      "correctText": "Logistic growth",
      "topic": "Sustainability",
      "explanation": "Logistic growth: dN/dt = rN(1 - N/K), producing an S-shaped sigmoidal curve. Pierre-Francois Verhulst derived it 1838; Raymond Pearl rediscovered and applied 1920 with Drosophila experiments. Population grows exponentially when N << K, decelerates as N approaches K, stabilizes at N = K. Maximum sustainable yield (MSY) occurs at N = K/2 — the harvest-management target under the Magnuson-Stevens Fishery Conservation and Management Act (1976). The Atlantic cod collapse off Newfoundland (1992 — 99% population loss prompting Canadian moratorium that put 30,000 fishers out of work, still not recovered) shows what happens when harvest exceeds MSY long-term. AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 3.4, 5.10) require students to calculate growth at various N/K ratios and identify MSY.",
