      "topic": "Toxicity",
      "explanation": "Dose-response curves plot biological response vs dose, showing how toxicity scales with exposure. Most curves are sigmoidal (S-shaped): low doses produce no observable effect (NOAEL, no observed adverse effect level), then response rises through LD50 to maximum effect. Threshold dose-response (most chemicals) assumes some safe dose below which no effect; non-threshold (carcinogens, mutagens, ionizing radiation) assumes any dose carries some risk. Paracelsus (16th century) coined 'the dose makes the poison.' Some curves are non-monotonic (e.g., endocrine disruptors) where low doses cause MORE effect than higher (hormesis). AP Environmental Science FRQs (CED Unit 8.8) require students to read curves, identify NOAEL, LD50, and apply safety factors.",
