      "explanation": "Albert Camus (1913-1960) is an AP French Language major Francophone author. L'Étranger (1942) introduces Meursault, an emotionally detached pied-noir clerk in colonial Algiers who kills an Arab man under the burning sun. La Peste (1947) allegorizes occupation and resistance through a plague-stricken city. Camus's philosophy of l'absurde holds that humans must create meaning in a silent universe. He won the Nobel Prize in Literature (1957). Vocabulary: l'absurde, la révolte, la solidarité, l'existentialisme, l'engagement. AP cultural comparison essays scoring 5 examine Camus's pied-noir identity and his refusal of facile political alignments.",
