      "correctText": "Albert Camus",
      "topic": "Vocabulary of Identity",
      "explanation": "Albert Camus (1913-1960) is the AP French Language Algerian-born French novelist and philosopher who won the Nobel Prize in Literature (1957). Major works: L'Étranger (1942), Le Mythe de Sisyphe (1942), La Peste (1947), L'Homme révolté (1951). His philosophy of l'absurde (absurdism) holds that humans must create meaning in a meaningless universe through revolt and solidarity. He resisted both Nazi occupation and Stalinist communism. Vocabulary: l'absurde, la révolte, la solidarité, l'existentialisme, l'engagement. AP cultural comparison essays scoring 5 examine Camus's pied-noir (Algerian-French) identity and his refusal of easy political alignments.",
