      "id": "ap-latin-pe-040",
      "prompt": "In Caesar's ethnographic descriptions, what is the principal distinction he draws between Germani (across the Rhine) and Gauls?",
      "choices": [
        "Germani are urbanized traders; Gauls are nomadic.",
        "Germani have no Druids and no formal sacrifices, devote themselves to war and hunting, and (according to Caesar) hold no settled private land; Gauls are more sedentary, agrarian, and stratified with Druids and equites.",
        "Germani worship Roman gods; Gauls do not.",
        "Germani serve in the Roman legions; Gauls always oppose Rome."
      ],
      "correctText": "Germani have no Druids and no formal sacrifices, devote themselves to war and hunting, and (according to Caesar) hold no settled private land; Gauls are more sedentary, agrarian, and stratified with Druids and equites.",
      "topic": "Culture",
      "explanation": "In BG 6, Caesar contrasts the two peoples ethnographically: Gauls have an organized priestly caste (Druids), a knightly class (equites), elaborate ritual (including the wicker-man sacrifice), and settled agriculture. The Germani, he claims, have none of these — they reassign land yearly, focus on warfare and hunting, and worship only what they can see (sun, fire, moon). The contrast serves Caesar's literary and political purposes as much as ethnographic accuracy.",
