      "correctText": "Accusative of place to which (with names of countries, often without preposition in poetry)",
      "topic": "Grammar/Syntax",
      "explanation": "'Italiam' is accusative singular feminine of Italia. Although standard prose normally uses 'ad' or 'in' + accusative for 'to a country,' Vergil (and Latin poetry generally) freely omits the preposition with country names. With city names and small islands, the preposition is omitted even in prose.",
      "domain": "Practice Exam",
