      "id": "ap-latin-pe-029",
      "prompt": "Identify the type and translate this conditional sentence: 'Si veniat, gaudeam.'",
      "choices": [
        "Simple present: 'If he comes, I am glad.'",
        "Future more vivid: 'If he comes, I shall be glad.'",
        "Future less vivid (should-would): 'If he should come, I would be glad.'",
        "Contrary to fact present: 'If he were coming, I would be glad.'"
      ],
      "correctText": "Future less vivid (should-would): 'If he should come, I would be glad.'",
      "topic": "Grammar/Syntax",
      "explanation": "Both verbs are present subjunctive ('veniat,' 'gaudeam'). Present subjunctive in both protasis (si-clause) and apodosis (main clause) = future less vivid (also called 'should-would'). Contrary-to-fact uses imperfect (present) or pluperfect (past) subjunctive.",
      "domain": "Practice Exam",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
