      "id": "ap-latin-pe-027",
      "prompt": "What kind of subordinate clause is 'ut hostes vinceret' in 'Caesar venit ut hostes vinceret' ('Caesar came in order to conquer the enemy')?",
      "choices": [
        "Result clause (ut + subjunctive)",
        "Purpose clause (ut + subjunctive)",
        "Indirect command",
        "Conditional clause"
      ],
      "correctText": "Purpose clause (ut + subjunctive)",
      "topic": "Grammar/Syntax",
      "explanation": "'ut' + subjunctive expresses purpose: 'in order to / so that he might conquer.' Result clauses also use ut + subjunctive but typically have a 'so/such' word (tam, ita, tantus, talis) in the main clause and translate as '(so that as a result).' Negative purpose uses 'ne,' negative result uses 'ut non.'",
