      "topic": "Aggregate Demand",
      "explanation": "Short-run aggregate supply (SRAS) shows the positive relationship between the price level and real output when input prices are sticky. Firms supply more when output prices rise faster than (fixed) input costs, increasing profit margins. SRAS shifts left when input costs rise (negative supply shock, rising wages) and right when input costs fall or productivity rises. In the AD-AS model, the SR equilibrium is the AD-SRAS intersection; if this differs from LRAS (Y*), a gap exists that drives long-run adjustment via changing wages and prices.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
