      "correctText": "Aggregate supply",
      "topic": "Aggregate Demand",
      "explanation": "Aggregate supply (AS) is the total quantity of real output producers are willing and able to supply at each price level. Short-run AS (SRAS) slopes upward because some input prices (wages, contracts) are sticky; Long-run AS (LRAS) is vertical at potential GDP (Y*) because all prices are flexible. Supply-side shocks, input cost changes, and productivity shifts move SRAS; changes in resources, technology, or institutions move LRAS. AP FRQs routinely distinguish SRAS shocks (short-run) from LRAS shifts (long-run growth).",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
      "sourceAuthority": "mr-macs-jeopardy",
      "assessmentSourceId": "jeopardy-ap-macroeconomics",
