      "correctText": "Aggregate demand",
      "topic": "Aggregate Demand",
      "explanation": "Aggregate demand (AD) is the total planned spending on final goods and services in an economy at each price level: AD = C + I + G + (X − M). The AD curve slopes downward (wealth effect, interest-rate effect, open-economy effect). AD shifts right when any component rises for non-price reasons (e.g., tax cuts raise C, lower interest rates raise I, government stimulus raises G, currency depreciation raises NX). AD shifts left during recessions, financial crises, or contractionary policy. Identifying the direction and size of AD shifts is the most frequently tested AD-AS skill on AP FRQs.",
