      "correctText": "Contractionary fiscal policy",
      "topic": "Aggregate Supply",
      "explanation": "Contractionary fiscal policy uses decreases in government spending (↓G) or increases in taxes (↑T) to shift AD left, lowering the price level and cooling output — the prescription for an inflationary gap. Effects are amplified by the spending and tax multipliers. The 1990s Clinton-era budget surplus, the 2011 U.S. sequester cuts, and post-financial-crisis eurozone austerity are examples. Risk: if the economy weakens unexpectedly, contractionary policy can deepen a recession. AP FRQs require students to trace the AD-AS graph showing AD shifting left, price level falling, and real GDP falling.",
