      "correctText": "Tax multiplier",
      "topic": "Aggregate Supply",
      "explanation": "The tax multiplier measures the impact of a lump-sum tax change on equilibrium real GDP: Tax Multiplier = −MPC / (1 − MPC) = −MPC × Spending Multiplier. It is negative (a tax cut raises GDP; a tax increase lowers it) and smaller in absolute value than the spending multiplier because some of a tax cut is saved (not spent). If MPC = 0.8, the tax multiplier = −4 and the spending multiplier = 5. AP FRQs may provide MPC and ask students to compute the change in GDP from a tax change, noting the multiplier is always 1 smaller in absolute value than the spending multiplier.",
