      "explanation": "The business cycle describes the recurrent fluctuations of real GDP around its long-run trend: expansion (recovery through peak), contraction (recession through trough), and repeat. A recession is technically two consecutive quarters of negative real GDP growth. The NBER's Business Cycle Dating Committee officially identifies peaks and troughs. Unemployment rises during contractions (Okun's Law: each 1% rise in unemployment ≈ 2% fall in real GDP below potential). Fiscal and monetary policy aim to smooth the cycle, moderating both peaks and troughs.",
