      "explanation": "Central bank credibility refers to the public's trust that the central bank will honor its stated inflation target and not succumb to political pressure to over-stimulate. High credibility anchors inflation expectations (π^e) near the target, shifting the SRPC downward and making disinflation less costly (less unemployment). The Fed established credibility through Volcker's aggressive disinflation (1979–1983). Credibility is maintained by transparency (forward guidance), operational independence, and consistent policy. Loss of credibility (e.g., excessive money printing) risks unanchoring expectations and requires painful recessions to restore.",
