      "explanation": "An inferior good is one for which demand falls as consumer income rises (negative income elasticity, YED < 0). Examples: instant noodles, bus rides, generic-brand products. As income rises, consumers substitute toward higher-quality normal goods. AP FRQ trap: 'inferior' is an economic term (demand falls with income), NOT a statement about product quality. Do not confuse with Giffen goods (demand rises as price rises — a special inferior good).",
