      "explanation": "A demand shifter is any non-price factor that moves the entire demand curve left or right. The main shifters (TRIBE): Tastes, Related-good prices (substitutes/complements), Income, Buyer count, and Expectations. A rightward shift means consumers want more at every price; leftward, less. Changes in price cause movement along the demand curve — not a shift.",
