      "explanation": "Ideal transformer power conservation: V_p I_p = V_s I_s (power in = power out). Combined with V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p (turns ratio), gives I_s/I_p = N_p/N_s (current ratio inverse to voltage ratio). On the AP equation sheet. AP Physics 2 FRQs (Science Practice 5: Calculations): a step-up transformer with V_s = 10 V_p has I_s = I_p/10. Used in power grids to step up generation voltage (low I, low I^2 R loss) for long-distance transmission, then step down for consumer voltage (higher I, lower V — but consumed at appliance, so I^2 R loss is local and minimal). Real transformers ~95-99% efficient; losses go to heat in iron core (eddy currents, hysteresis).",
