      "correctText": "Confounding variable",
      "topic": "Experimental Control",
      "explanation": "A confounding variable is an unmeasured factor that varies with the IV and could explain changes in the DV, making it impossible to attribute results to the IV alone. For example, if participants who drink caffeine also get more sleep, sleep is a confound. Random assignment is the primary tool for distributing confounds equally across groups, which is why true experiments permit causal claims but correlational studies do not.",
