      "correctText": "Double-blind procedure",
      "topic": "Experimental Control",
      "explanation": "In a double-blind procedure, neither the participants nor the researchers administering the study know who is in the experimental or control group. This prevents both placebo effects (participant expectation) and experimenter bias (unintentional cues from researchers). Drug trials routinely use this design: a pharmacist assigns pills by code, and neither the patient nor the prescribing clinician knows which is active.",
