      "explanation": "Stanley Milgram's obedience studies (1961-62), conducted partly in response to Nazi war crime trials, found that 65% of ordinary Yale-area adults would administer shocks up to 450V to a screaming stranger when instructed by an authority figure. Milgram identified key factors: proximity of victim (reduced obedience when face-to-face), distance from authority (reduced obedience when authority was by phone), and group context (seeing others refuse dramatically lowered obedience).",
