      "correctText": "Retroactive interference",
      "topic": "Memory Errors",
      "explanation": "Retroactive interference occurs when newly learned information disrupts memory of previously learned material — new material acts backward on old memories. Learning Italian after Spanish may cause you to forget some Spanish you once knew. Studying similar material back-to-back (English vocab, then Spanish vocab) creates maximum retroactive interference; interleaving different subjects reduces it. Sleep after studying helps, since there's no new interfering input.",
