      "correctText": "Proactive interference",
      "topic": "Memory Errors",
      "explanation": "Proactive interference occurs when older, previously learned information disrupts the encoding or retrieval of newer information — old material interferes forward into new learning. For example, a student who learned Spanish first may have old Spanish vocabulary interfere with learning Italian. The word 'proactive' means 'forward-acting.' Distinguishing proactive (old disrupts new) from retroactive (new disrupts old) interference is a frequent AP exam point.",
