      "correctText": "Operant conditioning",
      "topic": "Operant Conditioning",
      "explanation": "Operant conditioning (B.F. Skinner) is learning in which behavior is strengthened by reinforcement or weakened by punishment, occurring in a Skinner box where animals press a lever to receive food. Unlike classical conditioning (involuntary reflexes), operant conditioning involves voluntary behavior shaped by consequences. Schedules of reinforcement control response rates: fixed-ratio schedules produce high response rates with post-reinforcement pauses; variable-ratio (slot machines) produce highest resistance to extinction.",
