      "prompt": "Attachment pattern marked by trust and comfort with caregiver.",
      "choices": [
        "Secure attachment",
        "Dependent variable",
        "Erikson",
        "Identity vs. role confusion"
      ],
      "correctText": "Secure attachment",
      "topic": "Social Learning",
      "explanation": "Secure attachment (Type B in Ainsworth's classification) is the healthy pattern in which infants use their caregiver as a secure base for exploration, show distress on separation, and are easily soothed upon reunion. About 60-65% of American infants show this pattern. Securely attached children develop better social competence, emotion regulation, and academic performance. Sensitive, responsive caregiving is the key predictor — reinforcing the importance of early social experience.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
