      "correctText": "Prejudice",
      "topic": "Traits + Self",
      "explanation": "Prejudice is an unjustifiable, typically negative, attitude toward a group based on stereotypes rather than individual evaluation. It has cognitive (stereotypes), affective (hostility or fear), and behavioral (discrimination) components. Sources include in-group/out-group categorization, scapegoating, and social learning. Sherif's Robbers Cave experiment (1954) demonstrated how competition between groups creates prejudice, and that superordinate goals — shared challenges requiring cooperation — can reduce it.",
