      "topic": "Genre and Voice",
      "explanation": "La apóstrofe (apostrophe) is the AP Spanish Literature device where a speaker addresses an absent person, abstract entity, or the reader directly. Cortés apostrophizes Carlos V; Bolívar apostrophizes his British correspondent; Sor Juana addresses los hombres in her redondillas. Effect: builds intimacy, transfers authority from speaker to addressee, or rebukes a class of people. Genre conventions for letter (carta) and decimal poetry rely on apostrophe. AP analysis essays score 5 by examining how the choice of addressee shapes argument: writing to a king demands deference; writing to los hombres necios licenses critique; writing to a Caribbean reader (Jamaica) builds anti-Spanish alliance.",
