      "explanation": "El ritmo afroantillano and la poesía negra are AP Spanish Literature formal techniques pioneered by Nicolás Guillén (Cuba), Luis Palés Matos (Puerto Rico), and others in the 1920s-30s. The poems use African drum patterns, jitanjáforas (nonsense rhythmic words like sóngoro cosongo), onomatopoeic syllables, and Yoruba vocabulary to evoke afroantillano experience. Important: the technique is sometimes critiqued as caricature when used by white poets, more authentic when used by Black or mixed-race writers like Guillén. AP analysis essays score 5 by examining how rhythmic form connects to political claims: linguistic dignity for Afro-Caribbean voices, resistance to European prosodic norms.",
