      "correctText": "Sqrt n p (1 minus p)",
      "topic": "Binomial Distribution",
      "explanation": "The standard deviation of a binomial distribution is sigma_X = sqrt(n*p*(1-p)). AP Statistics Unit 4 derives this from Var(X) = n*p*(1-p), an immediate consequence of variance additivity for the n independent Bernoulli trials each contributing variance p*(1-p). The 2018 FRQ tested binomial SD: for n = 20, p = 0.75, sigma = sqrt(20*0.75*0.25) = sqrt(3.75) approximately 1.94. Rubric points required correct units AND interpretation. The maximum of sigma occurs at p = 0.5, where p*(1-p) = 0.25 is largest - useful for sample-size planning when p is unknown.",
