      "prompt": "As df increases, chi-square shape approaches.",
      "choices": [
        "Binomial distribution",
        "Chi-square table",
        "Normal",
        "Right skewed"
      ],
      "correctText": "Normal",
      "topic": "Chi-Square Distribution",
      "explanation": "As degrees of freedom increase, the chi-square distribution approaches a NORMAL distribution. AP Statistics Unit 8 explains this via the Central Limit Theorem: chi-square = sum of df independent squared Normal variables, and a sum of independent variables converges to Normal by CLT. The 2018 FRQ tested this property conceptually: for df = 100, the chi-square is approximately Normal with mean 100 and SD = sqrt(200). Rubric points required citing the CLT-based limit AND the mean df and variance 2df. For df >= 30, Normal approximation is acceptable; for smaller df, the right-skew matters and chi-square tables or technology are needed.",
