      "correctText": "Quadruple sample size",
      "topic": "Confidence Intervals",
      "explanation": "To halve the margin of error of a confidence interval, sample size must be QUADRUPLED. AP Statistics Unit 6 derives this from ME proportional to 1/sqrt(n): halving ME requires multiplying n by 4. The 2019 FRQ tested sample-size planning: a current poll has ME = 4 percent at n = 600; to achieve ME = 2 percent, n must be 2400. Rubric points required showing the quadrupling reasoning AND arriving at the correct n. This 'square-root law' of precision is why doubling poll accuracy is so expensive - and why national polls are typically capped at n = 1000-2000, beyond which further precision gains are minimal.",
