      "correctText": "Standard error doubles",
      "topic": "Confidence Intervals",
      "explanation": "The width of a confidence interval doubles when the standard error doubles - all else equal. AP Statistics Unit 6 emphasizes the CI half-width (margin of error) ME = z*-times-SE, so widening SE proportionally widens the CI. The 2018 FRQ tested CI width: if a survey doubles its variability (e.g., by sampling a more heterogeneous group), the CI doubles in width. Rubric points required citing both the SE relationship AND a contextual implication: less precision means less informative CI. Conversely, to NARROW a CI by half, SE must be halved - typically requiring 4 times the sample size.",
