      "correctText": "Coefficient of determination",
      "topic": "Correlation",
      "explanation": "The coefficient of determination r-squared is the square of the correlation coefficient - it represents the proportion of variation in y that is explained by the linear regression of y on x. AP Statistics Unit 2 expects students to interpret r-squared in context: e.g., 'r-squared = 0.81 means 81 percent of the variation in exam scores is explained by hours of study.' The remaining 19 percent is residual variation - random noise plus other unmeasured variables. The 2019 FRQ awarded full credit only when students mentioned all three elements: percent, variation in (response), explained by linear regression on (explanatory). Reporting just 'r-squared = 0.81' without context costs at least one rubric point.",
