      "correctText": "Bar chart",
      "topic": "Distributions",
      "explanation": "A bar chart displays the frequency or relative frequency of categorical data, with rectangular bars of equal width whose heights show counts or proportions. Unlike histograms, bar charts have gaps between bars to emphasize discreteness, and the category order is arbitrary or by frequency (Pareto chart). AP Stats Unit 1 expects students to read bar charts, compute relative frequencies, and avoid the common error of treating ordinal categories as quantitative. Two-way bar charts (segmented or side-by-side) display two categorical variables; the 2017 FRQ used a segmented bar chart to test conditional distributions and Simpson's paradox.",
