      "prompt": "Effect of larger sample on CI width.",
      "choices": [
        "Confidence interval",
        "Efficient estimator",
        "s over sqrt n",
        "Narrower CI"
      ],
      "correctText": "Narrower CI",
      "topic": "Estimating Mean",
      "explanation": "A larger sample size produces a NARROWER confidence interval - by the sqrt(n) law of SE. AP Statistics Unit 7 emphasizes that quadrupling n halves the margin of error; doubling n shrinks it by factor sqrt(2). The 2019 FRQ tested sample-size planning for CI width: to halve the margin of error, n must quadruple. Rubric points required citing the square-root relationship AND computing the new n. This is why precision improvements get progressively expensive: going from n = 100 to n = 400 halves the margin; going from n = 400 to n = 1600 halves it again. The square-root law applies to all standard error formulas in inference.",
