      "topic": "Least Squares Line",
      "explanation": "A residual is the difference between an observed value and the predicted value: residual = y - y-hat. AP Statistics Unit 2 uses residuals for three purposes: (1) measuring how far each observation falls from the LSRL, (2) computing s = sqrt(Sigma(residuals^2)/(n-2)) as the standard error of the regression, and (3) constructing residual plots to check linear fit. The 2018 FRQ asked students to compute and interpret a single residual: y = 78, y-hat = 72, residual = +6 means the actual score exceeded the predicted score by 6 points. Positive residuals = above the line; negative = below. Sum of all residuals from an LSRL is always zero.",
