      "explanation": "The least-squares regression line (LSRL) is the straight line y-hat = a + bx that minimizes the sum of squared residuals Sigma(y_i - y-hat_i)^2. Developed by Legendre (1805) and Gauss (1809), least squares is the standard method for fitting a regression line. AP Statistics Unit 2 emphasizes that the LSRL always passes through the point (x-bar, y-bar) and that its slope b = r*(s_y/s_x). The 2018 FRQ asked students to derive the LSRL from summary statistics: given x-bar, y-bar, s_x, s_y, and r, compute b and then a = y-bar - b*x-bar. Reporting both the equation and proper interpretation of slope and intercept in context earns full credit.",
