      "explanation": "Degrees of freedom for a paired t-test is n - 1, where n is the number of PAIRS (not the number of measurements). AP Statistics Unit 7 emphasizes this distinction: for 30 paired measurements (60 individual values), df = 29, NOT 58. The 2019 FRQ tested df identification: students were expected to count pairs and subtract 1. Rubric points required citing pairs - not total measurements. This is the key formula difference between paired and two-sample t: paired uses n - 1 (pairs), while two-sample uses Welch df (often n1 + n2 - 2 ish). Larger df means narrower CIs, so pairing benefits precision both through reduced variability AND additional df flexibility.",
