      "topic": "Matched Pairs",
      "explanation": "In a matched-pairs analysis, the statistic of interest is the MEAN DIFFERENCE d-bar = mean of (X1 - X2) across pairs. AP Statistics Unit 7 emphasizes that pairing reduces variability by eliminating between-subject variation - the same subject's measurements share confounders. The 2018 FRQ tested d-bar computation: for paired before/after weight data with mean difference of -2.5 lbs, students computed d-bar = -2.5. Rubric points required computing the mean of the differences (not the difference of the means - which equals d-bar in symmetric designs but loses pairing benefit when used). Always compute differences first, then their mean.",
