      "correctText": "Reduces variability",
      "topic": "Matched Pairs",
      "explanation": "Pairing improves precision by REDUCING variability - because pair-level differences eliminate between-subject confounders that would otherwise inflate noise. AP Statistics Unit 7 emphasizes that paired designs have smaller SE than equivalent two-sample designs when within-pair correlation is positive. The 2018 FRQ tested this in a drug-efficacy study comparing before/after blood pressure: pairing eliminated patient-to-patient variability in baseline blood pressure, isolating the drug's effect. Rubric points required citing variance reduction as the reason pairing improves power. When within-pair correlation is zero or negative, pairing may not help - but in practice it usually does.",
