      "correctText": "Normal distribution",
      "topic": "Normal Distribution",
      "explanation": "The Normal distribution N(mu, sigma) is the bell-shaped continuous distribution central to AP Statistics. Formalized by Carl Friedrich Gauss in 1809 to model astronomical measurement errors, it has density f(x) = (1/(sigma*sqrt(2*pi))) * exp(-(x-mu)^2/(2*sigma^2)). AP Statistics Unit 4 emphasizes the Normal as the foundation for inference via the Central Limit Theorem (CLT). The Normal arises in nature whenever many small random effects combine additively (e.g., human height, measurement error, IQ scores). The 2019 FRQ tested Normal probability computation using the calculator function normalcdf - rubric points required correct boundary inputs AND context interpretation.",
