      "correctText": "Central Limit Theorem",
      "topic": "Normal Distribution",
      "explanation": "The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) states that the sampling distribution of x-bar (or p-hat) approaches Normal as sample size n grows, regardless of the population distribution. AP Statistics Unit 4 previews the CLT before it is formally introduced in Unit 5. The 2019 FRQ tested the CLT consequence: for any population with mean mu and SD sigma, the sampling distribution of x-bar from n = 50 is approximately N(mu, sigma/sqrt(50)). Rubric points required citing both the mean AND the SD of x-bar. The CLT is the most important theorem in statistics - it justifies using Normal-based inference for means and proportions even when population shape is unknown.",
