      "correctText": "Experiment",
      "topic": "Observational Studies",
      "explanation": "Causation - a true cause-and-effect relationship - can only be established by a well-designed experiment with random assignment of subjects to treatments. AP Statistics Unit 3 emphasizes this as the central principle distinguishing experiments from observational studies: only random assignment guarantees that the groups are balanced on all confounders, allowing causal interpretation of any observed difference. The 2019 FRQ awarded rubric points for students who explicitly cited 'random assignment' (not just 'random sampling') as the key feature supporting causal inference. The Bradford Hill criteria (1965) offer additional standards for causal claims from observational data, but experiments remain the gold standard.",
