      "correctText": "Case-control study",
      "topic": "Observational Studies",
      "explanation": "A case-control study compares subjects WITH a particular outcome (cases) to subjects WITHOUT the outcome (controls), looking BACKWARD in time for differential exposures. AP Statistics Unit 3 introduces case-control as efficient for studying rare outcomes (cancer, rare diseases) where a cohort study would require enormous samples. The 1950 Doll-Hill case-control study comparing lung-cancer patients to controls established the smoking-cancer link a decade before randomized cohort evidence. The 2018 FRQ tested case-control identification and asked students to explain the trade-off: efficient for rare outcomes but vulnerable to recall bias (cases may better remember exposures).",
