      "explanation": "A cohort study follows a group of subjects (the cohort) over time, recording exposures and outcomes as they occur. AP Statistics Unit 3 introduces cohort studies as a stronger observational design than cross-sectional studies because the temporal sequence (exposure before outcome) is preserved, supporting causal inference if confounders can be controlled. The Framingham Heart Study (started 1948, tracking over 5,000 residents of Framingham, MA) is the classic example, having established smoking, blood pressure, and cholesterol as cardiovascular risk factors. The 2017 FRQ asked students to identify the cohort design and explain its advantage over case-control studies.",
