      "correctText": "Sqrt p (1-p) over n",
      "topic": "Sampling Distribution of p-hat",
      "explanation": "The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of p-hat is sigma_p-hat = sqrt(p*(1-p)/n), where p is the population proportion and n is the sample size. AP Statistics Unit 5 derives this from the variance of a binomial X = np-hat divided by n^2. The 2019 FRQ tested SD computation: for p = 0.4 and n = 100, sigma_p-hat = sqrt(0.4*0.6/100) = 0.049. Rubric points required showing the formula AND units. As n increases, sigma_p-hat decreases - quadrupling n halves sigma_p-hat. This is why doubling precision (halving margin of error) requires quadrupling sample size, as tested in Unit 6.",
