      "correctText": "Sampling distribution of x-bar",
      "topic": "Sampling Distribution of x-bar",
      "explanation": "The sampling distribution of x-bar is the probability distribution of the sample mean x-bar across all possible samples of size n from a population with mean mu and SD sigma. AP Statistics Unit 5 introduces this as the foundation for inference about means in Units 7 and 9. By the CLT, x-bar is approximately Normal for sufficiently large n, regardless of population shape. The 2018 FRQ tested sampling distribution understanding: for a population with mu = 100 and sigma = 20, samples of n = 50 give x-bar approximately N(100, 20/sqrt(50)) = N(100, 2.83). Rubric points required citing both the mean and the SD - and noting CLT justification.",
