      "topic": "Sampling Distribution of x-bar",
      "explanation": "The standard deviation of the sampling distribution of x-bar is sigma_x-bar = sigma/sqrt(n), where sigma is the population SD and n is the sample size. AP Statistics Unit 5 derives this from Var(X-bar) = sigma^2/n. The 2018 FRQ tested SD computation: for sigma = 20 and n = 50, sigma_x-bar = 20/sqrt(50) = 2.83. Rubric points required showing the formula AND units. As n increases, sigma_x-bar decreases - quadrupling n halves sigma_x-bar. This is the 'square-root law' of sampling: precision improves with sqrt(n), not n. This is why huge samples are needed for tiny precision gains beyond n = 1000.",
