      "topic": "Sampling Distribution of x-bar",
      "explanation": "The Central Limit Theorem (CLT) is the foundational theorem stating that the sampling distribution of x-bar is approximately Normal for sufficiently large n, regardless of the population's distribution. AP Statistics Unit 5 emphasizes the CLT as the single most important theorem in classical statistics - it justifies using Normal inference even when population shape is unknown. The 2019 FRQ tested CLT application: for a strongly right-skewed population, students were asked at what n the CLT assures approximate Normality of x-bar. Rubric points awarded for citing n >= 30 as the conventional threshold. Larger n is needed for stronger skewness; smaller n suffices for already-Normal populations.",
