      "correctText": "Systematic sample",
      "topic": "Sampling Methods",
      "explanation": "A systematic sample selects every kth individual from an ordered list, after choosing a random start between 1 and k. AP Statistics Unit 3 introduces systematic sampling as practical when no full list exists but a stream of individuals can be sampled (e.g., every 10th customer entering a store). The risk is periodic bias: if the underlying list has a cycle of length k, systematic sampling captures only one phase of the cycle. The 2018 FRQ tested this: a 'every 7th day' systematic sample of a hospital was biased toward Wednesdays, missing weekend variation. When randomness is intact, systematic sampling behaves much like an SRS.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
