      "correctText": "Left skewed",
      "topic": "Shape and Outliers",
      "explanation": "Left-skewed (negatively skewed) distributions have a long left tail - extreme low values pull the mean below the median, so mean < median. Classic examples are test scores with a ceiling (e.g., grades on an easy exam), age at retirement, and lifespan distributions in developed nations. AP Stats Unit 1 expects students to recognize left skew from histograms, boxplots (longer left whisker), and inequality mean < median. The Tukey (1977) five-number summary makes left skew visible: the distance from Q1 to median exceeds the distance from median to Q3. The 2015 FRQ #1 asked students to identify left skew in a test-score distribution and justify median as preferred center.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
