      "correctText": "z-score",
      "topic": "Standardization",
      "explanation": "A z-score z = (x - mu)/sigma standardizes an observation by expressing it as the number of standard deviations from the mean. Introduced in AP Stats Unit 1, z-scores enable comparison across distributions with different units or scales (e.g., comparing SAT score 1400 to ACT score 32). Negative z-scores indicate values below the mean; |z| > 2 is conventionally unusual; |z| > 3 is rare. The z-score is the foundation for the Empirical Rule (68-95-99.7) and for all Normal-distribution probability calculations using tables or calculators. The 2019 FRQ asked students to convert raw scores to z-scores and use them to identify which of two students performed better relative to their respective testing populations.",
