      "explanation": "A rate factor is any variable that changes the speed of a chemical reaction. The major rate factors and their effects: increasing temperature speeds rate (more particles exceed activation energy); increasing reactant concentration speeds rate (more collisions per second); increasing surface area speeds rate (more contact); adding a catalyst lowers activation energy and speeds rate without being consumed; increasing pressure speeds gas-phase reactions. All are explained by collision theory: rate increases when collisions become more frequent or more effective. Arrhenius quantified the temperature effect in 1889 with k = A·exp(−Ea/RT). NYS Regents items test which factors affect rate and why.",
