      "explanation": "Mountain ranges force rising air to cool and drop precipitation on the windward slope (orographic lifting). Once air crosses the summit and descends, it warms by compression, creating a dry rain shadow on the leeward side. The Sierra Nevada creates a classic rain shadow: wet forests on the west face, the Great Basin desert to the east.",
