      "correctText": "wind erosion",
      "topic": "Erosional Agents",
      "explanation": "Wind erosion (deflation and abrasion) is most effective in dry, sparsely vegetated regions. Fine silt (loess) is transported long distances; coarser sand rolls or saltates close to the ground forming dunes. Long Island's South Shore barrier islands include wind-formed dune fields. The ESRT particle-size scale shows silt and sand as the grain sizes most easily moved by typical wind speeds in arid environments.",
