      "topic": "Topographic Maps",
      "explanation": "The contour interval is the constant elevation difference between adjacent contour lines on a topographic map and is always labeled in the map legend. Standard USGS 7.5-minute quads use intervals of 10, 20, or 40 feet depending on terrain relief. NYS Regents items routinely require students to read contour intervals from a given map and calculate the elevation of a point located between two labeled index contours.",
