      "explanation": "Compound interest is interest earned on both the original principal and the accumulated interest from prior periods, following the formula A = P(1 + r/n)^(nt); the longer the time horizon and the higher the rate, the more dramatically wealth grows compared to simple interest. A $5,000 IRA contribution at 7% annual return grows to roughly $38,000 in 30 years with compounding versus $15,500 with simple interest. Starting to save at 22 versus 32 can mean hundreds of thousands of dollars difference at retirement.",
