      "correctText": "Tariff",
      "topic": "Global Trade",
      "explanation": "A tariff is a tax levied on imported goods, raising their domestic price above the world price; it reduces imports, protects domestic producers, and generates government revenue, but raises prices for consumers and creates deadweight loss. Tariffs are measured ad valorem (percentage of value, e.g., 25% on Chinese goods) or specific (per unit). The Smoot-Hawley Tariff Act (1930) raised average rates to nearly 50%, triggering global retaliation, collapsing world trade, and deepening the Great Depression.",
