      "correctText": "Public goods",
      "topic": "Incentives",
      "explanation": "Public goods are non-excludable (you can't prevent anyone from using them) and non-rival (one person's use doesn't reduce availability for others), so private markets undersupply them because firms can't charge free-riders effectively. National defense, lighthouses, and public fireworks displays are classic examples; markets fail here, creating a rationale for government provision funded by taxes. The free-rider problem is why toll-free bridges crowd while private clubs remain less congested.",
