      "correctText": "Fiscal policy",
      "topic": "Inflation",
      "explanation": "Fiscal policy is the use of government spending and taxation by Congress and the President to influence aggregate demand and, by extension, real GDP, unemployment, and the price level. Expansionary fiscal policy (increase G, cut taxes) shifts AD rightward, boosting output but potentially increasing the budget deficit and public debt. Contractionary fiscal policy (cut G, raise taxes) shifts AD leftward to cool inflation. Fiscal policy effectiveness is limited by recognition lags, legislative lags, and the crowding-out effect.",
