      "correctText": "Short-run aggregate supply curve",
      "topic": "Inflation",
      "explanation": "The Short-Run Aggregate Supply (SRAS) curve shows the total real output firms will produce at each price level when some input prices (especially wages and rents) are sticky (slow to adjust); it slopes upward because higher product prices raise profit margins with fixed input costs. Supply shocks (oil price spikes) shift SRAS left, raising the price level and reducing output simultaneously (stagflation). SRAS intersects LRAS and AD to determine the short-run macroeconomic equilibrium.",
