      "correctText": "Supply shock",
      "topic": "Inflation",
      "explanation": "A supply shock is an unexpected event that dramatically changes production costs or capabilities for the entire economy, shifting the SRAS curve; negative shocks (oil price spikes, pandemics, natural disasters) shift SRAS left, raising prices and lowering output — the worst possible combination for policymakers (stagflation). The 1973 OPEC oil embargo was a classic negative supply shock causing stagflation in the U.S. Positive supply shocks (technology breakthroughs, commodity price drops) shift SRAS right, reducing prices and raising output.",
