      "correctText": "Progressive tax",
      "topic": "Public Goods + Taxes",
      "explanation": "A progressive tax imposes higher marginal rates on higher income brackets, so each additional dollar earned above a threshold is taxed at a higher rate; the effective (average) tax rate rises with income but is always below the marginal rate. Under the 2024 U.S. code, a single filer earning $100,000 pays 10% on the first $11,600, 12% on the next tranche, and so on — not 22% on all income. The progressive structure embodies the ability-to-pay principle of tax equity.",
