      "correctText": "Externality",
      "topic": "Regulation + Trade Policy",
      "explanation": "An externality is a cost or benefit from a market transaction that falls on an uninvolved third party, causing markets to over- or underproduce relative to the socially optimal quantity. Negative externalities (factory pollution, traffic congestion) cause markets to produce too much — corrected by Pigouvian taxes or cap-and-trade. Positive externalities (vaccinations, research) cause markets to produce too little — corrected by subsidies. Environmental regulations, carbon taxes, and vaccine mandates are all government responses to externalities.",
