      "correctText": "Externalities",
      "topic": "Regulation + Trade Policy",
      "explanation": "Externalities are the full class of spillover costs and benefits generated by economic activity that fall outside the market transaction — the market price does not include them, causing market failure. Negative externalities push social cost above private cost, leading to overproduction; positive externalities mean social benefit exceeds private benefit, leading to underproduction. The fundamental policy insight is that when negative externalities exist, the socially optimal quantity is below market equilibrium, requiring government intervention to internalize the external cost.",
