      "explanation": "The output gap measures the difference between actual real GDP and potential GDP (what the economy could produce at full employment); a negative gap (actual below potential) is a recessionary gap indicating idle resources, while a positive gap (actual above potential) is an inflationary gap signaling overheating. During the COVID-19 recession, the U.S. output gap was estimated at roughly -10% of GDP in Q2 2020. The output gap guides fiscal and monetary policy responses — stimulus for negative gaps, tightening for positive gaps.",
