      "correctText": "Insurance",
      "topic": "Risk",
      "explanation": "Insurance is a risk-management contract where the insured pays regular premiums to transfer the financial risk of a large, uncertain loss to an insurer who pools premiums from many policyholders. By pooling risk across many customers, insurance companies exploit the law of large numbers to predict claims accurately and remain solvent while protecting individuals from catastrophic losses. Required car insurance in New York and mandated employer health coverage illustrate government recognition that insurance markets suffer from adverse selection and require regulation.",
