      "correctText": "Supply curve shift",
      "topic": "Supply",
      "explanation": "A supply curve shift occurs when a non-price factor changes the quantity supplied at every price level: input prices, technology, number of sellers, government taxes or subsidies, or expectations. A decrease in input costs (cheaper steel) shifts supply rightward, lowering equilibrium price; a supply-disrupting drought shifts supply leftward, raising food prices. The 2021 semiconductor shortage shifted supply left for autos, raising prices and creating months-long waiting lists.",
