      "correctText": "Consumer price index",
      "topic": "Unemployment",
      "explanation": "The CPI measures changes in the price of a fixed market basket of about 80,000 goods and services typically purchased by urban consumers; the BLS publishes it monthly and it is the primary gauge of inflation used to adjust Social Security, federal pensions, and Treasury Inflation-Protected Securities (TIPS). If the CPI rises from 280 to 295, inflation has been 5.4% — real wages fell if nominal wages rose less. Critics note the CPI may overstate inflation due to substitution bias and quality improvements.",
