      "explanation": "The Shakespearean (English) sonnet has 14 lines of iambic pentameter rhyming abab cdcd efef gg, structured as three quatrains and a closing couplet. Surrey developed it; Shakespeare perfected 154 of them. RL.9-10.5 covers form-meaning relationships. Sonnet 18 ('Shall I compare thee to a summer's day') is canonical. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice tests sonnet recognition. AP Lit requires identifying sonnet types and analyzing how form supports meaning. Distinct from the Petrarchan (Italian) sonnet.",
