      "explanation": "The Shakespearean sonnet's closing couplet (lines 13-14) typically resolves the argument, delivers a witty turn, or undermines the previous twelve lines. Sonnet 18 closes 'So long as men can breathe or eyes can see, / So long lives this, and this gives life to thee.' RL.9-10.5 covers form-meaning. AP Lit prizes couplet analysis: does it resolve, ironize, deepen, or undermine? Regents Part 3 source-based arguments may use sonnets. The volta in Shakespearean form often sits at the couplet.",
