      "correctText": "Iambic pentameter",
      "topic": "Sonnet Form",
      "explanation": "Iambic pentameter is the meter of English verse: five iambs per line, each iamb being an unstressed-stressed syllable pair (de-DUM de-DUM de-DUM de-DUM de-DUM). 'Shall I com-PARE thee TO a SUM-mer's DAY?' Shakespeare uses it in sonnets and plays. RL.9-10.5 covers form. AP Lit requires scansion (marking meter) and analysis of metrical variation. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice may test iamb recognition. Substitutions (trochees, spondees) often signal emphasis.",
