      "prompt": "A speaker openly mourning loss with grief-saturated language has this tone.",
      "choices": [
        "Sardonic",
        "Elegiac",
        "Indignant",
        "Foil"
      ],
      "correctText": "Elegiac",
      "topic": "Tone Vocabulary",
      "explanation": "Elegiac tone laments loss, like Antony's 'O mighty Caesar! Dost thou lie so low?' (Julius Caesar 3.1). Elegiac writing (the elegy tradition: Milton's Lycidas, Shelley's Adonais, Whitman's 'When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom'd') mourns specifically. RI.9-10.4 covers tone. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice tests advanced tone vocabulary. AP Lit demands precise tone vocabulary, especially for elegiac, threnodic, or lamenting prose. Wiesel's restrained tone differs from elegiac tone (which is more openly grieving).",
