      "explanation": "Hawthorne's Pearl is partly realistic child and partly allegorical embodiment of Hester's sin and the 'A' itself. Pearl's wildness, fey speech, and obsession with the scarlet letter mark her as allegorical figure within an otherwise realist novel. RL.11-12.4 covers symbolic meaning; RL.11-12.5 covers structure. AP Lit prizes recognition of allegorical characters. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on guilt, motherhood, or shame use The Scarlet Letter.",
