      "explanation": "Hawthorne's narrator in The Scarlet Letter is editorial omniscient: he comments on character motives, judges Puritan society from a 19th-century perspective, and tells readers what to think about events. Contrast with neutral omniscience (which just reports). RL.11-12.6 covers POV choices. AP Lit prizes recognition of editorial narration. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on shame, morality, or history use this novel. Hawthorne's narrator embodies American Romanticism's critique of Puritan severity.",
