      "topic": "Author's Stance",
      "explanation": "Critical stance exposes problems and advocates change. Upton Sinclair's The Jungle (1906), Jacob Riis's How the Other Half Lives (1890), Ida B. Wells's anti-lynching writings, and Rachel Carson's Silent Spring (1962) exemplify critical stance. RI.11-12.6 covers stance. Regents Part 3 source-based argument essays often include critical-stance texts. AP Lang treats muckraking journalism as canonical American rhetoric. Critical stance combines exposure (informing) with advocacy (persuading).",
