      "correctText": "Quiet defiance",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Hawthorne characterizes Hester Prynne through her embroidered, elaborate 'A' (which she made more ornate than the magistrates intended) and her decision to stay in Boston rather than flee. Hester's quiet defiance becomes the novel's moral center. RL.11-12.3 covers complex character development. AP Lit prizes character analysis of moral ambiguity. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on shame, identity, or gender use this novel. Hester anticipates later American heroines of refusal.",
