      "prompt": "Holden Caulfield, Huck Finn, and Jay Gatsby's first-person voices share this narrator quality.",
      "choices": [
        "Unreliable narration",
        "Self-realization",
        "Gettysburg Address",
        "Moral redemption"
      ],
      "correctText": "Unreliable narration",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Unreliable narrators have compromised credibility due to bias, age, moral failing, or madness. Nick Carraway claims to 'reserve judgments' while shaping every characterization; Huck Finn's child voice misreads adult morality; Holden's lies announce themselves. RL.11-12.6 covers narrator analysis. AP Lit demands precise distinction: limited (just bounded knowledge), unreliable (questionable accuracy), naive (innocent of context). Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on perspective use these texts. Wayne Booth coined the term in 1961.",
