      "correctText": "Self-realization",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Hurston traces Janie Crawford through three marriages (Logan, Joe Starks, Tea Cake) to selfhood. The pear tree blossoming in Chapter 2 symbolizes Janie's romantic ideal; her final return to Eatonville with 'the horizon' in her hand marks her self-realization. RL.11-12.3 covers character arcs. AP Lit treats Their Eyes as canonical for character analysis. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on gender and self-determination use Hurston's novel.",
