      "correctText": "Self-made identity",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Fitzgerald reveals Gatsby's true identity (James Gatz, North Dakota farm boy) in Chapter 6, gradually exposing his self-invention through Dan Cody's yacht, bootlegging, and Oxford-pretense. Gatsby embodies the American self-made man myth that the novel both celebrates and indicts. RL.11-12.3 covers complex character development. AP Lit treats Gatsby as canonical for character analysis. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on identity and the American Dream use this novel.",
