      "choices": [
        "Refutation",
        "Assertive",
        "Indignant",
        "Satirical"
      ],
      "correctText": "Indignant",
      "topic": "Tone Vocabulary",
      "explanation": "Indignant tone conveys righteous anger at wrongdoing. Frederick Douglass's 'What to the Slave Is the Fourth of July?' (1852) reaches indignant peaks: 'This Fourth of July is yours, not mine. You may rejoice, I must mourn.' RI.11-12.4 examines word choice and tone. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice tests precise tone naming. AP Lang argument and rhetorical analysis essays demand distinguishing indignant (righteous anger) from angry (unspecified rage) or bitter (resentful). Precise tone vocabulary unlocks rhetorical analysis.",
