      "correctText": "Triangle",
      "topic": "Argument and Rhetoric",
      "explanation": "The rhetorical triangle is Aristotle's framework (Rhetoric, c. 4th century BCE) of speaker/writer, audience, and subject - the three corners of every rhetorical situation, with the three Aristotelian appeals corresponding to each corner: ethos (speaker, character/credibility), pathos (audience, emotion), logos (subject, logic/evidence). All rhetorical analysis builds on the triangle. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers analyzing how authors use rhetoric; the AP English Language and Composition curriculum framework rests on the rhetorical triangle. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) implicitly grades students' ability to analyze how speakers balance the three appeals to fit their specific speaker-audience-subject configuration - upper-range essays integrate all three corners.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
