      "correctText": "Warrant",
      "topic": "Argument Structure",
      "explanation": "Stephen Toulmin's Uses of Argument (1958) names the warrant as the underlying reasoning, often implicit, that connects evidence (grounds) to claim. Example: claim 'Marie should not run for office'; grounds 'she was indicted for fraud in 2019'; warrant 'people indicted for serious financial crimes should not hold public office.' The warrant typically embodies a value, definition, or causal principle. NYS RI.11-12.8 covers evaluating the reasoning in arguments. AP Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis) rewards students who surface implicit warrants - rubric upper-range awards go to essays that identify the unstated reasoning bridging evidence to claim, not just summarize the surface argument.",
