      "correctText": "Persuade",
      "topic": "Author's Purpose",
      "explanation": "A writer arguing for policy change - say, to abolish standardized testing, or to ratify a treaty, or to raise the minimum wage - has primarily the purpose to persuade: to change the audience's belief or move them to action. Distinct from inform (convey factual material neutrally), entertain (amuse), and express (render personal experience). NYS W.11-12.1 covers argument writing whose primary purpose is persuasion. AP English Lang FRQ Question 3 (argument, 6 points, 40 minutes) requires students to write persuasively in response to a policy or value prompt - rubric distinguishes argument (claim + reasoning + evidence) from mere assertion or summary. Effective persuasion integrates logos, pathos, and ethos.",
