      "prompt": "Writing meant to expose problems, like muckraking journalism, has this stance.",
      "choices": [
        "Subjective",
        "Critical",
        "False dilemma",
        "Explicit"
      ],
      "correctText": "Critical",
      "topic": "Author's Stance",
      "explanation": "A critical stance is one that exposes problems, injustices, or failures - the stance of muckraking journalism, investigative reporting, and reformist literature. Upton Sinclair's The Jungle (1906, meatpacking industry abuses), Ida B. Wells's Southern Horrors (1892, lynching), Rachel Carson's Silent Spring (1962, pesticide harms), and Michelle Alexander's The New Jim Crow (2010, mass incarceration) adopt critical stance. Distinct from objective (apparent neutrality) and celebratory (praising). NYS RI.11-12.6 covers analyzing author's point of view and purpose. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards students who name critical stance and analyze its rhetorical strategies (vivid evidence, urgent tone, moral framing).",
