      "correctText": "Soliloquy",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Soliloquy is a dramatic convention in which a character alone on stage speaks their thoughts aloud, granting the audience access to interiority unavailable to other characters. Shakespeare gives Hamlet seven soliloquies - 'O, that this too too solid flesh would melt' (1.2), 'O, what a rogue and peasant slave am I' (2.2), 'To be, or not to be' (3.1), 'How all occasions do inform against me' (4.4) - which together constitute the most sustained Renaissance dramatization of mind. NYS RL.11-12.3 covers complex character development; RL.11-12.6 covers point of view. AP Lit Question 1 (poetry analysis) often tests soliloquy as a poetic form; rubric rewards attention to meter (iambic pentameter), syntax, and self-address.",
