      "correctText": "Modern tragic hero",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Arthur Miller's Death of a Salesman (1949) creates a distinctly modern tragic hero in Willy Loman, the failing 63-year-old traveling salesman. Miller's essay 'Tragedy and the Common Man' (Feb 1949 New York Times) explicitly argued that the everyman, not just kings and princes, can achieve tragic stature through 'total compulsion to evaluate himself justly.' Willy's expressionist flashbacks to brother Ben, the Boston hotel room and Biff's discovery, and the planted carrot seeds dramatize hamartia (the fatal flaw of self-deception) and hubris (chasing the wrong American Dream). NYS RL.11-12.3 covers tragic character; AP Lit FRQ Question 3 routinely uses Salesman as evidence for tragedy of the common person.",
