      "correctText": "Dynamic character",
      "topic": "Characterization",
      "explanation": "Dynamic characters change significantly through narrative pressure, contrasted with static (unchanging) characters. Orwell's Winston Smith transforms from secret diarist and rebel ('DOWN WITH BIG BROTHER' inscribed in 1984) through love affair with Julia, betrayal by O'Brien, torture in the Ministry of Love and the cage of rats in Room 101, to broken loyalist who 'loved Big Brother' in the gin-soaked Chestnut Tree Cafe finale. NYS RL.11-12.3 expects analysis of how complex characters develop and interact. AP Lit's open question (Question 3) prizes essays that trace character arc as vehicle for thematic argument - here, that totalitarianism can destroy not only behavior but consciousness itself.",
