      "explanation": "A counterclaim is the opposing view the writer must fairly acknowledge in argumentative writing. NYS W.11-12.1 explicitly requires students to 'develop claim(s) and counterclaim(s) fairly and thoroughly,' supplying evidence for both. The technique strengthens the writer's ethos (the audience trusts a writer who has considered objections), strengthens the argument (anticipating rebuttals neutralizes them), and demonstrates intellectual honesty. Distinct from the related move of concession (briefly granting a point) and refutation (disproving the opposing view). AP Lang FRQ Question 3 (argument, 6 points) rubric awards upper-range scores only to essays that engage with possible objections, not just one-sided assertion.",
