      "correctText": "Introductory adverbial phrase",
      "topic": "Comma Rules",
      "explanation": "An introductory adverbial phrase (or clause) beginning a sentence requires a comma after it: 'After the war ended, the economy slowly recovered.' 'Although she had practiced for weeks, the audition went poorly.' 'During the long winter of 1944, supply lines failed.' The comma signals the end of the introductory unit and the beginning of the main clause. Distinct from short prepositional phrases ('In 1944 supplies failed' - optional comma) and from inverted sentences where introductory element is essential subject. NYS L.11-12.2 covers punctuation conventions. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) and Question 3 (argument) implicitly reward sentence-structure variety, which often begins with adverbial phrases requiring commas.",
