      "correctText": "Counterargument structure",
      "topic": "Counterclaim and Concession",
      "explanation": "Counterargument structure addresses 'they say' before 'I say' in argumentative writing - acknowledging the opposing view fairly before contesting it. The 'they say / I say' framework (Gerald Graff and Cathy Birkenstein, They Say / I Say 2006) systematizes this move into a writing template. NYS W.11-12.1 explicitly requires students to 'develop claim(s) and counterclaim(s) fairly and thoroughly, supplying the most relevant evidence for each while pointing out the strengths and limitations of both in a manner that anticipates the audience's knowledge level, concerns, values, and possible biases.' AP English Lang FRQ Question 3 (argument, 6 points) rubric awards upper-range scores to essays engaging opposing views - mere assertion of position without engagement scores lower.",
