      "correctText": "Straw man",
      "topic": "Counterclaim and Concession",
      "explanation": "Straw man is the fallacy of misrepresenting an opponent's argument to make it easier to refute - constructing a weaker version of the opposing view rather than engaging its strongest form. Example: opponents argue for stricter gun regulations on specific types of weapons; the straw man response refutes 'banning all guns,' which is not the opponents' actual position. The fallacy works because the audience may not know the opposing argument's actual form. NYS RI.11-12.8 requires evaluating reasoning including fallacy identification; W.11-12.1 requires fair treatment of counterclaims. AP English Lang FRQ Question 3 (argument, 6 points) rubric awards essays that engage opposing views in their strongest form; straw-man treatment loses points for ethos and reasoning.",
