      "correctText": "Strengthened ethos",
      "topic": "Counterclaim and Concession",
      "explanation": "When a writer concedes one point and then asserts an even stronger position, strengthened ethos emerges as the rhetorical effect - the audience trusts a writer who has weighed objections and still defends the position more firmly. Format: 'Critics are right that X has costs, but those costs are smaller than the catastrophic alternative; in fact, X is not just acceptable but necessary because...' The double move (concede + intensify) demonstrates both intellectual honesty and confidence. NYS W.11-12.1 covers argument with counterclaims. AP English Lang FRQ Question 3 (argument, 6 points) rubric awards upper-range scores (5-6) to essays whose concession-then-intensification structure produces a sophisticated argumentative voice.",
