      "correctText": "In medias res",
      "topic": "Explanatory Style",
      "explanation": "In medias res (Latin: 'into the middle of things') is the narrative technique of beginning a narrative in the middle of action, with background and exposition supplied later through flashback, dialogue, or summary. The technique goes back to Homer's Iliad and Odyssey (8th c. BCE), where the wandering of Odysseus is recounted retrospectively. Modern thrillers and short fiction routinely open in medias res to seize attention. Distinct from chronological opening (begins at beginning) and from ab ovo (Latin: 'from the egg,' opening from origins). NYS RL.11-12.5 covers structural choices including ordering of events. AP English Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) rewards in medias res identification as authorial structural choice.",
