      "correctText": "Synecdoche",
      "topic": "Figurative Language",
      "explanation": "Synecdoche (Greek: 'taking together') is a figure of speech where a part stands for the whole, or the whole stands for a part. Calling a king 'the crown' (part - the symbol of office - representing the whole monarch), referring to workers as 'hands' (part - the body part doing the work - for whole laborers), calling cars 'wheels,' or naming a country 'the flag.' Distinct from metonymy (substitution by associated thing, not part-whole). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers figurative meaning. AP English Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry, 6 points) and Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) test synecdoche - rubric distinguishes naming the device from analyzing its effect (compression, emphasis on the relevant part, dehumanization in 'hands' for workers).",
