      "correctText": "Personification",
      "topic": "Figurative Language",
      "explanation": "Personification gives human qualities to non-human entities. Chaucer's General Prologue opens with April's 'shoures soote' (sweet showers) personified as wakening the dormant earth, the West Wind's breath inspiring 'every holt and heeth' to bud, and Zephyrus animating spring. The pathetic fallacy (John Ruskin, Modern Painters III, 1856) is the related practice of attributing human feelings to nature (the 'pathetic' here means feeling-laden, not pitiable). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers figurative language. AP Lit Question 1 (poetry analysis) rewards identification of personification and analysis of its effect (animating abstract concepts, intensifying setting, voicing emotional projection).",
