      "topic": "Figurative Language",
      "explanation": "Oxymoron (Greek: 'pointedly foolish,' itself oxymoronic) combines contradictory terms into a deliberate, compressed paradox. Classic examples: 'jumbo shrimp,' 'deafening silence,' 'open secret,' 'sweet sorrow,' 'living death,' 'bittersweet.' Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet (1597) is saturated with oxymora: 'O brawling love! O loving hate!,' 'cold fire, sick health, still-waking sleep' (1.1). Distinct from paradox (apparent contradiction revealing deeper truth, often longer) and from antithesis (contrast in parallel structure). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers figurative language. AP English Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry, 6 points) tests oxymoron - rubric upper-range awards specific effect-analysis (emotional complexity, pointed irony, compressed paradox).",
