      "correctText": "Symbolic motif",
      "topic": "Figurative Language",
      "explanation": "Conrad threads 'darkness' through Heart of Darkness as a recurring symbolic motif - literal jungle darkness of the upriver journey, moral darkness of King Leopold's Congo Free State, cognitive darkness of Marlow's incomplete understanding, and metaphysical darkness inside any human capable of Kurtz's degeneration. The motif begins on the Thames (the unnamed frame narrator's 'this also has been one of the dark places of the earth'), descends through the Outer/Central/Inner Stations, and culminates in Kurtz's 'The horror! The horror!' deathbed cry. NYS RL.11-12.4 covers figurative language. AP Lit FRQ Question 2 tests Conrad's symbolic compression - the same word doing literal, moral, and metaphysical work across the novella.",
