      "explanation": "Hosseini pairs Amir (Pashtun, privileged, only son of Baba the wealthy carpet merchant) with Hassan (Hazara, Shiite, son of Ali the family servant - later revealed as Baba's illegitimate son and Amir's half-brother). Hassan's unwavering loyalty - 'For you, a thousand times over' - foils Amir's privilege and the moral cowardice he displays in the alley behind the 1975 tournament. Pashtun/Hazara ethnic hierarchy (Sunni vs Shia, racial difference, historical Hazara persecution) structures the foil. Redemption requires Amir's 2001 return to Taliban-ruled Kabul to rescue Hassan's son Sohrab from Assef. NYS RL.11-12.3 covers foil-and-theme; AP Lit FRQ Question 3 uses Kite Runner as evidence for ethical foil pairings.",
