      "correctText": "Hamlet",
      "topic": "Foil Characters",
      "explanation": "Shakespeare gives Hamlet three deliberate foils that triangulate his hesitation. Fortinbras (Norwegian prince whose father Old Hamlet killed) raises an army and marches twenty thousand men to fight for a worthless strip of Polish ground - decisive action 'for a fantasy and trick of fame.' Laertes (Polonius's son) returns from Paris and immediately demands revenge for his father - 'To hell, allegiance! Vows, to the blackest devil!' Horatio (philosophical Stoic) shows loyalty without revenge-urge. The three foils spotlight Hamlet's intellectual delay - his soliloquy 'How all occasions do inform against me' (4.4) responds to seeing Fortinbras's army. NYS RL.11-12.3 covers foil; AP Lit FRQ Question 3 rewards triple-foil analysis.",
