      "explanation": "Miller pairs Willy's two sons across the Loman dinner table. Biff (the older brother, former Ebbets Field football star turned drifter, who at age seventeen caught Willy with The Woman in the Boston hotel room and abandoned his University of Virginia plans) confronts the family's salesman myths. Happy (the younger, womanizing, perpetually optimistic department-store assistant) replicates Willy's self-deceptions. Biff's Act 2 'We never told the truth for ten minutes in this house!' breaks the foil into thematic statement; Happy's 'I'm gonna show you and everybody else that Willy Loman did not die in vain' at the Requiem confirms his blindness. NYS RL.11-12.3 covers foil pairings; AP Lit Question 3 prizes Biff/Happy contrast.",
