      "correctText": "Foreshadowing",
      "topic": "Hook Strategies",
      "explanation": "Foreshadowing is the narrative technique of hinting at future events through suggestion - imagery, dialogue, symbolism, structural placement - without revealing them outright. The hint registers consciously or subliminally with the reader, creating anticipation and (later) recognition of the prepared significance. Shakespeare's witches at the opening of Macbeth (1.1) foreshadow the moral inversion - 'Fair is foul, and foul is fair.' Distinct from prophecy (explicit prediction) and from flashback (return to prior events). NYS RL.11-12.5 covers structural choices including foreshadowing; RL.11-12.3 covers character and plot development. AP English Lit FRQ Question 3 (open, 8 points) frequently rewards analysis of foreshadowing as structural choice.",
