      "topic": "Latin and Greek Roots",
      "explanation": "The Greek root 'demos' meaning 'people' generates an English political vocabulary: democracy (rule by the people), democrat (one favoring democracy), demographics (population-statistics), epidemic (upon-the-people - disease), pandemic (across-all-people), and demagogue - originally 'leader of the people' but now meaning a populist orator who appeals to prejudices and emotions to gain power, often through inflammatory rhetoric. The negative connotation traces to Greek political theory: Plato and Aristotle warned that democracies were vulnerable to demagogues who exploited the people's emotions. NYS L.11-12.4 covers using Greek roots as clues to meaning. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) often rewards demagogue-versus-statesman distinctions in political rhetoric analysis.",
