      "explanation": "The Latin root 'bene' meaning 'good' or 'well' generates a productive English word family: benefit (good outcome), beneficiary (one receiving good), beneficent (doing good), benevolent (good-willed), benediction (good-saying, blessing), benign (kindly), and benefactor - one who does good for others, especially through donations or patronage. The opposite root 'mal' (bad) generates malevolent, malign, malefactor, malicious. NYS L.11-12.4 explicitly covers 'use common, grade-appropriate Greek or Latin affixes and roots as clues to the meaning of a word.' The College Board's SAT Reading section, AP Lang and Lit vocabulary, and NYS Regents ELA passages all reward Greek/Latin root knowledge - students can decode unfamiliar words by combining known roots, prefixes, suffixes.",
