      "explanation": "Resolution (also called denouement, from the French 'untying') restores order and closes thematic threads after the climax's catastrophe. Fortinbras's arrival in Act 5 Scene 2, his command 'Take up the bodies,' and his bid for the Danish crown ('I have some rights of memory in this kingdom') accomplish political resolution: the Norwegian prince whose father Old Hamlet killed now inherits Denmark, completing a generational reversal. Horatio survives to 'tell my story.' NYS RL.11-12.5 covers structural closure. Aristotelian catharsis - purgation of pity and fear - is the audience's resolution, completing the tragic experience the Poetics defines.",
