      "topic": "Point of View",
      "explanation": "Free indirect discourse (FID, also called free indirect speech, free indirect style) merges third-person narrative voice with a character's interior voice, without the quotation marks of direct speech or the 'she thought' tags of indirect discourse. Austen pioneered FID in English: 'It was such a happy escape!' (Pride and Prejudice) renders Elizabeth Bennet's relieved thoughts in the narrator's grammatical voice. Flaubert (Madame Bovary 1856), Joyce, and Virginia Woolf extended the technique into modernist consciousness. NYS RL.11-12.6 covers POV; RL.11-12.4 covers voice. AP Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) tests students' ability to identify FID and analyze its effect (irony, intimacy, judgment-suspension).",
