      "explanation": "Conrad uses frame narrative: an unnamed sailor aboard the cruising yawl Nellie at anchor on the Thames reports Marlow's oral narration of his Congo journey. The opening ('The Nellie, a cruising yawl, swung to her anchor without a flutter of the sails, and was at rest') is the frame narrator; Marlow takes over in paragraph six ('And this also has been one of the dark places of the earth') and continues for most of the novella, with the frame narrator interrupting briefly at moments of stress. The framing creates multiple narrative distances from Kurtz, who himself remains largely unseen (his pamphlet, his shrunken-head fence, his deathbed cry). NYS RL.11-12.6 covers POV; RL.11-12.5 covers structure. Mary Shelley's Frankenstein (1818) uses Walton/Victor/creature triple-nested frames.",
