      "explanation": "Orwell uses third-person limited narration focalized through Winston Smith. The reader sees Oceania exclusively through Winston's perceptions and accesses his thoughts ('He thought he had been beaten') but never other characters' interiors - we infer Julia's, O'Brien's, and Charrington's motives only through Winston's interpretations. The technique creates intimacy with the rebel consciousness while preserving the narrative authority that exposes Winston's self-deceptions (his trust of O'Brien, his sentimental projection onto the proles). Genette (Narrative Discourse 1972) calls this 'internal focalization.' NYS RL.11-12.6 covers POV; AP Lit Question 2 (prose passage analysis) routinely tests focalization and the gap between what a focalizer sees and what the reader infers.",
