      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "Chiasmus (Greek 'chi-shape,' after the letter X) is the reversal of grammatical structure in successive phrases. John F. Kennedy's Inaugural Address (Jan 20 1961) deploys chiasmus famously: 'Ask not what your country can do for you - ask what you can do for your country.' The structure ABBA reverses 'country/you' to 'you/country.' Other classic examples: 'When the going gets tough, the tough get going,' 'Beautiful things have ugly aspects; ugly things have beautiful aspects.' NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis; RL.11-12.4 covers diction. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) tests chiasmus - rubric distinguishes naming the device from analyzing its memorable, balanced, often paradoxical effect.",
