      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "Anaphora is the deliberate repetition of a word or phrase at the start of successive clauses, sentences, or lines for emphasis, rhythm, and accumulating force. Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I Have a Dream' speech (Aug 28 1963, March on Washington) repeats 'I have a dream' across seven successive sentences and 'Let freedom ring' across nine. Winston Churchill's 'We shall fight on the beaches' (June 4 1940) builds anaphoric momentum: 'we shall fight on the seas... we shall fight on the beaches... we shall fight on the landing grounds... we shall never surrender.' NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards anaphora identification AND effect-analysis (cumulative force, rhythmic insistence, hypnotic intensity).",
