      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "Tricolon is the rhetorical pattern of three parallel words, phrases, or clauses, exploiting the audience's pattern-recognition preference for triplets. Abraham Lincoln's Gettysburg Address (Nov 19 1863) closes with the tricolon 'government of the people, by the people, for the people, shall not perish from the earth.' Caesar's 'Veni, vidi, vici' ('I came, I saw, I conquered'), Churchill's 'Blood, sweat, and tears,' and Obama's 'Yes, we can' applied to varied contexts all exploit tricolon. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards tricolon identification AND analysis of its effect (climax, completeness, balance, memorability).",
