      "correctText": "Triangle",
      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "The rhetorical triangle (Aristotle, Rhetoric c. 4th century BCE) is the triangular relationship of speaker/writer, audience, and subject - the three corners of every rhetorical situation. At each corner sit the three Aristotelian appeals: ethos (corresponding to the speaker, character/credibility), pathos (audience, emotion), logos (subject, logic/evidence). NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Language and Composition framework rests on the rhetorical triangle - FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) implicitly grades students' ability to analyze how authors balance the three appeals to fit their specific speaker-audience-subject configuration. Upper-range essays integrate all three corners; middle-range essays handle them serially.",
