      "correctText": "Exigence",
      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "Exigence (from Lloyd Bitzer's 1968 article 'The Rhetorical Situation') names the urgent issue or event that prompts rhetoric - the imperfection, defect, or obstacle marked by urgency that calls forth response. The Pearl Harbor attack (Dec 7 1941) was the exigence for FDR's 'date which will live in infamy' speech; the Watergate break-in (June 1972) was the exigence for the impeachment investigations; the Sept 11 2001 attacks were the exigence for the post-9/11 rhetoric. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards students who name exigence AND show how it shapes the rhetorical choices - rubric upper-range awards integration of situation and strategy.",
