      "topic": "Sensory Detail",
      "explanation": "Scene expansion is the deliberate technique of slowing narrative time to emphasize a moment - dwelling on a single instant for paragraphs while other narrative stretches compress days into sentences. Time-rate manipulation (Genette, Narrative Discourse 1972) is foundational to pacing: scene (real-time dramatization), summary (compressed), ellipsis (skipped time), pause (description). Expanded scenes signal significance - the climax of Heart of Darkness when Marlow witnesses Kurtz's 'The horror! The horror!' expands into pages of associated reflection. NYS W.11-12.3 covers narrative pacing; AP English Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) rewards scene-expansion analysis as authorial pacing choice.",
