      "topic": "Sensory Detail",
      "explanation": "'Show, don't tell' is the foundational craft principle of preferring dramatized action over labeled emotion - demonstrating anger through clenched fists, sharp dialogue, slammed doors, rather than stating 'she was angry.' The principle prioritizes the reader's inference over the writer's declaration, creating immediacy and respecting the reader's intelligence. Henry James's Notebooks (1890s) advocated dramatized scene over summary; modern creative-writing pedagogy (Burroway's Writing Fiction 1982 onward) systematized the principle. NYS W.11-12.3 covers narrative techniques including precise details. AP English Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) rewards essays that recognize dramatization as authorial choice and analyze its specific effects.",
