      "explanation": "A sensory hook opens with a striking image, sound, sensation, or detail that drops readers into a scene before any exposition. Example: 'The rain began at 4:17 AM, hitting the tin roof in irregular bursts that scattered her dreams.' Distinct from generalized opening ('On a rainy morning...'), the sensory hook commits to specific moment. The technique is foundational to in medias res openings (Latin: 'in the middle of things'). NYS W.11-12.3 covers narrative openings and sensory language. AP English Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) and AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) reward analysis of opening strategies - sensory hooks are common upper-range openings.",
