      "explanation": "Sensory details engage the five senses - sight, sound, smell, taste, touch - to immerse readers in scene and experience. Effective sensory description distributes across multiple senses rather than relying solely on the visual; the standard advice is to layer at least three senses per scene. The five senses go back to Aristotle's De Anima (c. 350 BCE), with modern psychology adding proprioception (body position) and equilibrioception (balance) as additional channels. NYS W.11-12.3 explicitly requires 'precise words and phrases, telling details, and sensory language to convey a vivid picture of the experiences, events, setting, and/or characters.' AP English Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry, 6 points) and Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) reward sensory-detail analysis.",
