      "explanation": "Jane Austen's Pride and Prejudice (1813) places its courtship plot in Regency-era England (1811-1820), the social geography of country estates encoding class and marriage economics: the Bennets at Longbourn (entailed away to Collins), the Bingleys at Netherfield (rented), Darcy's grand Pemberley in Derbyshire (10,000 pounds a year), and Lady Catherine de Bourgh's Rosings (status without warmth). Estate size determines marital eligibility - Mrs. Bennet's project is to convert daughters into estate-secured wives before Mr. Bennet dies. NYS RL.11-12.3 expects setting-as-theme analysis. AP Lit treats Austen's free indirect discourse and ironic narration as canonical for Question 2 prose passages.",
