      "topic": "Sonnet and Verse Form",
      "explanation": "Geoffrey Chaucer's The Canterbury Tales (c. 1387-1400) uses iambic pentameter rhyming couplets (aa bb cc dd...), a form sometimes called heroic couplets in its later Augustan refinement. Chaucer's General Prologue ('Whan that Aprille with his shoures soote') and most of the twenty-four tales employ the form, which became foundational for English versification - Dryden, Pope (The Rape of the Lock 1712, An Essay on Criticism 1711), and Samuel Johnson refined the closed heroic couplet through the 18th century. NYS RL.11-12.5 covers form-meaning. AP Lit FRQ Question 1 rewards students who recognize how couplet structure (closed, end-stopped) enables epigrammatic compression and witty antithesis.",
