      "topic": "Sonnet and Verse Form",
      "explanation": "The Shakespearean (English) sonnet is fourteen lines of iambic pentameter rhyming abab cdcd efef gg, structured as three quatrains developing the argument and a closing couplet delivering the volta (turn) and resolution. Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, adapted the Italian form for English in the 1540s; Shakespeare published the 154-sonnet sequence in 1609 (Sonnets to a Young Man, Sonnets to a Dark Lady). Sonnet 18 ('Shall I compare thee to a summer's day') and Sonnet 130 ('My mistress' eyes are nothing like the sun') are canonical. NYS RL.11-12.5 covers form-meaning relationships. AP Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry analysis, 6 points) routinely tests sonnet form - rubric rewards scansion, volta identification, and form-content integration.",
