      "correctText": "Blank verse",
      "topic": "Sonnet and Verse Form",
      "explanation": "Blank verse is unrhymed iambic pentameter, ten syllables per line in alternating unstressed/stressed pattern. Henry Howard, Earl of Surrey, introduced it to English with his 1557 translation of Virgil's Aeneid Books 2 and 4. Shakespeare made it the standard meter of English verse drama, using it for noble characters and serious matter while reserving prose for servants, comic scenes, and madness (Hamlet's prose with Polonius, Lear's prose in the storm). Milton's Paradise Lost (1667), Wordsworth's The Prelude (1799-1850), and Tennyson's Idylls of the King are extended blank-verse narratives. NYS RL.11-12.5 covers form. AP Lit Question 1 tests scansion of blank verse and identification of metrical departures.",
