      "correctText": "Sestet",
      "topic": "Sonnet and Verse Form",
      "explanation": "The Italian (Petrarchan) sonnet, perfected by Francesco Petrarca (1304-1374) in his Canzoniere addressing Laura, divides into an octave (eight lines, abbaabba) and a sestet (six lines, varied: cdcdcd or cdecde or cdcdee). The volta (turn) typically falls between octave and sestet at line 9, shifting argument, perspective, or feeling. Milton, Wordsworth, Edna St. Vincent Millay, and Robert Frost wrote Petrarchan sonnets; Shakespeare's English variant places the volta later (often at line 13, before the couplet). NYS RL.11-12.5 covers form-meaning. AP Lit FRQ Question 1 frequently asks students to identify volta placement and discuss how it organizes the poem's argument.",
