      "correctText": "Epistrophe",
      "topic": "Speech Analysis",
      "explanation": "Epistrophe (Greek 'turning back') is the rhetorical pattern of repeating a word or phrase at the END of successive clauses, sentences, or lines - the structural mirror of anaphora (repetition at the start). Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I Have a Dream' speech (Aug 28 1963) deploys epistrophe famously in the 'Let freedom ring' sequence: 'Let freedom ring from the prodigious hilltops of New Hampshire... Let freedom ring from the mighty mountains of New York... Let freedom ring from the heightening Alleghenies of Pennsylvania' - the repeated locations precede the constant 'Let freedom ring' phrase as the speech accumulates. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards epistrophe identification.",
