      "explanation": "Martin Luther King Jr.'s 'I Have a Dream' speech (delivered Aug 28 1963 at the March on Washington for Jobs and Freedom, to 250,000+ on the National Mall) has the primary rhetorical purpose to motivate to action - to move the audience beyond passive sympathy with civil rights into active commitment, while sustaining nonviolent discipline. Distinct from inform (conveying facts) and entertain. King uses anaphora ('I have a dream,' 'Let freedom ring'), allusion (Declaration of Independence, Lincoln's Emancipation Proclamation, the prophet Amos, Isaiah), and tricolon to build emotional and moral momentum. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) often uses MLK speeches as primary text.",
