      "correctText": "Constraints",
      "topic": "Speech Analysis",
      "explanation": "Constraints in rhetorical analysis are the limits on what a speaker can say or do, given the specific moment - audience expectations, time, occasion, prior public statements, the speaker's institutional role. Lloyd Bitzer (The Rhetorical Situation, 1968) names constraints alongside exigence and audience as the three components of any rhetorical situation. A presidential inaugural address constrains: the speaker must be the new president, the audience is national, the genre is ceremonial, the tone is dignified. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards students who identify constraints and analyze how the speaker works within and against them.",
