      "topic": "Speech Analysis",
      "explanation": "The audience in rhetorical analysis is the people whose actions, beliefs, or attitudes the speaker hopes to influence. Distinct from the bystanders, the immediate physical listeners, and the secondary audience (those overhearing). Aristotle's Rhetoric (c. 4th century BCE) distinguished three audiences corresponding to three rhetorical genres: deliberative (legislators), forensic (jurors), epideictic (ceremonial assembly). Modern rhetoric recognizes that audience analysis is foundational - speeches that misjudge audience fail regardless of inherent quality. NYS RI.11-12.6 covers rhetorical analysis. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) rewards students who specify audience precisely (not generic 'the reader') and analyze how rhetorical choices fit that audience.",
