      "explanation": "Narrative perspective is the voice and consciousness through which a narrative is told - first-person (Amir in The Kite Runner), third-person limited (Winston in 1984), third-person omniscient (the narrator of Austen's Pride and Prejudice who can enter every character's mind), or rarer modes (second-person, multiple first-person, stream of consciousness in Faulkner's The Sound and the Fury). Distinct from point of view (a broader term sometimes synonymous, sometimes distinguishing positional from voice-based choices) and focalization (Genette's term for whose perception shapes the narration). NYS RL.11-12.6 covers point of view. AP English Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points) rewards perspective analysis.",
