      "correctText": "Performance under duress",
      "topic": "Symbolism",
      "explanation": "Henrik Ibsen's A Doll's House (1879) deploys Nora's tarantella as the play's central performative symbol. Nora practices the southern Italian dance (named for spider bites the dancing was thought to cure) to perform at the Stenborgs' costume party, her dance becoming more frantic as Krogstad's threat to expose her forgery mounts. The tarantella symbolizes the strained performance of wifely obedience that masks her hidden self - sweet 'skylark' and 'squirrel' for Torvald, secret breadwinner who illegally borrowed to save his life. The play's famous final door-slam, when Nora leaves Torvald and her three children, became the founding moment of modern theatrical realism. NYS RL.11-12.4 covers symbol; RL.11-12.5 covers structural climax.",
