      "explanation": "Joseph Conrad's Heart of Darkness, serialized in Blackwood's Magazine in 1899 and published as a book in 1902, is the canonical modernist novella critiquing European imperialism. Charles Marlow narrates his Congo journey aboard a steamboat to find the ivory agent Kurtz at the Inner Station, encountering brutal exploitation of King Leopold II's Congo Free State (where 8-10 million Africans died c. 1885-1908). The novella uses frame narrative (unnamed sailor reporting Marlow on the Thames), the symbolic upriver journey, and Kurtz's deathbed cry 'The horror! The horror!' Achebe's 1975 critique 'An Image of Africa' complicates the canon. AP English Lit FRQ Question 3 (open, 8 points) regularly accepts Heart of Darkness.",
