      "explanation": "A motif is a recurring image, idea, or symbol that develops thematic meaning across a work through repeated occurrence in different contexts. Distinct from theme (the abstract idea) and symbol (a single object carrying meaning). Conrad threads 'darkness' through Heart of Darkness as a motif - literal jungle darkness, moral darkness of empire, cognitive darkness of Marlow's incomplete understanding, metaphysical darkness inside any human capable of Kurtz's degeneration. Other motifs: Hamlet's images of disease and rotting ('something is rotten in the state of Denmark'); 1984's recurring images of surveillance. NYS RL.11-12.2 covers theme and motif. AP English Lit FRQ Question 3 (open, 8 points) rewards motif tracking across a work.",
