      "correctText": "Dramatic irony",
      "topic": "Texts and Authors",
      "explanation": "Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something a character does not - producing tension, anticipation, and often tragic effect as the character acts in ignorance. Oedipus Rex (Sophocles, c. 429 BCE) is the canonical dramatic-irony tragedy: the audience knows from the prologue that Oedipus killed his father and married his mother; Oedipus discovers it through investigation. In Othello, the audience knows Iago's villainy while Othello does not. Distinct from verbal irony (speaker means opposite) and situational irony (events contradict expectation). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers irony. AP English Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry, 6 points), Question 2 (prose passage, 6 points), and Question 3 (open, 8 points) all reward dramatic-irony analysis.",
