      "explanation": "Elegiac describes a tone of mournful contemplation, especially of loss, often grief-saturated and meditative rather than acute. The elegy (Old English elegy 'wisdom of mourning') is the corresponding poetic genre - John Milton's Lycidas (1638) mourning Edward King, Thomas Gray's Elegy Written in a Country Churchyard (1751), Walt Whitman's When Lilacs Last in the Dooryard Bloom'd (1865) mourning Lincoln, W.H. Auden's In Memory of W.B. Yeats (1939). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers tone; RL.11-12.5 covers form. AP Lit FRQ Question 1 (poetry analysis, 6 points) routinely tests elegiac tone - rubric rewards specific tonal vocabulary over generic 'sad' or 'mournful.'",
