      "explanation": "Sardonic combines scorn with bitter humor - a tone that mocks while wounded, that laughs through clenched teeth. Distinct from sarcastic (verbally ironic but lighter) and from cynical (without humor). Jonathan Swift's A Modest Proposal (1729) suggesting Irish poor sell babies as food is canonically sardonic - mockery sharpened by genuine moral indignation at English colonial policy. NYS RI.11-12.4 covers determining meaning including tone; RL.11-12.4 covers tonal analysis in literature. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) and AP Lit FRQ Question 2 (prose passage) reward precise tone diagnosis - rubric distinguishes 'sardonic' from generic 'angry' or 'unhappy.' Tone-vocabulary precision separates upper-range essays from middle-range.",
