      "explanation": "Tentative describes a writer's tone of uncertainty, ambiguity, and provisional commitment - claims hedged with 'perhaps,' 'it may be that,' 'one possible reading,' 'though we cannot be certain.' Distinct from confident (sure) and from evasive (refusing to commit out of caution). Tentative tone signals intellectual honesty, openness to revision, and respect for the text's complexity. NYS RL.11-12.4 covers tone; W.11-12.1 covers argument including modal qualifiers. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis) and AP Lit FRQ Question 2 reward students who diagnose tentative tone in passages where the author is genuinely working through difficulty - rubric distinguishes genuine tentativeness from indecision or weakness.",
