      "explanation": "Satirical describes a tone that uses irony, exaggeration, and ridicule to expose folly, vice, or stupidity, often with a corrective intent. Jonathan Swift's A Modest Proposal (1729), Voltaire's Candide (1759), George Orwell's Animal Farm (1945), and Stanley Kubrick's Dr. Strangelove (1964) are touchstones. Satire differs from comedy (which aims primarily to amuse) and parody (which mimics specific style for effect). NYS RL.11-12.4 covers tone; RL.11-12.6 covers point of view. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis) often presents satirical passages - rubric rewards students who name satire AND identify its specific target and corrective purpose. Generic 'humorous' scores lower than specific 'satirical exposure of X.'",
