      "correctText": "Lexical chain",
      "topic": "Transitions and Cohesion",
      "explanation": "A lexical chain (M.A.K. Halliday and Ruqaiya Hasan, Cohesion in English 1976) is the repetition of key terms across paragraphs to build connection and reinforce thematic continuity. Distinct from simple word repetition (which may be redundant) and from synonyms-only chains (which lose specific resonance). Effective lexical chains repeat the central concept's name (e.g., 'totalitarianism,' 'surveillance,' 'consciousness' in a 1984 essay) at strategic points. NYS W.11-12.1 covers cohesion devices. AP English Lang FRQ Question 2 (rhetorical analysis, 6 points) and Question 3 (argument) rubric upper-range scores reward demonstrated cohesion - lexical chains are one mechanism by which essays achieve it.",
