      "correctText": "Word choice",
      "topic": "Author's Word Choice",
      "explanation": "Word choice (diction) is an author's deliberate selection of specific words to create tone, mood, or meaning in informational text. A Grade 7 article about wildfires might use 'devastated' versus 'damaged' to amplify emotional weight, or 'researchers' versus 'scientists' to convey authority. NYS Grade 7 ELA standard RI.7.4 explicitly tests word-choice analysis; the state's 2-point short-answer rubric awards full credit when students quote a specific word, describe its emotional or rhetorical weight, and explain how it shapes the reader's interpretation of the topic or the author's argument.",
