      "explanation": "Counterclaim signals are transition phrases that introduce opposing views, such as 'critics argue,' 'some readers may say,' 'on the other hand,' 'however,' and 'while it is true that.' These transitions cue readers that the author is about to acknowledge an opposing view, often to refute it. NYS Grade 7 ELA standard W.7.1.c explicitly tests cohesive transitions in argumentative writing; the state's 4-point rubric awards points for clear use of counterclaim and rebuttal signal phrases that guide readers through the rhetorical move from acknowledgment to refutation.",
