      "correctText": "Counterclaim",
      "topic": "Counterclaim",
      "explanation": "A counterclaim is an argument that opposes the author's main claim, acknowledged so it can be refuted. Strong Grade 7 argumentative writing acknowledges counterclaims to demonstrate fairness and strengthen the main argument. If the claim is 'middle schools should start later,' a counterclaim is 'later start times create child-care problems for working families.' NYS Grade 7 ELA standard W.7.1 explicitly requires counterclaim acknowledgment in argumentative writing; the state's 4-point rubric awards full credit when students name the counterclaim, refute it with evidence, and return to the main argument.",
