      "correctText": "Idiom",
      "topic": "Figurative Language",
      "explanation": "An idiom is a culturally specific expression whose figurative meaning differs from its literal words—'spill the beans,' 'kick the bucket,' 'cost an arm and a leg.' In S.E. Hinton's The Outsiders, the 1960s idiom 'tough as nails' describes greaser attitudes; Sharon Creech uses idioms throughout Walk Two Moons to characterize Sal's grandparents. NYS Grade 7 ELA standards RL.7.4 and L.7.5 both test idiom interpretation; the state's 2-point rubric requires students to give both the literal and figurative meanings, then explain how context supports the intended figurative reading.",
