      "correctText": "Exposition",
      "topic": "Plot Structure",
      "explanation": "Exposition delivers the foundational who, where, and when of a story, typically in opening chapters. In Jerry Spinelli's Crash (1996), the exposition introduces seventh-grader John 'Crash' Coogan's family, his Pennsylvania suburb, and his football-fueled bullying of Penn Webb. In R.J. Palacio's Wonder (2012), Auggie's opening lines establish his facial difference and his first day at Beecher Prep. NYS Grade 7 ELA standard RL.7.3 tests exposition through inference items; the state rubric rewards students who use opening details to predict later character behavior with at least two pieces of evidence.",
