      "correctText": "Rhyme",
      "topic": "Poetic Devices",
      "explanation": "Rhyme is the repetition of matching final sounds in two or more words, especially at line ends; rhyme scheme is the pattern, labeled with letters (ABAB, AABB). Alfred Noyes uses a strict rhyme scheme in 'The Highwayman' to create gallop-like momentum. Edgar Allan Poe uses musical rhyme in 'Annabel Lee' with the haunting refrain of 'sea' and 'Lee.' NYS Grade 7 ELA standard RL.7.5 tests rhyme through short-answer items; the state rubric awards full credit for accurate rhyme-scheme labeling and explanation of the emotional or musical effect produced.",
