      "correctText": "Onomatopoeia",
      "topic": "Poetic Devices",
      "explanation": "Onomatopoeia uses words whose sounds suggest their meanings—'buzz,' 'crash,' 'whisper,' 'thud.' Alfred Noyes uses onomatopoeia in 'The Highwayman' through 'tlot-tlot, tlot-tlot' for the horse's hoofbeats. Kwame Alexander uses onomatopoeic basketball sounds throughout The Crossover (2014) for net-swishes and dribbling. NYS Grade 7 ELA standard RL.7.4 tests onomatopoeia recognition; the state's 2-point rubric awards students who identify the onomatopoeic word and explain how the sound effect contributes to mood, characterization, or rhythm in the passage.",
