      "prompt": "A narrator whose account readers cannot fully trust.",
      "choices": [
        "Point of view",
        "Third-person limited",
        "Dynamic character",
        "Unreliable narrator"
      ],
      "correctText": "Unreliable narrator",
      "topic": "Point of View",
      "explanation": "An unreliable narrator's account is distorted by limited knowledge, bias, denial, or self-deception, forcing readers to interpret events against the narration. Crash Coogan in the opening chapters of Jerry Spinelli's Crash is partly unreliable—his bullying behavior toward Penn Webb is presented through self-justifying narration that readers must see past. Edgar Allan Poe's 'The Tell-Tale Heart' is the classic unreliable-narrator text. NYS Grade 7 ELA standard RL.7.6 tests this concept through inference items; the rubric requires students to cite contradictions between the narrator's claims and observable evidence.",
