      "prompt": "Marking the omission of words in a quotation.",
      "choices": [
        "Period",
        "Ellipsis",
        "Paraphrase",
        "Dash"
      ],
      "correctText": "Ellipsis",
      "topic": "Punctuation + Mechanics",
      "explanation": "An ellipsis (three dots: ...) marks an omission of words from a quotation, a trailing-off pause, or an unfinished thought. When quoting from sources, ellipses indicate the writer has shortened the quotation while preserving its meaning: 'In spite of everything … I still believe that people are really good at heart.' NYS Grade 8 ELA standard L.8.2.A specifically mentions 'comma, ellipsis, dash' for pauses or breaks; state-test items test correct ellipsis use, particularly in quoted evidence on argumentative or informative essays where students need to shorten lengthy quotations.",
