      "id": "ela-8-pe-016",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 4 — Informational.</strong> The following is an original article written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>Why Honey Bees Dance</em></p>\n<p>When a worker honey bee returns to her hive after finding a rich patch of flowers, she does not simply tell the other workers where to go. She <em>dances</em>. In the 1940s, the Austrian scientist Karl von Frisch published the first careful description of this dance, and what he discovered overturned a long-standing assumption that insects could not communicate complex information.</p>\n<p>The dance has two main forms. For a food source close to the hive, the returning forager performs a tight circle on the vertical face of the honeycomb — the \"round dance.\" For sources farther away, she performs the famous \"waggle dance,\" walking in a straight line while shaking her abdomen rapidly back and forth, then circling back to repeat the line.</p>\n<p>The information encoded is remarkably precise. The angle of the waggle run, measured against vertical, indicates the direction of the food relative to the sun. The duration of the waggle indicates the distance: roughly one second per kilometer. Other workers crowd around, touching the dancer with their antennae, and they leave the hive flying on the bearings she has given them.</p>\n<p>Some early entomologists doubted von Frisch's interpretation, arguing that the bees were really following scent trails. But careful experiments — including hives placed in featureless landscapes, and dances performed in total darkness — confirmed the dance language. Von Frisch was awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine in 1973.</p>\n<p>The waggle dance remains one of the most sophisticated examples of symbolic communication in the animal kingdom. A bee that has never visited a flower can find it on the strength of a sister's dance alone.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> The writer mentions that von Frisch received the Nobel Prize in 1973. Which rhetorical strategy does this detail most clearly use?</p>",
