      "id": "ela-8-pe-020",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 5 — Informational.</strong> The following are two short opinion pieces written for this practice test. They take opposing positions on the same question.</p>\n<p><em>Source A: \"Middle Schools Should Start Later.\"</em></p>\n<p>Every weekday at 7:15 a.m., millions of American middle schoolers shuffle into first-period classrooms with their eyes half open and their brains, biologically speaking, still asleep. This is not laziness. It is biology. Pediatric sleep researchers have shown for two decades that adolescents undergo a shift in their circadian rhythm — the internal clock that governs sleep — pushing their natural bedtime later and their natural wake time later. A 13-year-old asked to be alert at 7:30 a.m. is roughly equivalent, in physiological terms, to a 40-year-old asked to be alert at 4:30. The cost is measurable: lower grades, more car accidents involving teen drivers, higher rates of anxiety and depression. The American Academy of Pediatrics has recommended for years that middle and high schools begin no earlier than 8:30. Districts that have made the change report better attendance, fewer tardies, and improved test scores. The science is settled. The only question is whether we will act on it.</p>\n<p><em>Source B: \"Don't Move the School Bell.\"</em></p>\n<p>Calls to push the middle-school bell to 8:30 sound reasonable until you ask what happens to the rest of the day. Buses cannot pick up middle schoolers and elementary schoolers at the same time, so a later middle-school start typically forces elementary children onto buses at 6:45 — moving the problem onto younger kids whose own sleep needs are no less important. After-school sports practices get pushed into the dinner hour. Working parents who depend on the current schedule to coordinate child care, jobs, and commutes are left scrambling. Some studies of districts that shifted start times found modest academic gains; others found no measurable difference once the effects faded. Before we rearrange the lives of every family in a district, we should ask whether the same goals — better sleep, fewer tardies, less stress — could be achieved by other means, such as homework limits or earlier bedtimes encouraged by families themselves.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> A student writes: \"Source B is concerned that solving one group's problem may create another.\" Which sentence from Source B provides the strongest direct evidence for this claim?</p>",
      "choices": [
        "\"Calls to push the middle-school bell to 8:30 sound reasonable.\"",
        "\"Buses cannot pick up middle schoolers and elementary schoolers at the same time, so a later middle-school start typically forces elementary children onto buses at 6:45 — moving the problem onto younger kids whose own sleep needs are no less important.\"",
        "\"Some studies of districts that shifted start times found modest academic gains.\"",
        "\"The science is settled.\""
      ],
      "correctText": "\"Buses cannot pick up middle schoolers and elementary schoolers at the same time, so a later middle-school start typically forces elementary children onto buses at 6:45 — moving the problem onto younger kids whose own sleep needs are no less important.\"",
      "topic": "Reading Informational Text (RI)",
      "explanation": "This sentence directly names the trade-off — solving the middle-school sleep problem by creating an elementary-school sleep problem. It is the clearest evidence in the source that the policy may shift, not eliminate, the underlying harm. The fourth choice is from Source A, not Source B.",
      "domain": "Practice Exam",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
      "sourceAuthority": "mr-macs-practice-exam",
      "assessmentSourceId": "practice-exam-grade-8-ela-practice",
      "itemMode": "from-practice-exam",
      "course": "ela-8"
