      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 6 — Informational.</strong> The following is an original article written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>The Last Carrier Pigeons</em></p>\n<p>For most of human history, the fastest way to send a written message over long distances was to tie it to the leg of a bird. Carrier pigeons, descended from the rock dove, possess a navigational instinct that has never been fully explained. Released hundreds of miles from their loft, they will find their way home with astonishing reliability — and, more usefully for messengers, they will not stop along the way.</p>\n<p>The Romans used pigeons to report the outcomes of battles. The Rothschild banking family is said to have used them to receive early news of the Battle of Waterloo in 1815. By the time of the First World War, every major army maintained large pigeon corps. A bird named Cher Ami carried a message through enemy fire in October 1918 that is credited with saving nearly two hundred American soldiers; the pigeon arrived at her loft missing one leg and one eye, and she was awarded the French Croix de Guerre.</p>\n<p>Today, the carrier pigeon has been replaced by the telegraph, the telephone, the fax machine, the email, and the encrypted text. The last operational military pigeon service, in India, was retired in 2002. A small number of hobbyists still keep racing pigeons, and a few news agencies in remote regions have, in emergencies, returned to the old method when satellite links have failed.</p>\n<p>It is easy to dismiss the pigeon as a quaint relic. But for two thousand years, this small grey bird was, quite literally, the internet of its day — and it carried, on its slender leg, news that changed the course of empires.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> The closing line — \"this small grey bird was, quite literally, the internet of its day\" — is an example of which rhetorical strategy?</p>",
