      "id": "ela-8-pe-003",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 1 — Literature.</strong> The following is an original short story written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>The Tin Whistle</em></p>\n<p>Mara had carried the tin whistle in her coat pocket for six winters, and in all that time she had never played a single note where anyone could hear. Her grandfather had given it to her the spring before he died, pressing it into her palm with fingers that had once coaxed reels out of fiddles in three counties. \"It's only a cheap thing,\" he had said, looking at her with eyes the color of river ice. \"But the music in it is yours. Don't be saving it for some braver girl.\"</p>\n<p>She had nodded, and she had meant it, and she had failed him for six years.</p>\n<p>The whistle came out of her pocket only at the kitchen table after midnight, when the house was a held breath and the radiator clanged and her younger brother slept upstairs with the door wedged shut against bad dreams. Then she would lift it to her lips and her fingers would walk the holes the way her grandfather had shown her, and the notes would come out the color of a kitchen-light yellow — thin, tentative, almost apologetic, as if asking the room for permission.</p>\n<p>The talent show flyer was pinned to the corkboard outside the cafeteria. Mara walked past it three times on Monday. On Tuesday she stood in front of it for a full minute, pretending to study her schedule, and then walked away with her ears burning. On Wednesday she signed her name on the third line in handwriting so small the gym teacher had to lean down to read it.</p>\n<p>That night she practiced until the radiator went silent and the windows turned to mirrors. The notes were no longer apologetic. They had begun, very quietly, to insist.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> What is the effect of the grandfather's spoken line, \"But the music in it is yours. Don't be saving it for some braver girl\"?</p>",
      "choices": [
        "It establishes the central challenge the story asks Mara to meet.",
        "It explains why the whistle is made of tin instead of silver.",
        "It shifts the setting from the kitchen to a fiddle hall.",
        "It introduces the brother as a major character."
      ],
      "correctText": "It establishes the central challenge the story asks Mara to meet.",
      "topic": "Reading Literature (RL)",
      "explanation": "The grandfather's line names the very thing Mara has been doing — saving the music for some 'braver girl' — and the rest of the story is her slow attempt to stop doing exactly that. This dialogue plants the conflict that the talent-show plot resolves.",
