      "id": "ela-8-pe-005",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 2 — Literature.</strong> The following is an excerpt from an original short story written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>The Lighthouse Keeper's Daughter</em></p>\n<p>The keeper of the Black Point light was a tall, silent man named Halloran, and his daughter Brigid had grown up between the salt and the lamp. By the time she was twelve she could trim the wicks, polish the brass, and recite the difference between a nor'easter and a sou'wester before her father had finished his tea.</p>\n<p>One October evening the sky over the Atlantic took on the color of a bruise. Halloran stood on the gallery watching the horizon, his great-coat pulled tight, and said nothing for so long that Brigid began to be afraid. At last he turned to her.</p>\n<p>\"The Margaret Lee is coming up the channel tonight,\" he said. \"Captain Mears has thirty souls aboard, and the swell is rising. You'll watch the lamp with me until dawn, do you hear?\"</p>\n<p>Brigid nodded. She thought of the small parlor downstairs, of the warm stove and the book she had been reading, and she said nothing about any of it. She climbed the spiral stair behind her father with her shoulders squared, and when the first gust struck the tower and made the windows shudder she did not flinch.</p>\n<p>Years later, when she was a grown woman with daughters of her own, she would say that she had learned everything important about courage in that one long night — not from the storm, which only roared, but from her father, who simply did his work.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> The passage is told from a third-person point of view that focuses on Brigid. How does this point of view shape the reader's understanding of Halloran?</p>",
      "choices": [
        "The reader hears Halloran's private thoughts in his own words.",
        "The reader learns about Halloran only through what Brigid observes and what he says aloud, which makes his silence feel meaningful rather than empty.",
        "The reader cannot tell what Halloran is doing because the narrator is unreliable.",
        "The reader sees Halloran from the perspective of Captain Mears."
      ],
      "correctText": "The reader learns about Halloran only through what Brigid observes and what he says aloud, which makes his silence feel meaningful rather than empty.",
      "topic": "Reading Literature (RL)",
      "explanation": "Limited third person tied to Brigid means we receive Halloran only as she perceives him — his long silence on the gallery, his quiet command, his steady work. Without access to his thoughts, his composure becomes its own kind of teaching, which is exactly what the closing paragraph names.",
      "domain": "Practice Exam",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
      "sourceAuthority": "mr-macs-practice-exam",
      "assessmentSourceId": "practice-exam-grade-8-ela-practice",
      "itemMode": "from-practice-exam",
      "course": "ela-8"
