      "id": "ela-8-pe-007",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 2 — Literature.</strong> The following is an excerpt from an original short story written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>The Lighthouse Keeper's Daughter</em></p>\n<p>The keeper of the Black Point light was a tall, silent man named Halloran, and his daughter Brigid had grown up between the salt and the lamp. By the time she was twelve she could trim the wicks, polish the brass, and recite the difference between a nor'easter and a sou'wester before her father had finished his tea.</p>\n<p>One October evening the sky over the Atlantic took on the color of a bruise. Halloran stood on the gallery watching the horizon, his great-coat pulled tight, and said nothing for so long that Brigid began to be afraid. At last he turned to her.</p>\n<p>\"The Margaret Lee is coming up the channel tonight,\" he said. \"Captain Mears has thirty souls aboard, and the swell is rising. You'll watch the lamp with me until dawn, do you hear?\"</p>\n<p>Brigid nodded. She thought of the small parlor downstairs, of the warm stove and the book she had been reading, and she said nothing about any of it. She climbed the spiral stair behind her father with her shoulders squared, and when the first gust struck the tower and made the windows shudder she did not flinch.</p>\n<p>Years later, when she was a grown woman with daughters of her own, she would say that she had learned everything important about courage in that one long night — not from the storm, which only roared, but from her father, who simply did his work.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> Which sentence from the passage most directly states the theme the closing paragraph develops?</p>",
      "choices": [
        "\"She would say that she had learned everything important about courage in that one long night — not from the storm, which only roared, but from her father, who simply did his work.\"",
        "\"The keeper of the Black Point light was a tall, silent man named Halloran.\"",
        "\"The sky over the Atlantic took on the color of a bruise.\"",
        "\"You'll watch the lamp with me until dawn, do you hear?\""
      ],
      "correctText": "\"She would say that she had learned everything important about courage in that one long night — not from the storm, which only roared, but from her father, who simply did his work.\"",
      "topic": "Reading Literature (RL)",
      "explanation": "This is the passage's explicit thematic statement: courage is defined here as quietly doing one's work, contrasted with the loud-but-empty roar of the storm. The other sentences are setting, characterization, or plot — not thematic statement.",
