      "id": "ela-8-pe-011",
      "prompt": "<p><strong>Passage 3 — Literature.</strong> The following is an original short story written for this practice test.</p>\n<p><em>The Borrowed Bicycle</em></p>\n<p>It would have been better, Theo realized later, if he had simply told his cousin Patrick the truth at the start. But standing on the gravel drive at his uncle's farm on the first morning of August, with the new red bicycle leaning against the porch rail like a horse waiting to be saddled, Theo had said yes when he ought to have said no.</p>\n<p>\"You can ride, can't you?\" Patrick had asked, eyeing him sideways.</p>\n<p>\"Of course I can ride,\" Theo said, in the same easy tone his older sister used when she did not want a question repeated.</p>\n<p>What Theo had ridden, in all his thirteen years, was a small blue bicycle with training wheels in the parking lot behind his apartment building, two summers ago, for approximately fifteen minutes, before he had handed it back to its owner and announced that bicycling was for younger children. He had not been on one since.</p>\n<p>The red bicycle, of course, was magnificent. Patrick walked it down to the road and held it steady while Theo climbed on, and for one bright second Theo believed that the magnificence of the machine would simply lift him into competence — that gravity and balance would, for him alone, become polite suggestions. The bicycle, having no such illusions about its rider, gave a single thoughtful wobble and dumped him sideways into the gravel.</p>\n<p>Patrick said nothing. He picked up the bicycle, looked at Theo, and said only, \"Right. Tomorrow morning, six o'clock, before anyone's up. I'll teach you.\"</p>\n<p>And so the long week of lessons began.</p><p><strong>Question:</strong> What does Patrick's final line — \"Right. Tomorrow morning, six o'clock, before anyone's up. I'll teach you.\" — most reveal about him?</p>",
      "choices": [
        "He is impatient and embarrassed by his cousin.",
        "He is practical and discreet, willing to help Theo without humiliating him.",
        "He is uninterested in spending time with his cousin.",
        "He plans to tell his uncle about Theo's lie."
      ],
      "correctText": "He is practical and discreet, willing to help Theo without humiliating him.",
      "topic": "Reading Literature (RL)",
      "explanation": "Patrick says nothing about the lie, picks the bicycle up, sets a time before anyone is awake, and offers to teach. The choice of an early-morning, private lesson protects Theo from being seen — a quiet act of kindness that characterizes Patrick more sharply than any direct statement would.",
      "domain": "Practice Exam",
