      "explanation": "Steinbeck pairs George Milton (small, sharp, planning) with Lennie Small (huge, gentle, forgetful) to dramatize how rare companionship is in 1930s migrant life. Crooks's later confession 'a guy needs somebody' generalizes the foil's lesson. RL.9-10.2 asks for theme analysis with evidence; the central idea of loneliness emerges through paired contrasts (Curley's wife, Candy, Crooks). Regents argument essays often use this novella as evidence.",
