      "correctText": "Penelope",
      "topic": "Foil Characters",
      "explanation": "Penelope's loom trick (weaving Laertes's shroud by day, unraveling it by night) buys her three years against the suitors. Her cunning mirrors Odysseus's polymetis, and her bed test (Book 23) confirms his identity. RL.9-10.3 asks how characters develop themes; Penelope embodies loyalty (kleos) and intelligence. Regents Part 3 prompts on resilience or marriage often use her. Margaret Atwood's Penelopiad updates her perspective.",
