      "correctText": "Second person address",
      "topic": "Rhetorical Devices",
      "explanation": "Second-person address ('you' direct address) creates intimacy and pulls the reader into the argument. Persuasive writing often uses 'you' to build relationship. RI.9-10.6 covers rhetorical strategy. James Baldwin's 'My Dungeon Shook' (1963) addresses his nephew directly, modeling how second-person address makes general arguments personal. Regents Part 3 source-based argument essays sometimes feature second-person texts. AP Lang demands students analyze pronoun choice.",
