      "correctText": "Doppelganger",
      "topic": "Symbolism in The Raven",
      "explanation": "Mary Shelley's Frankenstein (1818) and Poe's poetry share the Romantic-Gothic motif of the doppelganger, the dark double of the protagonist's self. Victor and the Creature mirror each other; the raven externalizes the speaker's grief. RL.9-10.4 asks how figurative language develops complex meaning. Regents Part 3 source-based arguments on identity and isolation often draw on doppelganger analysis. AP Lit later builds on this.",
