      "correctText": "Mournful never-ending remembrance",
      "topic": "Symbolism in The Raven",
      "explanation": "Edgar Allan Poe in 'The Philosophy of Composition' (1846) explicitly stated the raven symbolizes 'Mournful and Never-ending Remembrance' of his lost Lenore. The bird's single word 'Nevermore' becomes the speaker's torment. RL.9-10.4 covers figurative meaning of words and phrases; analyzing symbolism is core. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice often asks 'What does X symbolize in the passage?' Poe's deliberate symbol design is a teaching staple.",
