      "explanation": "Dramatic irony occurs when the audience knows something a character does not, creating tension between knowledge levels. Shakespeare's Romeo and Juliet ending (audience knows Juliet is alive when Romeo drinks poison) is canonical. RL.9-10.5 covers how authors structure texts for effect. Regents Part 2 multiple-choice tests irony types. AP Lit narrative analysis demands precise irony naming: verbal (saying opposite), situational (event-outcome mismatch), dramatic (audience-character gap).",
