      "correctText": "Odysseus",
      "topic": "The Odyssey Basics",
      "explanation": "Odysseus (Latin: Ulysses) is the Greek king of Ithaca whose ten-year return from Troy frames Homer's epic. Homer's recurring epithet polymetis ('of many devices') captures his defining trait: cunning over brute strength. RL.9-10.3 asks how complex characters develop; Odysseus is the prototype. Regents Part 3 source-based prompts on perseverance, hubris, or identity routinely cite The Odyssey. AP Lit later uses Tennyson's 'Ulysses' as a companion text.",
