      "explanation": "A coordinate proof uses coordinates and algebraic formulas (distance, midpoint, slope) to verify geometric properties (G-GPE.4). To set up a coordinate proof, place the figure strategically — one vertex at the origin simplifies calculations. Compute relevant quantities and cite the algebraic results as evidence. On NY Geometry Regents Part III and IV, students compute slopes for parallelism/perpendicularity, distances for side and diagonal lengths, and midpoints for bisection. Descartes's 1637 analytic geometry made this proof method possible.",
