      "topic": "Foundations",
      "explanation": "A right angle measures exactly 90° and is marked with a small square symbol in diagrams (G-CO.9). Right angles are foundational: the Pythagorean theorem applies only to right triangles; trig ratios are defined in terms of right-triangle sides; perpendicular lines form right angles. The converse of the Pythagorean theorem (if a²+b²=c², the triangle is a right triangle) allows students to verify a right angle from given side lengths — a common proof technique on NY Geometry Regents.",
