      "correctText": "Pythagorean theorem",
      "topic": "Foundations",
      "explanation": "The Pythagorean Theorem states that in a right triangle with legs a, b and hypotenuse c, a²+b²=c² (G-SRT.8). Pythagoras of Samos (~500 BCE) gave a general proof, but the relationship was known to Babylonians (Plimpton 322, ~1800 BCE) and Egyptians. Over 370 distinct proofs exist, including Euclid's (Elements Book I, Prop. 47) and President James Garfield's 1876 trapezoid proof. On NY Geometry Regents, it appears in nearly every unit: finding side lengths, verifying right angles, finding diagonals, and computing coordinate distances.",
