      "correctText": "Pythagorean triple",
      "topic": "Foundations",
      "explanation": "A Pythagorean triple is a set of three positive integers satisfying a²+b²=c², such as 3-4-5, 5-12-13, 8-15-17, and 7-24-25 (G-SRT.8). Multiples are also triples: 6-8-10, 9-12-15. General formula: a=m²-n², b=2mn, c=m²+n² for integers m>n>0. On NY Geometry Regents, Pythagorean triples appear in problems designed for clean integer answers — recognizing 3-4-5 or 5-12-13 immediately gives the answer without a calculator. Memorizing the most common triples saves significant time on the exam.",
