      "correctText": "AA similarity",
      "topic": "Foundations",
      "explanation": "AA (Angle-Angle) Similarity Theorem: two triangles are similar when two pairs of corresponding angles are congruent (G-SRT.3). Because the angle sum of a triangle is 180°, two equal angle pairs force the third pair to be equal. AA is the most efficient similarity criterion — no side lengths are needed. On NY Geometry Regents, AA is cited when parallel lines create congruent corresponding or alternate interior angles, or when a shared angle and vertical angle provide two equal pairs. Euclid proved AA similarity in Elements Book VI Proposition 4 (~300 BCE).",
