      "explanation": "SSS Similarity Theorem: two triangles are similar when all three pairs of corresponding sides are proportional — ratios a'/a = b'/b = c'/c all equal the same constant k (G-SRT.5). Unlike SSS congruence (equal lengths), SSS similarity requires equal ratios. On NY Geometry Regents, SSS similarity appears when all six side lengths are given and students must verify proportionality or find a missing side. Euclid's Elements Book VI Proposition 5 (~300 BCE) contains the equivalent result. Once similarity is established, corresponding angles are congruent.",
