      "explanation": "SAS Similarity Theorem: two triangles are similar when two pairs of corresponding sides are proportional and the included angles between them are congruent (G-SRT.5). The included angle requirement is essential — SAS without the included angle does not guarantee similarity. On NY Geometry Regents, SAS similarity is applied when two side ratios are verified equal and the angle between them is given or proved congruent. It appears in multi-step proof questions and problems involving midsegments and parallel-cut lines.",
