      "correctText": "similar triangles",
      "topic": "Foundations",
      "explanation": "Two triangles are similar (△ABC ~ △DEF) if corresponding angles are congruent and corresponding sides are proportional (G-SRT.2). Criteria are AA, SAS~, and SSS~. Once similarity is established, all corresponding angle pairs are equal and all side ratios equal the scale factor k. On NY Geometry Regents, problems ask students to set up proportions, solve for missing lengths, or write similarity statements with vertices in correct corresponding order. Thales of Miletus (~600 BCE) used similar triangles to measure the height of Egyptian pyramids by comparing shadow lengths.",
