      "correctText": "radical side length",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "A radical side length is an exact length expressed using a square-root symbol (e.g., 3√2 or 5√3) rather than a decimal approximation (G-SRT.8). Radical lengths arise from the Pythagorean theorem and special right triangles. On NY Geometry Regents, exact radical form is required for answers involving surds; decimals lose precision. To simplify: √50=√(25·2)=5√2. Add/subtract like radicals: 3√2+5√2=8√2. Rationalize denominators: 1/√2=√2/2. The reference sheet reminds students to leave answers in simplest radical form.",
