      "correctText": "center of dilation",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "The center of dilation is the fixed point from which all image points are located along rays through their pre-image counterparts at distance k times the original. For origin-centered dilations, the rule is (x,y)→(kx,ky) (G-SRT.1). To find the center from a pre-image/image pair, draw lines through each pair of corresponding vertices — they converge at the center. Regents items typically provide the center's coordinates for non-origin centers. Identifying the center from a graph is a recurring exam skill in the similarity unit.",
