      "explanation": "Under a dilation, the image of any segment not passing through the center of dilation is parallel to the original segment (G-SRT.1). This follows from the definition: every point moves along a ray through the center by factor k, leaving the direction of the segment unchanged. On NY Geometry Regents, this parallelism is cited in proofs where a dilation establishes the Midsegment Theorem (midsegment parallel to base) or the Side-Splitter Theorem (proportional cuts create a parallel line).",
