      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "An enlargement factor k>1 means every distance from the center of dilation is multiplied by k, producing a larger similar figure (G-SRT.1). If k=2, every length doubles and area quadruples (k²=4). On NY Geometry Regents, enlargement problems give the pre-image coordinates and scale factor and ask for image coordinates, or give both figures and ask students to calculate k by dividing image by pre-image lengths. Enlargement factors appear in real-world modeling problems (projecting an image, enlarging a blueprint), connecting to G-MG applications.",
