      "correctText": "dilation factor",
      "topic": "Key Ideas",
      "explanation": "The dilation factor k multiplies every distance from the center to produce the image. If k>1 the image is larger; if 0<k<1 it is smaller. In coordinate notation for origin-centered dilations, (x,y)→(kx,ky) (G-SRT.1). Area scales by k². On NY Geometry Regents, students find k by dividing image length by pre-image length, or find image coordinates by multiplying by k. The dilation factor appears explicitly in Regents transformation problems and is the foundation for all similarity proportion work.",
