      "correctText": "perpendicular bisector",
      "topic": "Lines and Equations",
      "explanation": "The perpendicular bisector passes through the midpoint of a segment at right angles to it — the locus of all points equidistant from the two endpoints (G-GPE.5, G-CO.12). To find it: compute the midpoint, compute the segment slope, take the negative reciprocal, write point-slope form through the midpoint. On NY Geometry Regents, perpendicular bisector questions appear in coordinate proof and locus problems. The circumcenter of a triangle is the intersection of the three perpendicular bisectors.",
