      "explanation": "The locus of all points equidistant from two fixed points is the perpendicular bisector of the segment connecting them (G-GPE.5). In coordinate geometry, the equation is found using the midpoint formula and the negative-reciprocal slope. On NY Geometry Regents, compound locus problems often combine this with a second condition (e.g., fixed distance from a point = a circle), and the solution is the intersection of the two loci — typically 0, 1, or 2 points.",
