      "correctText": "radian measure",
      "topic": "Measures",
      "explanation": "Radian measure expresses angle size as arc length divided by radius: θ (rad)=s/r, so s=rθ (G-C.5). One full revolution = 2π radians = 360°. Conversion: degrees × π/180 = radians; radians × 180/π = degrees. Common conversions: 90°=π/2, 180°=π, 270°=3π/2. On NY Geometry Regents, radian measure appears in arc length s=rθ and in degree-radian conversions. The name 'radian' was coined in 1873 by James Thomson; Roger Cotes (~1714) developed the concept.",
