      "explanation": "Precision in measurement is the degree of detail, determined by the smallest unit of the measuring instrument (G-MG.2). A millimeter-graduated ruler measures to the nearest mm; a centimeter-graduated one to the nearest cm. On NY Geometry Regents, precision governs rounding: 'to the nearest tenth' or 'to the nearest square foot.' Reporting more decimal places than the instrument supports is false precision; rounding too aggressively loses information. Small measurement errors can compound in multi-step calculations.",
