      "explanation": "A reasonable approximation is a value close enough to the exact answer for the real-world purpose (G-MG.1, G-MG.3). On NY Geometry Regents, 'estimate to the nearest integer' calibrates the acceptable level. Using π≈3.14 vs. the calculator's π introduces an acceptable small error. Archimedes (~250 BCE) bounded π between 223/71 and 22/7 — an early example of bounding by reasonable approximations. In Regents justification questions, students must state that their approximation is reasonable given the context.",
