      "explanation": "The Parallel Postulate (Euclid, Elements Book I, ~300 BCE): through a point not on a given line, exactly one line can be drawn parallel to the given line — known as Playfair's Axiom (1795). This postulate distinguishes Euclidean geometry from non-Euclidean geometries developed by Gauss, Bolyai, and Lobachevsky in the 1820s. On NY Geometry Regents, the parallel postulate underpins all parallel-line theorems used in proofs. G-CO.9 requires students to prove theorems about parallel lines using this foundational axiom.",
