      "correctText": "angle bisector theorem",
      "topic": "Power + Change",
      "explanation": "The Angle Bisector Theorem: the bisector of an angle of a triangle divides the opposite side in the ratio of the adjacent sides: BD/DC = AB/AC when BD bisects angle B (G-SRT.4). On NY Geometry Regents, problems give three of the four segment lengths and ask for the fourth by proportion. The theorem follows from similar triangles or the Law of Sines. Euclid proved a related proposition in Elements Book VI Proposition 3 (~300 BCE). Students must not confuse this with the perpendicular bisector.",
