      "explanation": "The ratio of the perimeters of two similar figures equals the similarity ratio k (G-SRT.2). If two similar triangles have sides in ratio 3:5, perimeters are also in ratio 3:5. This follows because perimeter is a sum of lengths, all scaling by the same k. On NY Geometry Regents, perimeter ratio problems give the similarity ratio and one perimeter and ask for the other: P₂=k·P₁. The area ratio equals k². Students must keep the three scaling tiers distinct: linear k, area k², volume k³.",
