      "correctText": "altitude to hypotenuse",
      "topic": "Power + Change",
      "explanation": "When an altitude is drawn to the hypotenuse of a right triangle, it creates two smaller triangles each similar to the original and to each other (G-SRT.4). Three geometric mean relationships follow: altitude = √(p·q) where p and q are the hypotenuse segments; each leg = √(full hypotenuse × adjacent segment). On NY Geometry Regents, students set up these proportions and solve for unknown lengths. The property is a consequence of AA similarity applied to the three right triangles sharing the right-angle vertex.",
