      "correctText": "logical sequence",
      "topic": "Proof Language",
      "explanation": "A logical sequence in a two-column proof is the ordered chain of statements, each supported by a cited reason, that moves from given information to the prove statement without gaps (G-CO.9). Each reason must be a definition, postulate, or previously proved theorem. On NY Geometry Regents, partial credit is awarded per step — a broken logical sequence loses credit at the point of the gap. Euclid's Elements (~300 BCE) established this model: every proposition is proved by a numbered sequence of justified steps from first principles.",
