      "correctText": "postulate",
      "topic": "Proof Language",
      "explanation": "A postulate (axiom) is a statement accepted as true without proof — the starting assumptions of an axiomatic system. Euclid's five postulates (~300 BCE) include: a straight line can be drawn between any two points, and all right angles are equal (G-CO.9). The Parallel Postulate (through a point not on a line, exactly one parallel exists) is the foundation of Euclidean geometry. On NY Geometry Regents, postulates appear as proof reasons: 'Segment Addition Postulate,' 'Angle Addition Postulate,' 'SAS Postulate.'",
