      "correctText": "theorem",
      "topic": "Proof Language",
      "explanation": "A theorem is a mathematical statement proved deductively from postulates, definitions, and previously established theorems. Euclid's Elements (~300 BCE) organized hundreds of theorems in logical order from five postulates (G-CO.9, G-CO.10). On NY Geometry Regents, students cite theorems by name in proof Reason columns: 'Alternate Interior Angles Theorem,' 'Base Angles Theorem,' 'Pythagorean Theorem.' A theorem may only be cited after it has been proved or is listed as given. Stating the theorem name alone is sufficient as a reason.",
