      "correctText": "bioaccumulation",
      "topic": "Biodiversity Threats",
      "explanation": "Bioaccumulation is the buildup of a persistent, fat-soluble toxin (mercury, DDT, PCBs) inside an organism's body over its lifetime, faster than it can be metabolized or excreted. The organism absorbs the toxin through food, water, or air, and stores it (often in fatty tissue). Concentrations rise as the organism ages. Bioaccumulation is the first step in biomagnification, where toxins concentrate up the food chain. Mercury bioaccumulates particularly in long-lived predator fish (tuna, swordfish, king mackerel), leading to FDA advisories on consumption. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses bioaccumulation in food-chain pollution questions.",
