      "explanation": "A pathogen is any organism or virus that can cause disease — bacteria (Streptococcus, E. coli), viruses (influenza, HIV, SARS-CoV-2), fungi (athlete's foot, ringworm, Candida), protozoa (malaria-causing Plasmodium, Giardia), and parasitic worms (tapeworms, roundworms, schistosomes). Pathogens enter the body through respiratory droplets, contaminated food and water, vectors like mosquitoes and ticks, sexual contact, or direct skin contact. Pathogen-host interactions drive much of the immune system's evolution. Louis Pasteur's germ theory (1860s) was the foundation of modern medicine, showing that microscopic pathogens — not 'bad air' — cause infectious diseases. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses pathogens in disease and immunity questions.",
