      "explanation": "A conservation strategy combines science, law, and policy to protect and restore biodiversity and ecosystems. Tools include protected areas (national parks, wildlife refuges, marine reserves); sustainable harvest quotas (fishing limits, hunting seasons); endangered species laws (1973 U.S. Endangered Species Act); habitat restoration (replanting native forests, removing invasive species, dam removal); captive-breeding programs (California condor, black-footed ferret); wildlife corridors connecting fragmented habitats; and international agreements (CITES regulating trade in endangered species, the Paris climate agreement). Conservation involves tradeoffs with economic development, jobs, and indigenous rights. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses conservation strategies in human-impact and biodiversity questions.",
