      "topic": "Ecology Transfer",
      "explanation": "Energy flow through ecosystems is one-way: sunlight is captured by producers through photosynthesis, becomes chemical energy in biomass, and is passed up trophic levels as organisms eat each other. About 90% of energy is lost at each level — used for metabolism, lost as heat from cellular respiration, or in undigested waste — leaving only ~10% to the next level. This explains why food chains rarely exceed 4-5 links and why top predators are rare. Energy ultimately leaves the ecosystem as heat. This is fundamentally different from matter cycling, which recirculates. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses energy pyramids and the 10% rule constantly.",
