      "explanation": "An energy pyramid (also called a trophic pyramid) shows how energy is distributed across the trophic levels of an ecosystem. The producer level (base) is the widest because producers capture the most energy from sunlight. Each successive level (primary consumer, secondary consumer, tertiary consumer) is about 10% the size of the level below — because most energy is lost as heat from metabolism, used for movement, or excreted. This is why top predators are rare and why short food chains support more biomass than long ones. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses energy pyramids and asks students to identify producer/consumer levels and predict effects of removing one level.",
