      "correctText": "comparative anatomy",
      "topic": "Evidence for Evolution",
      "explanation": "Comparative anatomy compares the body structures of different species. Homologous structures (same underlying anatomy, different functions — human arm, whale flipper, bat wing, cat leg) reveal descent from a common ancestor. Analogous structures (different anatomy, same function — bird wing and insect wing) reveal convergent evolution in similar environments. Vestigial structures (the human appendix, whale pelvic bones, blind cavefish eye sockets) are reduced or non-functional remnants of features useful in ancestors. Darwin used comparative anatomy as a centerpiece of his evidence in On the Origin of Species (1859). NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses limb diagrams to test this constantly.",
