      "correctText": "biogeography",
      "topic": "Evidence for Evolution",
      "explanation": "Biogeography is the study of where species live and why. Closely related species are often clustered in nearby geographic regions because they share recent common ancestry. Darwin's Galapagos finches (HMS Beagle voyage 1831-1836) were all closely related to one mainland South American finch, but each Galapagos island had its own variant adapted to that island's food. Australia's diverse marsupials (kangaroos, koalas, wombats, Tasmanian devils) evolved in isolation after Australia separated from the other continents. Convergent evolution explains why marsupial mammals and placental mammals from other continents independently evolved similar body forms (marsupial mole vs. true mole). NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests biogeography in evolution questions.",
