      "topic": "Exam Misconceptions",
      "explanation": "An acquired trait is a feature an individual gains during its lifetime through use, learning, or environment — bigger biceps from weight training, a tan from sun exposure, a scar from injury, knowledge from school. Acquired traits are NOT inherited by offspring because they are not encoded in the gametic DNA. This is a key Regents misconception trap — Jean-Baptiste Lamarck's discredited 'inheritance of acquired characteristics' theory (1809) held that a giraffe stretching its neck reaching for leaves would pass on the longer neck to its offspring. The correct Darwinian explanation: giraffes with variation in neck length already existed; longer-necked variants survived and reproduced more. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests this every year.",
