      "correctText": "environmental pressure",
      "topic": "Exam Misconceptions",
      "explanation": "An environmental pressure (also called selective pressure or selection pressure) is any factor in the environment that affects an organism's survival and reproduction differently depending on its inherited traits. Examples: a drought favors plants with deep roots; a new predator favors prey that are well-camouflaged; antibiotic use favors resistant bacteria; cold weather favors warm-blooded animals with thick fur or feathers. When the environmental pressure changes, the direction of natural selection changes — peppered moths during the Industrial Revolution evolved darker coloration when tree bark was stained with soot. NY Living Environment Regents Part B asks students to identify the environmental pressure in a scenario and predict which variants will be favored.",
