      "correctText": "common ancestor",
      "topic": "Exam Misconceptions",
      "explanation": "A common ancestor is a species (often extinct, sometimes still living) from which two or more current species descended. On a cladogram or phylogenetic tree, the common ancestor is the branch point (node) where two lineages diverge. The most recent common ancestor of two species lies at their lowest shared node. Universal common ancestry — the claim that all life on Earth shares a single most-distant common ancestor (LUCA, the Last Universal Common Ancestor) — is supported by the universality of DNA, the genetic code, ATP, ribosomes, and core metabolic pathways across all known life. NY Living Environment Regents Part B routinely asks students to identify the most recent common ancestor of two species on a cladogram.",
