      "topic": "Feedback Loops",
      "explanation": "Positive feedback amplifies a change instead of reversing it — the response makes the change even bigger. Examples in healthy human physiology include childbirth (uterine contractions release oxytocin, which causes stronger contractions, releasing more oxytocin, until birth) and blood clotting (platelets at a wound site release chemicals that attract more platelets, until the clot is complete). Positive feedback is rare compared to negative feedback because it must terminate (when birth or clot formation is complete) — otherwise it spirals out of control. NY Living Environment Regents Part B asks students to distinguish negative from positive feedback in physiological scenarios.",
