      "topic": "Feedback Loops",
      "explanation": "Negative feedback is the most common homeostatic mechanism. When a variable strays from its set point, sensors detect the change, the body sends signals to effectors, and the effectors act to reverse the change — bringing the variable back toward the set point. Examples: body temperature drops, you shiver (muscle contraction produces heat); blood sugar rises after a meal, the pancreas releases insulin (cells absorb glucose and blood sugar drops); blood pressure rises, blood vessels dilate (pressure falls). The system is 'negative' because it counteracts (reduces, negates) the disturbance. NY Living Environment Regents Part B-2 uses negative-feedback diagrams in homeostasis questions constantly.",
