      "correctText": "mutation effect",
      "topic": "Genes to Traits",
      "explanation": "A mutation's effect depends on what changes and where. Silent mutations change a codon to another codon for the same amino acid — no effect. Missense mutations change one amino acid; effects vary from minor to severe (sickle-cell anemia is missense). Nonsense mutations create a stop codon, truncating the protein. Frameshift mutations (insertions or deletions not in multiples of three) shift the reading frame, usually destroying protein function. Mutations in non-coding regions can affect gene expression. Most mutations in body cells affect only the individual; mutations in gametes are passed to offspring. Mutations are the ultimate source of new alleles on which natural selection acts. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests mutation effects in evolution and disease contexts.",
