      "correctText": "DNA replication",
      "topic": "Genes to Traits",
      "explanation": "DNA replication is the process by which a cell duplicates its entire DNA before dividing. It is semi-conservative — each new DNA molecule contains one original strand and one newly synthesized strand. The double helix unwinds, helicase enzymes separate the strands, and DNA polymerase reads each template strand and adds complementary nucleotides (A pairs with T, G pairs with C). Errors are rare (~1 in a billion bases) thanks to proofreading; uncorrected errors are mutations. Replication occurs in S-phase of the cell cycle, before mitosis or meiosis. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses DNA-replication diagrams and the base-pairing rule to test students.",
