      "prompt": "The two-step process by which the DNA's coded message becomes a working protein — transcription in the nucleus, then translation at the ribosome.",
      "choices": [
        "protein synthesis",
        "biotechnology evidence",
        "introduced species",
        "inheritance pattern"
      ],
      "correctText": "protein synthesis",
      "topic": "Genes to Traits",
      "explanation": "Protein synthesis converts the DNA code into a functioning protein in two steps. Transcription (in the nucleus): DNA is read by RNA polymerase, which produces a complementary messenger RNA (mRNA) strand using the bases A, U, G, C. Translation (at the ribosome, in the cytoplasm): the ribosome reads the mRNA three bases at a time (a codon), and each codon specifies one amino acid via the tRNA carrying it. Amino acids link in order to form a polypeptide, which folds into a functioning protein. A single base change (mutation) can change one codon, alter one amino acid, and dramatically change protein function (sickle-cell anemia is the classic example). NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses codon charts every year.",
