      "correctText": "inheritance pattern",
      "topic": "Genes to Traits",
      "explanation": "Inheritance patterns describe how traits move from parents to offspring. Simple Mendelian patterns: dominant-recessive with one gene having two alleles. Codominance: both alleles fully expressed (AB blood type). Incomplete dominance: heterozygote intermediate (pink flowers from red x white). Sex-linked: genes on X (mostly) — affects males more often (color blindness, hemophilia). Polygenic: many genes contribute (height, skin color, intelligence). Environmental influence: same genotype produces different phenotypes in different conditions. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses pedigree charts and Punnett squares to test these patterns — students must identify whether a trait is dominant or recessive, autosomal or sex-linked.",
