      "topic": "Human Reproduction",
      "explanation": "The placenta is a disc-shaped organ that develops on the wall of the uterus during pregnancy. The fetus is connected to it by the umbilical cord. Fetal and maternal blood do not mix directly — instead, the placenta provides a thin barrier across which nutrients and oxygen diffuse from the mother to the fetus, and CO2 and metabolic wastes diffuse the other way. The placenta also produces hormones (progesterone, hCG) that maintain pregnancy. Unfortunately, some substances also cross the placenta — including alcohol, nicotine, many drugs, and infectious agents — which is why prenatal care emphasizes avoiding teratogens. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests placental function in pregnancy diagrams.",
