      "prompt": "Darwin's mechanism for evolution — the process where individuals with favorable inherited traits survive, reproduce, and pass those traits on at higher rates than others.",
      "choices": [
        "overproduction",
        "natural selection",
        "membrane transport",
        "fitness"
      ],
      "correctText": "natural selection",
      "topic": "Natural Selection",
      "explanation": "Natural selection is the central mechanism of evolution proposed by Charles Darwin in On the Origin of Species (1859) and independently by Alfred Russel Wallace. Its four conditions: (1) populations show heritable variation; (2) populations produce more offspring than can survive; (3) competition for limited resources occurs; (4) individuals with traits better suited to the environment survive and reproduce more — passing those alleles to the next generation. Over many generations, the population's allele frequencies shift. Classic evidence: Darwin's Galapagos finches with different beak shapes for different food sources, peppered moths during the Industrial Revolution, and antibiotic resistance in bacteria. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests natural selection in every evolution unit.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
      "standardRefs": [],
      "sourceAuthority": "mr-macs-jeopardy",
