      "topic": "Natural Selection",
      "explanation": "Competition for limited resources — food, water, mates, light, space, nesting sites — is the engine that turns variation into selection. Intraspecific competition (within a species) is especially intense because individuals need exactly the same resources. Interspecific competition (between species) can drive species to evolve different niches (resource partitioning). In Darwin's Galapagos finches, multiple species coexist on the same island by specializing on different food sources, reducing competition. NY Living Environment Regents Part B asks students to identify the resource being competed for in a scenario and predict which variants will be favored as a result.",
