      "explanation": "Digestive enzymes are proteins that catalyze the breakdown of food into smaller absorbable molecules. Major examples: salivary amylase (starch to maltose, in the mouth); pepsin (proteins to peptides, in the stomach, optimal pH ~2); pancreatic amylase, lipase (fats to fatty acids), and trypsin (proteins to peptides) in the small intestine; lactase (lactose to glucose + galactose, on intestinal lining). Each enzyme has an optimum pH; stomach enzymes work best in acid, intestinal enzymes work best in slightly basic conditions. Enzyme function depends on protein shape — too high temperature denatures them. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests digestive enzymes with enzyme-activity graphs.",
