      "correctText": "alveolus",
      "topic": "Nutrition and Gas Exchange",
      "explanation": "Alveoli (plural) are tiny grape-like air sacs at the ends of the smallest bronchioles in the lungs. Each is wrapped in capillaries and is one cell-layer thick — perfect for gas exchange by diffusion. Oxygen diffuses from alveolar air (where its concentration is higher) into capillary blood; CO2 diffuses from blood (where it's higher) into the alveoli to be exhaled. Each human lung holds roughly 300 million alveoli, giving a total gas-exchange surface area of about 70 m2 (the size of a tennis court) — an extreme example of high surface-area-to-volume ratio in service of efficient diffusion. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses alveoli diagrams in gas-exchange and respiratory-system questions.",
