      "correctText": "diaphragm",
      "topic": "Nutrition and Gas Exchange",
      "explanation": "The diaphragm is a large dome-shaped sheet of skeletal muscle that separates the chest cavity from the abdomen. When the diaphragm contracts, it flattens downward, expanding chest volume; this lowers pressure inside the lungs, and air rushes in (inhalation). When the diaphragm relaxes, the chest cavity shrinks, pressure rises, and air is pushed out (exhalation). The intercostal muscles between the ribs assist breathing. Hiccups are involuntary contractions of the diaphragm. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses the diaphragm in respiratory diagrams; students should understand inhalation and exhalation as a pressure-difference mechanism driven by diaphragm contraction.",
