      "correctText": "kidney nephron",
      "topic": "Nutrition and Gas Exchange",
      "explanation": "A nephron is the microscopic functional unit of the kidney; each human kidney has about 1 million nephrons. Each nephron has a glomerulus (a tight ball of capillaries) wrapped in a Bowman's capsule, where blood is filtered under pressure — water, ions, glucose, urea, and small molecules pass into the tubule, while blood cells and large proteins stay in the blood. The tubule then selectively reabsorbs glucose, salts, and water back into the blood while concentrating wastes (urea, excess salts) as urine. The kidney's careful balancing of filtration and reabsorption is central to homeostasis of water, blood pressure, pH, and electrolytes. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses nephron diagrams in excretion questions.",
