      "correctText": "carrier",
      "topic": "Pedigrees and Probability",
      "explanation": "A carrier is heterozygous for a recessive allele — they have one normal allele and one recessive disease allele. They do not show the disease (the dominant normal allele masks it) but can pass the recessive allele to offspring. If two carriers (Aa x Aa) have children, the predicted ratio is 1 AA : 2 Aa : 1 aa, so 25% of offspring will be affected and 50% will be carriers. Cystic fibrosis, sickle-cell anemia, Tay-Sachs, and PKU all show this pattern. NY Living Environment Regents Part B pedigree questions often require identifying carriers among phenotypically normal family members, particularly when an affected child is born to two unaffected parents.",
