      "correctText": "acid rain",
      "topic": "Pollution Sources",
      "explanation": "Acid rain forms when sulfur dioxide (SO2) and nitrogen oxides (NOx) emitted by coal-burning power plants, vehicles, and industry react with water vapor in the atmosphere to form sulfuric and nitric acids. These acids fall back to Earth as rain, snow, or fog with pH below 5.6 (normal rain pH ~5.6 from natural CO2). Acid rain damages forests (especially conifers and at high elevations), acidifies lakes (killing fish), erodes stone buildings and statues, and dissolves toxic metals like aluminum from soil. The U.S. Acid Rain Program (1990 Clean Air Act amendments) cut SO2 emissions sharply. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses acid rain in human-impact questions.",
