      "correctText": "thermal pollution",
      "topic": "Pollution Sources",
      "explanation": "Thermal pollution is the release of warm water (or sometimes very cold water) into a natural body of water, raising or lowering its temperature beyond what aquatic life can tolerate. Power plants — coal, gas, nuclear — use enormous amounts of river or lake water for cooling, then discharge it back several degrees warmer. Warmer water holds less dissolved oxygen (think: warm soda goes flat faster), stressing fish and other aerobic aquatic life. It can also alter spawning cycles and favor invasive species that tolerate warmer water. Solutions include cooling towers and using treated wastewater for cooling. NY Living Environment Regents Part B includes thermal pollution in human-impact questions.",
