      "correctText": "point-source pollution",
      "topic": "Pollution Sources",
      "explanation": "Point-source pollution comes from a single identifiable origin — a factory smokestack, a wastewater discharge pipe, an oil spill from a single tanker, a chemical plant leak. Because the source is identifiable and located in one place, point-source pollution is easier to regulate, monitor, and clean up. The U.S. Clean Water Act (1972) and Clean Air Act (1970) impose permits, emission limits, and reporting requirements on point sources. The 2010 Deepwater Horizon oil spill is a famous example of point-source water pollution. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses pollution-source identification in human-impact questions and asks students to distinguish point vs. nonpoint sources.",
