      "correctText": "symbiosis",
      "topic": "Population Ecology",
      "explanation": "Symbiosis is any close, long-term biological interaction between two species. The three main types: (1) Mutualism — both species benefit (lichens are fungi + algae; gut bacteria help us digest food while feeding on what we eat; pollinators get nectar while moving pollen). (2) Commensalism — one species benefits, the other is unaffected (barnacles on whales). (3) Parasitism — one species benefits at the other's expense (tapeworms in intestines, fleas on dogs, mistletoe on trees). Lynn Margulis's endosymbiotic theory (1967) extended symbiosis to evolutionary timescales: mitochondria and chloroplasts descend from once-free-living bacteria that became permanent symbionts inside ancestral eukaryotes. NY Living Environment Regents tests symbiosis on Part A and Part B.",
