      "correctText": "data-based claim",
      "topic": "Regents Lab Reasoning",
      "explanation": "A data-based claim is a statement about the natural world that is supported by experimental data — measurements, observations, statistical patterns. It is the gold standard of scientific reasoning: every conclusion must be tied back to the evidence, with the strength of the claim matching the strength of the data. Strong data-based claims: come from repeated, controlled experiments; specify both the effect and its size; quantify uncertainty; avoid overgeneralizing beyond what was tested. NY Living Environment Regents has emphasized data-based claims since the 2017 framework — Part B-2 and Part D CRQ items routinely ask students to make claims based on a data table or graph and to defend the claims with specific evidence from the data.",
