      "correctText": "controlled variable",
      "topic": "Regents Lab Reasoning",
      "explanation": "Controlled variables are factors held constant during an experiment so they cannot interfere with the measured effect of the independent variable. In the light-and-plant-growth experiment, controlled variables would include plant species, soil type, water volume, pot size, temperature, and duration — all kept identical across treatment groups. Without controlled variables, any observed effect could be due to one of these other factors rather than the IV being tested — the experiment is invalid. NY Living Environment Regents Part D required-lab questions routinely ask students to identify controlled variables and to explain why each one needs to be held constant for the experiment to be valid.",
