      "correctText": "teratogen",
      "topic": "Regents Risk Factors",
      "explanation": "A teratogen is an environmental agent — chemical, biological, or physical — that can cause birth defects when a fetus is exposed during development. Classic examples: alcohol (causes fetal alcohol syndrome with characteristic facial features and developmental delays), thalidomide (a sedative that caused severe limb malformations in the 1960s), rubella virus, the Zika virus (causes microcephaly), tobacco smoke (low birth weight, preterm birth), and ionizing radiation. Teratogens cross the placenta from mother to fetus. Most are most harmful during the first trimester when organs are forming. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses teratogens in human development and in environmental-health questions.",
