      "correctText": "prenatal care",
      "topic": "Regents Risk Factors",
      "explanation": "Prenatal care is the medical care a pregnant person receives during pregnancy — including nutrition (folic acid to prevent neural-tube defects), screening tests (ultrasound, amniocentesis to detect chromosomal disorders), prenatal vitamins, and avoidance of teratogens. Adequate prenatal care reduces the risk of premature birth, low birth weight, and birth defects. The first eight weeks (during organogenesis) are the most critical, since most major organs form then and the developing embryo is most vulnerable to alcohol, drugs, infection, and radiation. NY Living Environment Regents Part B routinely tests prenatal-care concepts as part of human-development units and asks students to explain why early pregnancy is the riskiest period.",
