      "correctText": "birth control",
      "topic": "Regents Risk Factors",
      "explanation": "Birth control methods prevent pregnancy by interrupting one of three steps: ovulation, fertilization, or implantation. Hormonal methods (the pill, patch, ring, injection, implant) use synthetic estrogen and progesterone to suppress FSH and LH, preventing ovulation. Barrier methods (condoms, diaphragms) physically block sperm. IUDs work by preventing fertilization or implantation. Surgical methods (vasectomy, tubal ligation) provide permanent contraception. Each method has different effectiveness and side effects. NY Living Environment Regents Part A may name birth-control methods; Part B uses hormonal contraception in feedback-loop questions about how negative feedback from synthetic hormones suppresses the hypothalamus and pituitary.",
