      "topic": "Reproductive Strategies",
      "explanation": "Sexual reproduction combines genetic material from two parents through meiosis (gamete formation) and fertilization. The resulting offspring carry unique combinations of alleles never seen in either parent. Three sources of variation occur: crossing over during meiosis I, independent assortment of homologous chromosomes, and random fertilization. The variation produced by sexual reproduction is the raw material on which natural selection acts; populations that reproduce sexually can adapt to changing conditions much more rapidly than asexual populations. NY Living Environment Regents Part B tests this in evolution questions about why sexual reproduction is widespread despite its costs (time, energy, finding a mate).",
