      "correctText": "renewable resource",
      "topic": "Resource Use",
      "explanation": "A renewable resource is one that naturally replenishes within a human timescale (years, not millions of years). Examples include solar energy, wind, flowing water, geothermal heat, biomass (firewood, ethanol, biogas), and sustainably-harvested timber and fish. Renewable resources can still be depleted if used faster than they regenerate (overfishing, clear-cutting). The challenge in transitioning to renewables is scale, intermittency (sun doesn't always shine, wind doesn't always blow), storage, and the materials needed for batteries and panels. NY Living Environment Regents Part B distinguishes renewable from nonrenewable resources in human-impact and energy questions, and tests their environmental tradeoffs.",
