      "correctText": "overfishing",
      "topic": "Resource Use",
      "explanation": "Overfishing is the harvesting of fish at a rate that prevents the population from replenishing itself. Industrial fishing fleets with sonar, large nets, and freezer capacity have decimated many fish stocks. The Atlantic cod fishery off Newfoundland collapsed in 1992 after centuries of intensive fishing; over 35,000 workers lost their jobs and the population has still not fully recovered three decades later. Bluefin tuna, swordfish, and many shark species are heavily depleted. Solutions include fishing quotas, marine protected areas where fishing is banned to let populations recover, gear restrictions, and consumer pressure for sustainably-sourced seafood. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses overfishing in resource-use and biodiversity questions.",
