      "correctText": "sustainable yield",
      "topic": "Resource Use",
      "explanation": "Sustainable yield is harvesting a renewable resource at or below its natural replacement rate so the resource pool stays stable. In fisheries, the maximum sustainable yield is the largest annual catch that doesn't reduce the breeding population. In forestry, sustainable logging removes trees no faster than the forest can regrow them. Failure to maintain sustainable yields leads to collapse — Atlantic cod off Newfoundland was overfished and collapsed in 1992, putting 35,000 people out of work. Sustainable agriculture, sustainable fisheries, and sustainable forestry are all attempts to balance human consumption with ecosystem regeneration. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses sustainability in resource-use questions.",
