      "correctText": "cost-benefit analysis",
      "topic": "Solutions and Tradeoffs",
      "explanation": "Cost-benefit analysis (CBA) systematically compares the expected costs and benefits of a decision — economic, social, and environmental — to help choose among options. CBAs assign monetary values to outcomes where possible (jobs created, health-care savings, agricultural production) and provide qualitative descriptions for outcomes that are harder to monetize (species loss, scenic beauty, cultural heritage). Tradeoffs are central — almost every environmental decision benefits some people and harms others. CBAs are used in EISs, in setting pollution standards, in cap-and-trade markets, and in carbon pricing. Critics note that CBA can undervalue what isn't easy to monetize. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses tradeoffs in environmental-decision questions.",
