      "correctText": "tissue",
      "topic": "Specialized Structures",
      "explanation": "A tissue is a group of similar cells with the same specialization that work together. Animals have four basic tissue types — epithelial (covers surfaces and lines cavities), connective (blood, bone, fat, cartilage), muscle (skeletal, smooth, cardiac), and nervous (neurons and supporting cells). Plant tissues include dermal (epidermis), vascular (xylem and phloem), and ground tissue. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses the levels-of-organization sequence — cells form tissues, tissues form organs, organs form organ systems, organ systems form the organism — and requires students to place a structure (e.g., 'heart muscle' or 'leaf epidermis') at the right level.",
