      "correctText": "ecological succession",
      "topic": "Stability and Change",
      "explanation": "Ecological succession is the gradual, predictable change in a community over time after a disturbance. Primary succession starts on bare rock with no soil — after a glacier retreats, a volcanic eruption, or a sandbar emerges — beginning with pioneer species like lichens and mosses that break rock into soil; then grasses, shrubs, fast-growing trees, and eventually a mature climax community. Secondary succession starts where soil already exists but vegetation was wiped out — after a fire, flood, or abandoned farm — and proceeds faster because soil and seed banks remain. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses succession to test community-level dynamics and the role of pioneer species.",
