      "topic": "Trait Expression",
      "explanation": "A sex-linked trait is one whose gene is on a sex chromosome — almost always the X chromosome since the Y carries very few genes. Males (XY) have only one copy of any X-linked gene, so a recessive allele on the X is expressed in males with no second copy to mask it. This explains why disorders like red-green color blindness and hemophilia are far more common in males than females; a female (XX) usually has a second normal allele on her other X. NY Living Environment Regents Part B uses sex-linked Punnett squares (X^a X x X^A Y) and pedigrees with skipped-generation father-to-grandson patterns to test this concept.",
