      "topic": "Transport and Balance",
      "explanation": "Active transport moves substances from low to high concentration — uphill — and requires energy (ATP) and a transport protein. The classic example is the sodium-potassium pump in nerve and muscle cells, which pumps 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ in per ATP. Plant roots use active transport to pull mineral ions (nitrate, potassium) from soil where concentrations are lower than inside the root. Endocytosis and exocytosis (the cell forming a vesicle to engulf or expel material) are also forms of active transport. NY Living Environment Regents Part B distinguishes active transport from diffusion using gradient direction and ATP need.",
