      "explanation": "Passive transport moves substances from high concentration to low concentration — downhill — with no ATP required. The three types are: (1) simple diffusion (small nonpolar molecules slip through the lipid bilayer — O2, CO2); (2) facilitated diffusion (larger or polar molecules pass through a channel or carrier protein — glucose, ions); (3) osmosis (water moves across a selectively permeable membrane). NY Living Environment Regents Part B regularly asks students to read a diagram of a membrane with arrows and identify whether each movement is passive or active, based on gradient direction and presence/absence of ATP.",
