      "explanation": "When a plant cell is in a hypotonic environment (less solute outside than inside), water flows into the central vacuole by osmosis, which presses outward against the rigid cell wall — this is turgor pressure. Turgor pressure keeps non-woody plants upright; when soil is dry and cells lose water, turgor pressure drops and the plant wilts. Place a wilted celery stalk in fresh water and turgor returns. NY Living Environment Regents Part B regularly tests this with experimental data showing plant mass change in different sugar/salt solutions and asks students to explain the water movement by osmosis and the resulting turgor change.",
