      "correctText": "Repeating decimal",
      "topic": "Decimal Operations",
      "explanation": "A repeating decimal has a digit or sequence of digits that repeats indefinitely, written with a bar over the repeating portion. NYS Grade 7 Math standard 7.NS.2.D explicitly tests recognizing that 'every rational number has a decimal expansion that eventually repeats or terminates.' Common Grade 7 examples: 1/3 = 0.333... = 0.3̄; 1/7 = 0.142857142857... = 0.1̄4̄2̄8̄5̄7̄; 1/9 = 0.111... = 0.1̄; 5/6 = 0.8333... = 0.83̄. Fractions whose denominators in simplest form have prime factors other than 2 and 5 give repeating decimals; those with only 2 and 5 give terminating decimals.",
