      "correctText": "Margin of error",
      "topic": "Estimation + Prediction",
      "explanation": "Margin of error indicates how much a sample estimate may differ from the true population value—typically expressed as ±x%. NYS Grade 7 Math standard 7.SP.2 explicitly mentions to 'gauge how far off the estimate or prediction might be.' Worked example: if a sample estimates 55% favor candidate A with margin of error ±3%, the true value is likely between 52% and 58%. Smaller samples have larger margins; larger samples have smaller margins. State-test items test informal margin-of-error reasoning: 'The sample shows 60% favor pizza. The true value is probably somewhere between 55% and 65%, depending on sample size.' Formal margin-of-error calculation comes in high school statistics.",
