      "correctText": "Experimental probability",
      "topic": "Estimation + Prediction",
      "explanation": "Experimental probability is the observed frequency of an event from actual trials: favorable outcomes / total trials. NYS Grade 7 Math standard 7.SP.7 tests experimental probability through 'developing a probability model.' Worked example: rolling a die 60 times and getting 12 fours: experimental probability = 12/60 = 0.20 = 20%. Theoretical probability for a fair die is 1/6 ≈ 16.7%; the experimental value approaches the theoretical value as the number of trials increases (law of large numbers). State-test items test experimental probability calculation and comparison to theoretical predictions: 'After 1,000 trials, the experimental probability should be close to the theoretical value.' Connects directly to Unit 6 (probability).",
