      "correctText": "Theoretical probability",
      "topic": "Estimation + Prediction",
      "explanation": "Theoretical probability is the predicted likelihood of an event based on mathematical reasoning—favorable outcomes / total outcomes. NYS Grade 7 Math standard 7.SP.5 tests probability and 7.SP.6 connects theoretical to experimental probability. Worked example: rolling a standard die, theoretical probability of rolling a 4 = 1/6 (1 favorable out of 6 possible). State-test items test theoretical probability for fair dice, coins, spinners, and card draws. Theoretical probability assumes all outcomes are equally likely (a fair process); when the process is biased, experimental probability provides a better estimate. Theoretical probability connects to Unit 6 (probability) and provides expected long-run frequencies.",
