      "topic": "Angles + Parallel Lines",
      "explanation": "The EXTERIOR ANGLE THEOREM: an exterior angle of a triangle equals the SUM of the two NON-ADJACENT (remote) interior angles. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.G.A.5 explicitly tests 'the exterior angle of triangles.' Worked example: if a triangle has interior angles 50°, 60°, 70°, the exterior angle at the 70° vertex (extending one side) equals 50° + 60° = 110°. Verification: exterior + adjacent interior = 180° (linear pair), so exterior = 180° - 70° = 110° ✓. State-test items test the exterior angle theorem extensively: given two interior angles, find the corresponding exterior angle. The theorem follows from the triangle angle sum 180° and the linear pair property 180°. Foundation for many geometry proofs.",
