      "explanation": "When a transversal crosses two PARALLEL lines, ALTERNATE INTERIOR angles are EQUAL (congruent). NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.G.A.5 tests this. Worked example: if one alternate interior angle is 65°, the other is also 65°. State-test items test this property extensively: given parallel lines and a transversal with one angle marked, find the others using alternate interior, corresponding (also equal), or same-side interior (supplementary). The equality follows from the parallel postulate of Euclid (around 300 BC): if alternate interior angles are equal, the lines are parallel; conversely, parallel lines give equal alternate interior angles. This biconditional is foundational for proving lines parallel and for solving angle puzzles.",
