      "explanation": "A function with a non-constant rate of change is NONLINEAR — its graph is a CURVE, not a straight line. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.F.A.3 explicitly tests linear vs nonlinear distinction. Worked example: y = x^2 has rate of change 1 from x=0 to 1 (y goes 0 to 1), rate of change 3 from x=1 to 2 (y goes 1 to 4), rate of change 5 from x=2 to 3 (y goes 4 to 9) — not constant, so nonlinear. y = 2^x is also nonlinear (exponential). State-test items test linear vs nonlinear from tables (check constant differences), equations (linear: y = mx + b form; nonlinear: any other form), and graphs (line vs curve). The notion that linear functions have CONSTANT rate of change is the distinguishing property tested in Grade 8.",
