      "topic": "Integer Exponents",
      "explanation": "The product-of-powers rule: when multiplying powers with the SAME base, keep the base and ADD the exponents: x^a · x^b = x^(a+b). NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.EE.A.1 explicitly tests 'know and apply the properties of integer exponents to generate equivalent numerical expressions.' Worked example: 2^3 · 2^4 = 2^(3+4) = 2^7 = 128. Verification by counting factors: 2^3 = 2·2·2 (3 factors), 2^4 = 2·2·2·2 (4 factors), product = 2·2·2·2·2·2·2 (7 factors). This rule applies to variables and constants alike: x^5 · x^2 = x^7; 10^3 · 10^4 = 10^7. The rule fails when bases differ: 2^3 · 3^4 cannot be simplified by adding exponents (different bases).",
