      "explanation": "The power-of-a-power rule: when raising a power to a power, keep the base and MULTIPLY the exponents: (x^a)^b = x^(a·b). NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.EE.A.1 tests this rule alongside product/quotient/zero/negative rules. Worked example: (x^3)^4 = x^(3·4) = x^12. Verification: (x^3)^4 = x^3 · x^3 · x^3 · x^3 (multiplied 4 times) = x^(3+3+3+3) = x^12. State-test items test combined rules: (2x^3)^2 = 2^2 · (x^3)^2 = 4x^6 (power distributes over multiplication); (x^2 · x^4)^3 = (x^6)^3 = x^18. Common error: students add instead of multiply — (x^3)^4 ≠ x^7. Memorize: 'PARENS-POW means multiply.' This rule generalizes to all bases.",
