      "topic": "Real-World Data",
      "explanation": "To predict y from x using a linear model y = mx + b, you need both the SLOPE m and the Y-INTERCEPT b. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.3 tests this. Worked example: given m = 2 and b = 5, the model is y = 2x + 5; at x = 4, y = 2(4) + 5 = 13. State-test items test 'write the linear model' from given information: slope and y-intercept, or two points (slope from rise/run, y-intercept from substituting one point into y = mx + b to solve for b). The complete model y = mx + b lets you generate predictions for any x, summarize the relationship, and project beyond observed data (with caution about extrapolation).",
