      "correctText": "Congruent",
      "topic": "Rigid Transformations",
      "explanation": "Two figures related by a sequence of rigid transformations (translations, rotations, reflections) are CONGRUENT — same shape and same size. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.G.A.2 explicitly defines this: 'Understand that a two-dimensional figure is congruent to another if the second can be obtained from the first by a sequence of rotations, reflections, and translations.' Worked example: a triangle and its image after reflection-then-translation are congruent — corresponding sides have equal lengths and corresponding angles have equal measures. State-test items test 'are these figures congruent?' by identifying whether one is a rigid-transformation image of the other. The congruence symbol ≅ denotes this relationship. Critical concept for the rigorous definition of congruence: not just 'looks the same' but provably related by rigid motions.",
