      "explanation": "An OUTLIER is a data point that lies FAR from the overall pattern of the scatter plot. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.1 explicitly tests 'outliers' as a feature of bivariate data. Worked example: in a scatter plot of student heights and weights, a single point at (height 5 ft, weight 250 lb) might be an outlier if all other students are around 100-150 lb. Outliers can result from measurement errors, unusual cases, or natural variation. State-test items test outlier identification: which point doesn't fit the trend? Outliers can heavily influence the trend line (line of best fit) — Grade 8 discusses qualitatively. In real practice, outliers may be excluded, kept, or studied separately depending on whether they reflect errors or genuine atypical cases.",
