      "explanation": "A scatter plot whose points tend to FALL from left to right shows NEGATIVE association — as x increases, y tends to decrease. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.1 tests this. Worked example: a scatter plot of car age (x) vs. resale value (y) typically shows negative association — older cars tend to be worth less. State-test items test negative-association recognition from scatter plots and word descriptions. Distinguish weak from strong negative association: weak = points loosely clustered around a downward trend (some outliers); strong = points tightly clustered. Other examples of likely-negative pairs: exercise hours (x) vs. body fat percentage (y); time studying (x) vs. number of errors (y, in some contexts).",
