      "explanation": "To multiply (2 × 10^4)(3 × 10^5): multiply coefficients (2·3 = 6), add exponents (4+5 = 9), result is 6 × 10^9. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.EE.A.4 explicitly tests scientific-notation multiplication. Verification: 2 × 10^4 = 20,000; 3 × 10^5 = 300,000; product = 6,000,000,000 = 6 × 10^9. ✓ Worked example with coefficient adjustment: (5 × 10^3)(4 × 10^2) = 20 × 10^5 = 2 × 10^6 (since 20 = 2 × 10^1, total exponent becomes 5+1=6). State-test items test products including those needing adjustment for valid scientific-notation form (coefficient must be in [1, 10)). Practice: (7 × 10^-2)(2 × 10^5) = 14 × 10^3 = 1.4 × 10^4.",
