      "topic": "Scientific Notation Arithmetic",
      "explanation": "To add numbers in scientific notation with the SAME exponent: add the coefficients, keep the exponent. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.EE.A.4 tests scientific-notation addition. Worked example: (3 × 10^4) + (5 × 10^4) = (3+5) × 10^4 = 8 × 10^4 = 80,000. If exponents DIFFER: first convert to a common exponent (often the larger), then add coefficients. Example: (3 × 10^4) + (2 × 10^3) = (3 × 10^4) + (0.2 × 10^4) = 3.2 × 10^4. State-test items test both equal-exponent and different-exponent additions, and require results in valid scientific notation. Common error: adding exponents in addition — exponents only add in MULTIPLICATION; in addition/subtraction, exponents must match before combining coefficients.",
