      "topic": "Slope",
      "explanation": "Slope (m) measures a line's steepness: m = rise/run = (change in y)/(change in x) = (y2 - y1)/(x2 - x1). NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.EE.B.6 explicitly tests using similar triangles to explain why slope is the same between any two distinct points on a non-vertical line. The slope concept developed in the 17th century with Descartes' coordinate geometry (1637). Worked example: between (1, 3) and (4, 9): rise = 9-3 = 6, run = 4-1 = 3, slope = 6/3 = 2 (line rises 2 units for every 1 unit right). State-test items test slope from points, tables, and graphs. Positive slope = up-right; negative slope = down-right; zero slope = horizontal; undefined = vertical (run = 0).",
