      "topic": "Two-Way Tables",
      "explanation": "Different RELATIVE FREQUENCIES across rows or columns in a two-way table indicate ASSOCIATION between the two categorical variables. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.4 tests this: 'Use relative frequencies calculated for rows or columns to describe possible association between the two variables.' Worked example: if 80% of pet-owners play sports vs. only 40% of non-pet-owners, the proportions differ substantially — suggesting association between pet ownership and sport participation. State-test items test 'is there association?' by comparing row/column relative frequencies; substantially different proportions indicate association; similar proportions across rows/columns indicate NO association. The two-way table mirrors scatter plots for categorical data: it visualizes the joint distribution of two variables to reveal associations.",
