      "explanation": "A TWO-WAY TABLE (also called a contingency table or cross-tabulation) displays counts at the intersection of two categorical variables. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.4 explicitly tests 'understand that patterns of association can also be seen in bivariate categorical data by displaying frequencies and relative frequencies in a two-way table.' Worked example: a table with rows 'plays sport' / 'does not play sport' and columns 'has pet' / 'does not have pet' shows counts for each combination — e.g., 25 students play and have a pet, 10 play and don't, 5 don't play but have a pet, 15 don't play and don't. State-test items test two-way table construction (from raw data) and interpretation (count, relative frequency, association). Bivariate categorical data analysis is foundational for inferential statistics.",
