      "topic": "Two-Way Tables",
      "explanation": "In a two-way table, ROW TOTALS are placed in the RIGHT MARGIN (a column at the far right), and COLUMN TOTALS in the BOTTOM MARGIN. The grand total (overall sum) sits in the bottom-right corner. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.SP.A.4 tests two-way table construction with marginal totals. Worked example: a table of 100 students broken into pet/no-pet × sport/no-sport: row totals sum each row (e.g., 35 play sport total, 65 don't play); column totals sum each column (e.g., 30 have pets, 70 don't); grand total = 100 students. State-test items test marginal-total computation as the first step in two-way table analysis; relative frequencies use marginal totals as denominators. Foundational table organization skill.",
