      "explanation": "Volume of a cone: V = (1/3)πr²h. NYS Common Core Grade 8 standard 8.G.C.9 explicitly tests cone volume. A cone has 1/3 the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height — a beautiful relationship proven by Eudoxus (around 350 BC) and rigorously by Archimedes. Worked example: cone with r = 3 and h = 10 has V = (1/3)π(3)²(10) = 30π ≈ 94.25 cubic units, exactly one-third of the corresponding cylinder volume (90π). State-test items test cone volume in real contexts (ice cream cones, funnels, party hats). The factor 1/3 is crucial — common error: students forget the 1/3 and compute as if it were a cylinder. Memorize: 'cone is one-third of its cylinder.'",
