      "topic": "Atomic and Nuclear Physics",
      "explanation": "Mass-energy equivalence is Einstein's relation E = m·c^2 (Special Relativity, 1905), expressing that mass and energy are interchangeable: any quantity of mass m has an equivalent rest energy E_rest = m·c^2, and any energy possesses an equivalent mass m = E/c^2. The constant c = 3.00 × 10^8 m/s makes c^2 enormous, so even tiny masses correspond to large energies: 1 gram of matter has rest energy m·c^2 = 0.001 × (3 × 10^8)^2 = 9 × 10^13 J — equivalent to about 21 kilotons of TNT (comparable to the Hiroshima bomb). In nuclear reactions, mass defects (slight differences between reactant and product masses) convert to energy released: a nuclear fission event releases ~200 MeV (≈ 3 × 10^-11 J per fission, corresponding to ~0.1% of the U-235 nucleus's rest mass). Nuclear fusion in stars (H → He) converts mass to energy at higher rates. On Regents items, students compute E from mc^2 and identify the universe's mass-energy connection.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
