      "explanation": "Frequency (f) is the number of complete cycles per unit time. SI unit is the hertz (Hz), defined as 1 cycle per second (s^-1). The NYS Reference Tables list f = 1/T. A 60-Hz generator completes 60 full rotations per second; a typical AM radio carrier might be 1 MHz = 10^6 Hz. For circular motion: omega = 2·pi·f (angular frequency in rad/s). For waves: v = f·lambda (wave speed equals frequency times wavelength, Unit 9). On Regents items, frequency appears in circular motion (revolutions per second), AC electricity (60 Hz in the US, 50 Hz in Europe), waves (sound, light, radio), and quantum physics (E = h·f, Planck's relation, Unit 10). High frequency means short period and rapid oscillation. Heinrich Hertz (1887) first generated and detected radio waves, confirming Maxwell's electromagnetic theory and earning him the unit now bearing his name.",
