      "explanation": "A nonconservative force is one whose work depends on the path taken between two points, not just the endpoints — equivalently, a force that does not have a potential energy function. The most common nonconservative force is friction (kinetic and air drag); others include applied forces, tension when ropes stretch, and viscous fluid drag. Nonconservative forces typically convert mechanical energy (KE + PE) into thermal energy (heat) or other non-mechanical forms, decreasing E_mech. The energy-conservation statement with nonconservative work is ΔE_mech = W_nonconservative (which is negative for friction). Contrast with conservative forces (gravity, ideal spring, electrostatic), whose work depends only on initial and final positions and which DO have potential energy. On Regents items, students compute W_friction = -f_k·d and use it to find energy lost to heat, or to determine how much KE remains after sliding.",
