      "correctText": "power calculation",
      "topic": "Energy and Momentum",
      "explanation": "Power calculations use P = W/t (work per time) or P = F·v (force times velocity), with SI unit the watt (W = J/s). NYS Reference Tables list both forms. For a motor lifting a 100 kg load through 5 m in 10 s: W = mgh = (100)(9.81)(5) = 4905 J; P = W/t = 4905/10 = 490 W. For a car moving at constant 25 m/s with engine providing 6000 N of force to overcome air resistance: P = F·v = (6000)(25) = 150,000 W = 150 kW (about 200 hp). For electrical power: P = I·V = I^2·R = V^2/R. Energy consumption: W = P·t. On cumulative Regents items, power problems combine work, energy, and time concepts; students may need to find the rate of energy delivery, the time required to complete a task with given power, or the speed at which a vehicle reaches steady state under constant engine power. James Watt's improvements to the steam engine (~1769) gave us the unit; horsepower (746 W) is the historical horse-equivalent unit.",
