      "correctText": "impulse graph area",
      "topic": "Energy and Momentum",
      "explanation": "Impulse equals the area under a force-time graph: J = integral of F·dt = area (NYS Reference Tables list J = F·Δt). For a constant force: area = rectangle = F·Δt. For a varying force: area is computed as a sum of rectangles, triangles, or trapezoids (or counted as grid squares for a discrete graph). The impulse-momentum theorem then connects impulse to momentum change: J = Δp = m(v_f - v_i). For a 5 kg cart at rest receiving a triangular force pulse with peak 100 N over Δt = 0.4 s: impulse = (1/2)(100)(0.4) = 20 N·s. Final velocity: v_f = J/m = 20/5 = 4 m/s. On cumulative Regents items, students extract impulse from gridded F-t graphs and translate to Δp, then to v_f. The technique parallels reading displacement from v-t graphs (area = accumulated quantity), unified under the integral interpretation.",
      "domain": "Review",
