      "explanation": "A potential energy curve is a graph of an object's potential energy (PE) versus position (x). The negative slope at any point gives the conservative force: F = -dU/dx. Minima in the curve represent stable equilibrium points (force zero, restoring); maxima represent unstable equilibrium (force zero, destabilizing). For a simple spring: PE_s = (1/2)k·x^2 is an upward parabola with minimum at x = 0 — the equilibrium position. For gravity near Earth: PE_g = mgh is a straight line with slope mg — gravity pulls down (F = -dU/dh = -mg). On Regents items, students read PE curves to identify equilibrium positions, find turning points (where total energy equals PE, so KE = 0), and determine the direction of force from slope. The shape of a PE curve encodes the dynamics: 'walking down a slope' on the curve gains KE; 'walking up' loses KE. Bound motion occurs in PE wells between turning points.",
