      "explanation": "Ohm's law (V = I·R, Georg Ohm 1827) is the foundational circuit relation; the NYS Reference Tables list V = IR, P = IV, P = I^2R, P = V^2/R. For a series circuit: I is the same through all components, V drops add to source voltage, R_eq = sum of R. For parallel circuits: V is the same across all branches, I splits according to I_i = V/R_i, 1/R_eq = sum of 1/R. To solve a mixed circuit: identify innermost series/parallel groupings, compute equivalent resistance step by step, find total current from V_source/R_eq, then work outward to find each component's V and I using V = IR. Power dissipation in each resistor follows P = I^2·R (more current = more power for same R) and total power = I_total·V_source. On cumulative Regents items, students analyze simple series, simple parallel, and mixed circuits, applying Ohm's law and Kirchhoff's rules at each step.",
