      "explanation": "Inertia is the tendency of an object to maintain its current velocity (whether at rest or moving uniformly) in the absence of a net force. The greater the mass, the greater the inertia — a loaded truck is harder to accelerate or decelerate than an empty one. Newton's first law (the law of inertia, 1687) states that an object's velocity remains constant unless a net force acts on it; this overturned the Aristotelian view that motion requires continuous force. Galileo first articulated the modern concept of inertia in his analysis of frictionless horizontal motion. Mass, measured in kilograms, is the quantitative measure of inertia. On Regents items, students recognize that a passenger continues forward when a car brakes (inertia keeps them moving) and slides outward in a turning car (inertia keeps them moving in a straight line while the car turns).",
