      "topic": "Force Basics",
      "explanation": "Mass is the scalar measure of how much matter an object contains and how much it resists changes in motion (inertia). SI unit is the kilogram. Mass is intrinsic to the object — it does not change with location, gravity, or motion (at non-relativistic speeds). A 5 kg object has 5 kg of mass on Earth, on the Moon, in deep space, and at the bottom of the ocean. Mass is distinguished from weight, which is the gravitational force on the object (W = mg) and changes with location: a 5 kg object weighs 49 N on Earth but only about 8 N on the Moon. On Regents items, mass appears in F = ma (inertial mass), W = mg (gravitational mass — Einstein's equivalence principle states these are identical), p = mv (momentum), and KE = (1/2)mv^2 (kinetic energy). Mass is conserved in chemical and ordinary physical processes.",
