      "topic": "Force Basics",
      "explanation": "Force is a vector interaction between objects that can change motion (accelerate, decelerate, redirect) or deform an object. The SI unit is the newton (N), defined as 1 kg·m/s^2 — the force required to accelerate a 1 kg mass at 1 m/s^2. Newton's three laws (Principia, 1687) define and quantify force: (1) an object's velocity changes only when a net force acts on it; (2) F_net = ma; (3) every force has an equal and opposite reaction. Forces always arise from interactions between two objects: gravitational (Earth on book), normal (table on book), tension (rope on box), friction (floor on shoe), spring (Hooke), electric (Coulomb), magnetic (current-carrying wires). On the Regents, students draw free-body diagrams listing all forces on a single object, then apply F_net = ma component-wise.",
