      "topic": "Force Basics",
      "explanation": "The net force (F_net) is the vector sum of every individual force acting on an object: F_net = F_1 + F_2 + ... . By Newton's second law, F_net = ma, so an object accelerates in the direction of F_net with magnitude a = F_net/m. When F_net = 0, the object has zero acceleration and either remains at rest or moves at constant velocity (Newton's first law — equilibrium). On Regents items, students draw a free-body diagram, decompose each force into x and y components, sum each component separately, then apply F_net,x = m·a_x and F_net,y = m·a_y. A common distractor confuses net force with the largest individual force; only the vector sum matters. For an object sliding down a frictionless incline, the net force is the component of gravity along the slope: F_net = mg·sin(theta), giving a = g·sin(theta).",
