      "topic": "Force Problems",
      "explanation": "When multiple objects are connected (by ropes, contact, pulleys), they move together with the same acceleration — the system acceleration. The standard approach: (1) treat the system as one object with total mass m_total, (2) sum only external forces (internal tensions cancel within the system), (3) apply F_net,external = m_total·a. For an Atwood machine with masses m_1 > m_2 on an ideal pulley: F_net = (m_1 - m_2)g, m_total = m_1 + m_2, so a = (m_1 - m_2)g/(m_1 + m_2). For two blocks pushed together horizontally (block 1 against block 2) with applied force F on block 1: a = F/(m_1 + m_2). The contact force between blocks (an internal force) is then F_contact = m_2·a. On Regents items, students often solve a connected-system problem twice: once for system acceleration, then for internal tensions or contact forces.",
