      "explanation": "An inclined plane at angle theta to the horizontal is a standard Regents geometry. Gravity (mg, downward) decomposes into a component parallel to the slope (mg·sin(theta), directed down the slope) and a component perpendicular to the slope (mg·cos(theta), into the surface, balanced by the normal force). For a frictionless incline, F_net = mg·sin(theta) along the slope, giving acceleration a = g·sin(theta) independent of mass. With kinetic friction (mu_k), the friction force is f_k = mu_k·N = mu_k·mg·cos(theta), opposing motion. If the block slides down: F_net = mg·sin(theta) - mu_k·mg·cos(theta), so a = g(sin theta - mu_k cos theta). On Regents items, students tilt their coordinate system to align x-axis with the slope, simplifying the force decomposition. Galileo used inclined planes to slow down free fall and measure acceleration accurately in the early 1600s.",
