      "correctText": "charged particle path",
      "topic": "Forces on Charges",
      "explanation": "A charged particle moving in a magnetic field follows a curved path. When velocity is entirely perpendicular to B, the path is a circle of radius r = m·v/(q·B) (from setting magnetic force equal to centripetal force: q·v·B = m·v^2/r). When v has both perpendicular and parallel components to B, the path is a helix (perpendicular component circles; parallel component moves straight through). The magnetic force is always perpendicular to velocity, so it does no work and the speed is constant — only direction changes. Period of circular motion: T = 2·pi·m/(q·B), independent of speed (cyclotron period). This is the principle of cyclotrons (1929, Lawrence) and mass spectrometers. On Regents items, students compute the radius of circular motion for given charge, mass, speed, and field; identify the curvature direction using the right-hand rule; and recognize that the path is a circle for v perpendicular to B.",
