      "explanation": "The motor effect is the force experienced by a current-carrying conductor in a magnetic field: F = B·I·L·sin(theta), where I is the current, L is the conductor's length in the field, and theta is the angle between the current direction and B. The NYS Reference Tables list F = B·I·L (with theta = 90°). Direction is given by the right-hand rule: fingers along I direction, curl toward B; thumb shows F direction. This is the basis of electric motors: a current loop in a magnetic field experiences a torque that causes it to rotate. A commutator (split-ring connector) reverses the current direction every half-rotation, maintaining continuous torque in the same direction. On Regents items, students compute force from F = B·I·L, identify direction with the right-hand rule, and explain motor operation as repeated motor-effect torques on the rotating coil. Discovered in 1820 (Oersted observed current deflecting compasses); first practical motor built by Faraday in 1821.",
