      "explanation": "Magnetic force on a moving charged particle is F = q·v·B·sin(theta), where q is charge, v is speed, B is field magnitude, and theta is the angle between v and B. The force is always perpendicular to both v and B (vector cross product F = q·v × B). The direction is given by the right-hand rule (or left-hand for negative charges): point fingers in v direction, curl toward B; thumb points in F direction for positive charge (reverse for negative). A charge moving parallel to B (theta = 0) experiences zero force; perpendicular (theta = 90°) experiences maximum force. The force does no work (always perpendicular to v) — so magnetic force changes direction of motion but not speed. SI unit: F in newtons, requires B in tesla. For an electron (q = -e) moving rightward in an upward B field, F is into the page (right-hand rule for q, reverse for negative).",
