      "topic": "Forces on Charges",
      "explanation": "A mass spectrometer is an instrument that separates ions by their mass-to-charge ratio (m/q) using a magnetic field. Ions are accelerated through a potential difference, gaining KE = q·V, so v = sqrt(2qV/m). They then enter a region of uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to their velocity. The magnetic force bends them into a circular path of radius r = m·v/(q·B). Ions with different m/q follow different curvature radii, separating spatially. Mass spectrometers are used in chemistry (identifying molecular masses), nuclear physics (measuring isotope abundances), forensics, and astronomy. The Hubble Space Telescope's UV spectrometer and many planetary probes use mass spectrometers. On Regents items, students apply the radius formula r = m·v/(q·B), recognize that heavier ions (or those with smaller charge) curve less for the same speed, and combine acceleration kinematics with magnetic-force circular motion to find r in terms of V and B.",
