      "correctText": "right-hand rule",
      "topic": "Forces on Charges",
      "explanation": "The right-hand rule is a mnemonic for determining vector directions in electromagnetism. Several variants exist: (1) For magnetic force on a moving positive charge: point fingers in v direction, curl toward B; thumb shows F direction. (2) For magnetic field around a current-carrying wire: thumb along current direction (conventional current, from + to -), fingers curl in field direction. (3) For the field through a current loop: fingers curl in current direction, thumb shows the direction of B through the loop's axis (north pole side). (4) For force on a current-carrying wire in a field: same as variant (1) with current direction instead of v. For negative charges or electron current, reverse the result (or use the left-hand rule). On Regents items, students apply the right-hand rule to find force direction on charges, current direction in induced loops (Lenz's law), and field direction around currents. Mastery of the rule is essential for Unit 8.",
