      "correctText": "solenoid",
      "topic": "Forces on Wires",
      "explanation": "A solenoid is a long coil of wire wound in a helical (corkscrew) shape. When current flows through it, the solenoid produces a strong, nearly uniform magnetic field inside (along the central axis) and a weaker field outside (resembling a bar magnet). For a tightly wound solenoid with N turns per length L, the interior field is B = mu_0·n·I (where n = N/L turns per meter). Field lines exit one end (north pole) and re-enter the other (south pole), then continue through the interior to complete loops. Solenoids are used as electromagnets in: relays, valves, magnetic locks, scrap-yard cranes, MRI machines (very large solenoids producing strong uniform fields). On Regents items, students recognize a solenoid as 'a current loop multiplied by many turns,' identify N and S poles using the right-hand rule (curl fingers in current direction; thumb points to N), and explain that placing iron inside greatly increases B (iron's high permeability).",
