      "correctText": "electromagnet",
      "topic": "Forces on Wires",
      "explanation": "An electromagnet is a solenoid with an iron (or other ferromagnetic) core, designed to produce a strong magnetic field that can be turned on and off by switching the current. Iron's high magnetic permeability (mu_iron / mu_0 ~ 1000-5000) dramatically amplifies the field inside the solenoid compared to an air core, making electromagnets thousands of times stronger for the same current and number of turns. Applications: scrap-yard cranes (huge electromagnets lift cars, drop them when current is cut); doorbells and electric bells (electromagnet pulls a hammer to strike a chime); relays and contactors (small current activates a switch for large currents); MRI machines; transformers; loudspeakers. On Regents items, students explain how an electromagnet works (current through a coil + iron core), identify N/S poles using the right-hand rule, and contrast with permanent magnets (electromagnets have on/off control and adjustable strength).",
