      "topic": "Free Body Diagrams",
      "explanation": "An applied force (F_app or F_a) is any external push or pull on an object that is not gravity, normal, tension, friction, or another standard force — typically the force a person, motor, or external agent exerts directly on the object. On a free-body diagram, the applied force is drawn at its actual angle (horizontal, vertical, or inclined). When applied at an angle theta above horizontal, F_app has components F_app·cos(theta) horizontal and F_app·sin(theta) vertical; the vertical component reduces the normal force, which in turn reduces friction (f_k = mu_k·N where N = mg - F_app·sin(theta) for upward-angled pull). On Regents items, applied forces are the most common 'unknown' or 'given' in F_net = m·a problems. Students must always check whether the applied force has components in multiple directions and decompose accordingly. The applied force is the user-controlled term in the force balance.",
