      "explanation": "Friction is the contact force parallel to a surface that opposes relative sliding (kinetic friction) or impending sliding (static friction). Static friction adjusts up to a maximum value f_s,max = mu_s·N before sliding begins; kinetic friction is f_k = mu_k·N once sliding occurs, with mu_k typically less than mu_s. The NYS Reference Tables lists coefficients of friction for several material combinations (e.g., rubber on dry concrete, steel on steel). Friction always acts opposite to the direction of motion (or impending motion) and is parallel to the contact surface, never perpendicular. On Regents items, students compute f_k from f = mu_k·N, where N may not equal mg if there is a vertical applied force or incline. Friction converts mechanical energy to heat, explaining energy loss in real systems. Air resistance is a type of fluid friction.",
