      "explanation": "Tension (T) is the pulling force transmitted along a rope, string, cable, or chain when stretched taut. Tension is the same throughout an ideal (massless, inextensible) rope and pulls equally on whatever is attached at each end. SI unit is the newton. On Regents items, common tension problems include: hanging weights (T = mg in static equilibrium); Atwood machines (two masses on a pulley — T is the same on both sides for ideal pulley); inclined plane systems; a sign suspended from two ropes at different angles (decompose each tension into x and y, then solve the equilibrium equations). A pulley redirects the tension but does not change its magnitude in the ideal case. If a rope can support up to a certain breaking tension, problems often ask the maximum acceleration or load before failure. Tension is always along the rope's length and pulls inward at each end.",
