      "topic": "Free Body Diagrams",
      "explanation": "A free-body diagram (FBD) isolates one object and shows every force acting on it as a labeled arrow originating from the object's center, drawn to scale and in the correct direction. Forces from outside (gravity, normal force, friction, tension, applied force, air resistance) are included; internal forces and forces the object exerts on other objects are not. The FBD enables application of Newton's second law: sum forces in x and y, then set each component sum equal to m·a_x or m·a_y. On Regents items, students must include only the forces actually acting (no fictitious forces in inertial frames), label each clearly (W or mg for weight, N for normal, T for tension, f for friction, F_app for applied), and orient them correctly. A common error: drawing centripetal force as a separate arrow — it is the net force on an object in circular motion, not an additional applied force.",
