      "correctText": "static friction",
      "topic": "Friction and Surfaces",
      "explanation": "Static friction (f_s) acts on stationary surfaces and opposes the tendency to slide. Unlike kinetic friction, static friction is variable: it equals exactly the applied force opposing it (so the object remains stationary) until the applied force exceeds the maximum static friction f_s,max = mu_s·N. At that point, the surfaces begin sliding and kinetic friction takes over. A 50 N applied force on a 10 kg block with mu_s = 0.6 and N = 98 N: f_s,max = 58.8 N, so the 50 N force is fully balanced by 50 N of static friction and the block does not move. If the applied force rises to 70 N, it exceeds f_s,max = 58.8 N, the block begins sliding, and kinetic friction (smaller than f_s,max) takes over. On Regents items, students determine whether an object slides by comparing applied force to f_s,max.",
