      "correctText": "electric generator",
      "topic": "Generators and Transformers",
      "explanation": "An electric generator converts mechanical (rotational) energy into electrical energy by rotating a coil in a magnetic field (or rotating a magnet near a stationary coil). As the coil rotates, the flux through it changes sinusoidally (Φ = B·A·cos(omega·t)), inducing a sinusoidal EMF (Faraday's law): EMF(t) = N·B·A·omega·sin(omega·t), giving alternating current (AC). The frequency equals the rotation rate (60 Hz in US power grids, 50 Hz in Europe). At every commercial power plant — coal, gas, nuclear, hydroelectric, wind, geothermal — mechanical energy (from steam turbines, water flow, or wind) spins a generator that produces electricity. Solar PV is the exception (direct photovoltaic conversion). On Regents items, students explain generator operation as Faraday induction, distinguish from motors (which use the motor effect in reverse), and recognize that all bulk electricity production is induction-based. First generator built by Michael Faraday in 1831 as a disk generator.",
      "domain": "Review",
      "gradeBand": "Multi",
      "subjectArea": "Review",
