      "prompt": "Transformer with fewer turns on the secondary than primary, decreasing voltage and increasing current.",
      "choices": [
        "step-down transformer",
        "normal force change",
        "alternating current",
        "transformer"
      ],
      "correctText": "step-down transformer",
      "topic": "Generators and Transformers",
      "explanation": "A step-down transformer has fewer turns on the secondary than on the primary: N_s < N_p, giving V_s < V_p. By power conservation: I_s > I_p — the current increases by the same ratio that voltage decreases. Step-down transformers are used throughout the power distribution grid: at substations (reducing 230 kV to 13.8 kV for local distribution); on neighborhood utility poles (the cylinder-shaped transformer that takes 13.8 kV down to 240/120 V for residential service); inside electronic devices (a wall-wart power adapter typically steps 120 V AC down to 5 V or 12 V before rectifying to DC); in welding equipment (low voltage, very high current). On Regents items, students apply V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p to find secondary voltage from turns ratio, and recognize step-down transformers as the workhorses of the power-distribution last-mile and of consumer electronics.",
