      "topic": "Generators and Transformers",
      "explanation": "A step-up transformer has more turns on the secondary coil than on the primary: N_s > N_p, giving V_s > V_p. By power conservation (ideal case): I_s < I_p — the current decreases by the same ratio that voltage increases. Step-up transformers are used at electric power plants to raise the voltage from the generator's output (typically 10-30 kV) to transmission-line levels (115 kV, 230 kV, 500 kV, or even 765 kV). Higher voltage means lower current for the same power, and power losses on transmission lines (P_loss = I^2·R) are minimized by reducing current. Without step-up transformation, long-distance power transmission would be infeasible due to enormous I^2·R losses. On Regents items, students apply V_s/V_p = N_s/N_p to find the secondary voltage given the primary voltage and turns ratio, and recognize the trade-off: voltage up, current down, power approximately constant.",
