      "correctText": "position-time graph",
      "topic": "Graph Meanings",
      "explanation": "A position-time (x-t) graph plots an object's position on the vertical axis against time on the horizontal axis. The slope dx/dt at any instant equals the instantaneous velocity, and the slope of a secant line between two points gives the average velocity. A horizontal line indicates an object at rest; a straight tilted line indicates constant velocity; a curve indicates acceleration. A parabola opening upward represents uniform positive acceleration (as Galileo first demonstrated for free fall in his inclined plane experiments). On Regents items, students must read slope correctly (with units of m/s), identify when an object reverses direction (where slope changes sign), and distinguish position graphs from velocity graphs — a common distractor. The shape of an x-t graph encodes the full motion story without requiring algebra.",
