      "correctText": "velocity-time graph",
      "topic": "Graph Meanings",
      "explanation": "On a velocity-time (v-t) graph, the slope dv/dt at any point equals instantaneous acceleration, and the area under the curve between two times equals the displacement over that interval (positive area = positive displacement; negative area = displacement in the opposite direction). A horizontal v-t line means constant velocity (zero acceleration); a straight tilted line means uniform acceleration. The NYS Reference Tables kinematic equations all derive geometrically from v-t graph analysis. On Regents items, students are routinely asked to compute displacement as the trapezoidal area under a v-t graph or to identify acceleration from the slope. A common distractor confuses the y-intercept (initial velocity) with the slope (acceleration). Galileo's geometric reasoning in the 1638 Two New Sciences is the historical origin of this technique.",
