      "correctText": "area under graph",
      "topic": "Graph Meanings",
      "explanation": "The area under a graph encodes an accumulated quantity that depends on the vertical axis: area under a velocity-time graph equals displacement (m); area under an acceleration-time graph equals change in velocity (m/s); area under a force-time graph equals impulse (N·s = kg·m/s); area under a force-displacement graph equals work (J); area under a power-time graph equals energy delivered (J). On Regents items, students compute these as triangles, rectangles, or trapezoids from gridded data. A common distractor confuses area (displacement) with slope (acceleration) on a v-t graph. The integral interpretation underlies the kinematic equation d = v_i·t + (1/2)at^2, which is geometrically the area under a linearly increasing v-t line — a result Galileo derived geometrically before Newton's calculus.",
