      "correctText": "slope",
      "topic": "Graph Meanings",
      "explanation": "Slope on a motion graph carries a specific physical meaning based on the axes: on a position-time graph the slope equals velocity (m/s); on a velocity-time graph the slope equals acceleration (m/s^2); on a force-displacement graph the slope is the spring constant (N/m); on a current-voltage graph for an ohmic resistor, slope equals 1/R (conductance). Students must always check axis units before interpreting slope. On the NYS Reference Tables, many fundamental relationships (F = kx, V = IR, p = mv) appear as straight lines whose slopes equal a physical constant. A Regents item showing a non-linear position-time graph requires students to recognize that the instantaneous slope (tangent line) gives velocity, while average slope (secant line) gives average velocity over the chosen interval.",
