      "explanation": "Instantaneous velocity is the limit of average velocity as the time interval approaches zero — v(t) = dx/dt — and it gives the velocity at one specific instant rather than averaged over an interval. Graphically, it is the slope of the tangent line at one point on a position-time graph. A speedometer reads instantaneous speed (the magnitude of instantaneous velocity). On a curved x-t graph, average velocity between two points (slope of secant line) generally differs from the instantaneous velocity at either endpoint. On Regents items, students must distinguish the two: a parabolic x-t graph has an instantaneous velocity that increases with time, but an average velocity over [0,t] equal to half the final value if starting from rest. Newton's calculus, developed in the 1660s, made this limit rigorous.",
