      "correctText": "collision time",
      "topic": "Graphs and Data",
      "explanation": "Collision time (Δt) is the duration of contact during a collision. From the impulse-momentum theorem F_avg·Δt = Δp, for a fixed momentum change Δp, the average force F_avg is inversely proportional to Δt: F_avg = Δp/Δt. A baseball bat in contact with a ball for 0.001 s applies a much larger force than a foam paddle in contact for 0.1 s for the same momentum change. Collision times for hard-hard contact (steel-on-steel) are typically very short (milliseconds), producing large peak forces. Soft contacts (rubber, foam, padding) extend Δt, dramatically reducing peak forces — the principle behind airbags, helmets, and crumple zones. On Regents items, students use F = Δp/Δt to compute average force, recognize that doubling Δt halves F (for the same Δp), and explain the safety benefit of cushioning materials.",
