      "correctText": "peak force",
      "topic": "Graphs and Data",
      "explanation": "Peak force is the maximum instantaneous force achieved during a collision, occurring at the moment of maximum deformation/compression. For a sinusoidal or roughly triangular force-time pulse, peak force is typically about 2× the average force (F_peak ≈ 2·F_avg for a half-sine pulse; F_peak = 2·F_avg for a triangular pulse). For a hard, brief collision (steel ball on steel anvil), peak force can be enormous despite small impulse. Safety engineering aims to reduce peak force (which causes injury or material failure) by extending collision time, even though Δp and impulse remain the same. On Regents items, students may be asked to identify peak force as the highest point on an F-t graph and to compare it to the average force (rectangle of same area). A car's crumple zone reduces peak force on the passenger compartment by spreading the collision over a longer time and larger deformation distance.",
