      "correctText": "force-time graph",
      "topic": "Graphs and Data",
      "explanation": "A force-time (F-t) graph plots the applied force on the vertical axis against time on the horizontal axis. The area under the F-t curve between two times equals the impulse delivered: J = integral of F dt = area. For a constant force F applied for time Δt, the area is a rectangle: J = F·Δt. For a triangular F-t pulse (force rises linearly to a peak then falls back to zero, total time Δt, peak force F_peak): J = (1/2)·F_peak·Δt. The impulse-momentum theorem connects this to Δp = J. On Regents items, students compute impulse as the area under a gridded F-t graph (counting squares or using rectangle/triangle/trapezoid formulas), then equate to Δp to find the change in velocity. F-t graphs are the visual analog of v-t graphs (where area is displacement) — both encode an accumulated quantity via integration.",
