      "correctText": "area impulse",
      "topic": "Graphs and Data",
      "explanation": "Area under a force-time graph is impulse — the geometric interpretation of the impulse-momentum theorem. The units check: N · s = (kg·m/s^2)·s = kg·m/s, which is the unit of momentum. For a constant force: impulse = F · Δt = rectangle area = (height)(width). For a varying force: impulse = the irregular area under the curve, computed by adding rectangles, triangles, trapezoids, or counting grid squares. For two F-t graphs delivering the same impulse: a tall narrow rectangle and a short wide rectangle give the same Δp. On Regents items, this is the most common 'read-a-graph' impulse problem: students count area under a piecewise-linear F-t curve and equate to Δp = m(v_f - v_i) to find the final velocity. The technique is identical to finding displacement from a v-t graph, just with different physical quantities.",
