      "correctText": "gravitational force",
      "topic": "Gravity",
      "explanation": "Gravitational force is the attractive force between any two masses, given by Newton's universal law of gravitation (Principia, 1687): F_g = G·m_1·m_2/r^2, where G = 6.67 × 10^-11 N·m^2/kg^2 is the universal gravitational constant (NYS Reference Tables), m_1 and m_2 are the masses, and r is the distance between their centers. The force is attractive and acts along the line connecting them. Two masses each of 1 kg, 1 m apart, experience a force of only 6.67 × 10^-11 N — gravity is the weakest fundamental force at human scales. Near Earth's surface, F_g = m·g where g = G·M_Earth/R_Earth^2 ≈ 9.81 m/s^2. On Regents items, students compute gravitational force between planets, between satellites and Earth, and between objects with different separations. Newton's law unified terrestrial gravity (apple falling) with celestial gravity (Moon orbiting), one of the great unifications in physics.",
