      "correctText": "orbital speed",
      "topic": "Gravity",
      "explanation": "Orbital speed (v_orbit) is the speed at which a satellite must travel to maintain a circular orbit at radius r around a central mass M. From Newton's second law applied to circular motion: gravitational force = centripetal force, so G·M·m/r^2 = m·v^2/r, giving v_orbit = sqrt(G·M/r). For a satellite in low Earth orbit (r ≈ R_Earth ≈ 6.37 × 10^6 m): v ≈ 7.9 km/s (about 17,500 mph), period T ≈ 90 minutes. The Moon orbits Earth at v ≈ 1 km/s, r ≈ 384,000 km, T ≈ 27.3 days. Note: orbital speed depends on r but not on the satellite's mass (just as free-fall acceleration is independent of mass). On Regents items, students compute orbital speed from sqrt(G·M/r), find period via T = 2·pi·r/v_orbit, and connect to Kepler's third law T^2 = (4·pi^2/GM)·r^3.",
