      "explanation": "Object distance (d_o) is the distance from the object to the lens or mirror, measured along the principal axis. SI unit is the meter. In the standard sign convention: d_o is positive for objects on the incoming-light side of the optical element (the usual case). The object distance is the user-controlled input that determines image properties via the thin-lens/mirror equation: 1/f = 1/d_o + 1/d_i. When d_o approaches infinity (very distant object, like a star), the incoming rays are nearly parallel and 1/d_o → 0, so d_i → f — image forms at the focal point. When d_o = 2f, d_i = 2f and m = -1 (real, inverted, same size). When d_o = f, parallel rays emerge (no image at finite distance). When d_o < f, a virtual upright magnified image forms (magnifying glass case). On Regents items, students measure d_o, plug it into the lens equation, and identify which regime applies.",
