      "explanation": "Uniform (constant) acceleration means a is the same at every instant during the interval — the kinematics equations on the NYS Physics Reference Tables (v_f = v_i + at, d = v_i·t + (1/2)at^2, v_f^2 = v_i^2 + 2ad) apply only under this assumption. The most common uniform acceleration on the Regents is free-fall near Earth's surface, g = 9.81 m/s^2 downward, valid when air resistance is negligible. On a velocity-time graph, uniform acceleration appears as a straight line; on a position-time graph, as a parabola. Non-uniform acceleration (e.g., air drag, varying force) requires calculus and is beyond Regents scope. Galileo's inclined plane experiments demonstrated uniform acceleration empirically; Newton's second law F = ma explains it (constant net force produces constant acceleration on a constant mass).",
