      "correctText": "free fall",
      "topic": "Kinematics Equations",
      "explanation": "Free fall is motion governed solely by gravity, with no air resistance. Near Earth's surface, the NYS Reference Tables give g = 9.81 m/s^2 directed toward Earth's center (downward). A foundational result, demonstrated by Galileo from the Leaning Tower of Pisa (legend) and rigorously in the 1638 Two New Sciences: all objects fall with the same acceleration regardless of mass, because gravitational force F_g = mg and Newton's second law F = ma give a = g, independent of m. Apollo 15 astronaut David Scott famously demonstrated this on the Moon in 1971 by dropping a hammer and feather together. On Regents items, students apply the standard kinematics equations with a = -g (taking up as positive), recognize that an object thrown upward has v = 0 at the peak but still accelerates at g downward, and compute fall times and impact speeds for vertical drops.",
