      "correctText": "final velocity",
      "topic": "Kinematics Equations",
      "explanation": "Final velocity (v_f) is the velocity at the end of the chosen time interval and appears in v_f = v_i + at and v_f^2 = v_i^2 + 2ad on the NYS Reference Tables. For an object in free fall starting from rest, v_f = -gt (down negative). At the peak of a vertical throw, v_f = 0, even though the object is still accelerating downward at g = 9.81 m/s^2. Sign conventions are critical: a ball thrown up at 20 m/s reaches v_f = 0 at the peak, then has v_f = -20 m/s back at its launch height (same speed, opposite direction). Galileo first noted this symmetry in the 1638 Two New Sciences. On Regents items, students using v_f^2 = v_i^2 + 2ad must take the appropriate square root sign — the equation gives the magnitude, and direction must be argued from the physics.",
