      "correctText": "field-line pattern",
      "topic": "Magnetic Fields",
      "explanation": "Field-line patterns are visual representations of magnetic field structure. For a bar magnet: field lines emerge from the north pole, curve through space, and re-enter the south pole; inside the magnet, lines continue from S to N, completing closed loops. For a current-carrying straight wire: field lines form concentric circles around the wire (right-hand rule: thumb along current direction, fingers curl in field direction). For a current loop or coil: field lines pass through the coil's axis, emerging from one face (like a bar magnet's N) and entering the other (like S). For a solenoid: field lines are nearly uniform and parallel inside the coil, similar to those inside a bar magnet. Field-line density indicates field strength (closer lines = stronger field). On Regents items, students sketch field-line patterns for bar magnets, current-carrying wires, loops, and solenoids; identify directions; and infer pole orientations from given patterns. Iron filings reveal real field-line patterns experimentally.",
