      "correctText": "Doppler interpretation",
      "topic": "Magnetism and Waves",
      "explanation": "The Doppler effect (Christian Doppler, 1842) is the apparent frequency shift due to relative motion between source and observer. Approach: observed frequency > source frequency (compressed wavelengths). Recede: observed frequency < source frequency (stretched wavelengths). For sound: a fire truck siren rises in pitch as it approaches, then drops as it passes. For light: blue-shift (toward shorter lambda) for approaching stars/galaxies, red-shift for receding ones. Edwin Hubble (1929) measured red-shifts of distant galaxies, finding that recession velocity is proportional to distance — establishing the expanding universe model (Big Bang cosmology). The Doppler shift in sound (non-relativistic): f_obs = f_src·(v ± v_obs)/(v ∓ v_src). For light (relativistic for high speeds): f_obs = f_src·sqrt((1 - v/c)/(1 + v/c)) for recession. On cumulative Regents items, students predict whether observed frequency increases or decreases (approach raises f, recede lowers f) and explain the wavelength stretching/compressing mechanism qualitatively.",
