      "correctText": "wave-speed equation",
      "topic": "Magnetism and Waves",
      "explanation": "The wave-speed equation v = f·lambda (NYS Reference Tables) connects speed, frequency, and wavelength for any wave: sound, light, water, seismic, electromagnetic. Wave speed depends on the medium (~343 m/s for sound in air; c = 3.00 × 10^8 m/s for light in vacuum). When a wave crosses an interface, frequency stays the same (boundary condition) but v and lambda change proportionally. For sound at f = 1000 Hz in air (v = 343 m/s): lambda = 0.343 m; in water (v = 1480 m/s): lambda = 1.48 m. For light at f = 5 × 10^14 Hz (green) in vacuum: lambda = c/f = 600 nm; in glass (v = c/n with n = 1.5): lambda = 400 nm (shorter, because v slowed but f preserved). On cumulative Regents items, students apply v = f·lambda across wave types and across boundaries, computing any one quantity from the other two. The relationship is universal — from radio waves to gamma rays, from ocean swells to atomic vibrations.",
