      "explanation": "Right-hand-rule applications: (1) Magnetic field around a current-carrying wire — thumb along conventional current direction (+ to -), fingers curl in B direction. (2) Magnetic force on a moving positive charge — fingers in v direction, curl toward B, thumb gives F direction. (3) Force on a current-carrying wire in B field — fingers along I direction, curl toward B, thumb gives F. (4) Direction of B through a current loop or coil — fingers curl in current direction, thumb shows B's direction along the axis (which side is the 'N pole' of the loop). For negative charges or for electron current, reverse the result (use the left hand, or apply the rule normally and then flip the answer). On cumulative Regents items, students apply the right-hand rule to find force directions on charges in fields, determine which way current must flow to produce a given field, and identify the direction of induced current in a changing-flux scenario (combined with Lenz's law). Mastery is essential for all Unit 8 problems.",
