      "topic": "Mirrors and Lenses",
      "explanation": "Focal length (f) is the distance from a mirror or lens to its focal point — the point where parallel incident rays converge (converging optics, f positive) or appear to diverge from (diverging optics, f negative). SI unit is the meter, though optical instruments often use mm or cm. For a thin lens: f depends on the lens shape and refractive index n. For a spherical mirror: f = R/2, where R is the radius of curvature. The thin-lens/mirror equation relates object distance d_o, image distance d_i, and focal length: 1/f = 1/d_o + 1/d_i. Shorter focal lengths produce stronger bending (more powerful lens/mirror); longer focal lengths produce weaker bending. Camera lenses are characterized by focal length (e.g., 50 mm 'normal' lens, 200 mm telephoto, 24 mm wide-angle). On Regents items, students use f in the thin-lens equation, identify f as positive for converging or negative for diverging optics, and recognize that f is a fixed property of the lens or mirror.",
