      "explanation": "A converging lens (also called a convex lens or positive lens) has surfaces curved so that parallel light rays passing through converge to a focal point on the other side. Common shapes: biconvex (both sides curved outward), plano-convex (one side flat, one curved outward), meniscus (one concave, one stronger convex). Converging lenses produce real images when the object is beyond the focal point (used in cameras, projectors, eyeglasses for farsightedness, magnifying glasses when held far from the object) and virtual images when the object is within f (used as a magnifying glass to enlarge text or stamps). The thin-lens equation is the same form as for mirrors: 1/f = 1/d_o + 1/d_i. The human eye contains a converging lens that focuses light onto the retina. On Regents items, students apply the lens equation, compute magnification m = -d_i/d_o, and trace principal rays through lenses to construct images.",
