      "topic": "Momentum Basics",
      "explanation": "Change in momentum (Δp) is a vector quantity equal to the final momentum minus the initial momentum: Δp = m·v_f - m·v_i (assuming constant mass). For a 1 kg ball thrown at 10 m/s right that bounces off a wall and returns at 8 m/s left: Δp = (1)(-8) - (1)(10) = -18 kg·m/s — a substantial change due to the direction reversal. Note that even when |v| barely changes, a direction reversal produces a large Δp. The impulse-momentum theorem states that this change equals the net impulse on the object: Δp = F_net·Δt. On Regents items, students compute Δp with careful attention to vector signs (Δp can be much larger than mv_f alone if the direction reverses), then relate Δp to impulse to find force or time. This is the heart of collision analysis.",
