      "explanation": "The impulse-momentum theorem states that the net impulse on an object equals its change in momentum: F_net·Δt = Δp = m·v_f - m·v_i. This is Newton's second law in integral form (F = dp/dt integrated over time). The NYS Reference Tables list J = F·Δt = Δp. On Regents items, the theorem provides a powerful alternative to F = ma when forces vary or act briefly. For an egg dropped on the floor (Δt very small), F is large; on a pillow (Δt larger), F is smaller — the change in momentum (m·v) is the same, but the force depends on the stopping time. For a force-time graph, impulse equals the area under the curve, and equals Δp. The theorem underlies safety engineering: longer collision times reduce peak forces and injury risk.",
