      "explanation": "Momentum (p) is a vector quantity equal to mass times velocity: p = m·v. SI unit is kg·m/s (equivalent to N·s). The NYS Physics Reference Tables list this formula. Momentum carries direction — a 1000 kg car moving 20 m/s east has momentum 20,000 kg·m/s east. Newton's second law in its original form was stated by Newton in the 1687 Principia as F = dp/dt (force equals rate of change of momentum), reducing to F = ma for constant mass. The total momentum of a system is the vector sum of individual momenta. On Regents items, students compute momentum, recognize that doubling either mass or speed doubles momentum, and use momentum in collision and impulse problems. Momentum is conserved in any system with zero external net force (isolated system) — a foundational principle of physics from Newton through quantum mechanics and Einstein's relativity.",
