      "correctText": "system momentum",
      "topic": "Momentum Basics",
      "explanation": "System momentum is the vector sum of momenta of all objects in a chosen system: p_system = sum of m_i·v_i. The law of conservation of momentum states that the system's total momentum is constant if no external force acts on the system (isolated system). Internal forces (the objects interacting with each other via collision, explosion, spring, etc.) cannot change total momentum because they come in equal-opposite pairs (Newton's third law) — they cancel within the system. On Regents items, conservation of momentum is the key tool for collision problems: m_1·v_1i + m_2·v_2i = m_1·v_1f + m_2·v_2f (assuming straight-line motion). For 2D, both components are separately conserved. A cannon firing a cannonball: initial system momentum = 0, so afterward (cannon momentum) + (cannonball momentum) = 0, giving the cannon recoil velocity. Conservation of momentum is one of the most powerful laws in physics.",
