      "explanation": "The friction model on the NYS Reference Tables: kinetic friction f_k = mu_k·N (constant magnitude for sliding surfaces, opposite to motion direction); static friction f_s ≤ mu_s·N (variable up to maximum, opposite to impending motion direction). The friction coefficients (NYS table) depend on the material pair (e.g., rubber on dry concrete mu_k ≈ 0.68). Normal force N may not equal mg if there are vertical applied forces or inclined planes — for a horizontal surface with weight only, N = mg; on an incline at angle theta, N = mg·cos(theta); with an upward-angled applied force F_app at angle phi, N = mg - F_app·sin(phi). Friction converts mechanical energy to heat: W_friction = -f·d (negative work, energy goes into thermal energy of surfaces). On cumulative Regents items, students calculate N first, then compute friction, then use in F_net = ma equations. Static-vs-kinetic distinction: if F_applied < mu_s·N, no sliding; if F_applied > mu_s·N, sliding begins with reduced kinetic friction.",
