      "explanation": "Free-body diagram (FBD) construction requires careful choice of which forces to include: only forces acting ON the chosen object (not forces it exerts on other objects). Standard forces: gravity (weight, mg downward, acts on every mass); normal force (perpendicular to contact surface, away from surface); tension (along ropes/cables, pulling); friction (parallel to contact surface, opposing relative motion or tendency); applied force (from external agents). Newton's third-law pairs always act on DIFFERENT objects, so only ONE member of each pair appears on a given object's FBD. Common errors: drawing 'centripetal force' as a separate arrow (it's the SUM of inward forces); including forces the object exerts on others; using vectors of incorrect length or direction. On cumulative Regents items, students construct FBDs and use them as the starting point for net-force calculations, ensuring all forces are real and correctly oriented.",
