      "correctText": "projectile component",
      "topic": "Motion and Forces",
      "explanation": "Projectile motion is decomposed into independent horizontal and vertical components — Galileo's principle of independence (1638). Horizontal: v_x = v·cos(theta), constant throughout flight (no horizontal force); displacement x = v_x·t. Vertical: v_y(t) = v·sin(theta) - g·t, changing under gravity; displacement y = v·sin(theta)·t - (1/2)g·t^2. At the peak, v_y = 0 but v_x is unchanged. Maximum range on level ground occurs at launch angle 45°. The trajectory is a parabola. The two components share only one variable: time t. Galileo first proved the parabolic trajectory in his 1638 Two New Sciences. On cumulative Regents items, students decompose initial velocity into components, find time of flight from vertical kinematics, then find range from x = v_x·t_total, and identify peak height from y_max = (v·sin theta)^2/(2g).",
