      "explanation": "Equivalent resistance (R_eq) is the single resistance value that would behave identically to a network of resistors at the terminals: V/I_total at the terminals = R_eq. For resistors in SERIES (end-to-end): R_eq = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + ... (current is the same through each; voltages add). For resistors in PARALLEL (side-by-side, same two endpoints): 1/R_eq = 1/R_1 + 1/R_2 + 1/R_3 + ... (voltages are equal; currents add). For two resistors in parallel: R_eq = R_1·R_2/(R_1 + R_2) (product over sum). Adding a resistor in series increases R_eq; adding one in parallel decreases R_eq. On Regents items, students compute R_eq for series, parallel, and mixed configurations by working step-by-step from the innermost combinations outward, then apply Ohm's law to the entire circuit using R_eq.",
