      "explanation": "Photoelectric threshold: the minimum frequency of incident light required to eject electrons from a particular metal surface, f_0 = W/h (NYS Physics Reference Tables provide values of W for common metals). Below f_0, no photoelectrons are emitted regardless of intensity — a fundamental signature of quantum behavior (energy comes in discrete photons of energy h·f, not in arbitrarily small amounts). Above f_0, the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons is KE_max = h·f - W (Einstein equation, 1905, Nobel 1921). For cesium (W ≈ 2.1 eV = 3.36 × 10^-19 J): f_0 = W/h = 5.07 × 10^14 Hz, corresponding to lambda_0 = c/f_0 ≈ 590 nm (yellow) — visible light suffices to eject photoelectrons from cesium. For zinc (W ≈ 4.3 eV): f_0 ≈ 1.04 × 10^15 Hz, lambda_0 ≈ 288 nm — only UV light works. On cumulative Regents items, students compute f_0 from W/h, determine whether given light frequency exceeds f_0, and find KE_max above threshold.",
