      "explanation": "A short circuit is a circuit path of very low (near-zero) resistance, drawing very large current from the source. By Ohm's law (I = V/R), as R → 0, I → infinity (limited by source capacity). Short circuits are usually unintentional and dangerous: a damaged wire touching a metal case can create a short across the battery, drawing huge current that can melt wires, start fires, or damage components. Household circuit breakers and fuses protect against short circuits by tripping when current exceeds a safe limit (typically 15-20 A in residential circuits). In a parallel circuit, a short across one branch effectively places that branch at zero resistance: all the source current flows through the short (the parallel combination is dominated by the smallest R). On Regents items, students recognize a short circuit by R = 0 across two points, predict that all current diverts through the short, and explain the safety role of circuit breakers.",
