      "explanation": "Kepler's first law (1609) states that planetary orbits are ellipses with the Sun at one focus. An ellipse is characterized by its semimajor axis (a, longest radius) and eccentricity (e, from 0 = circle to less than 1 = elongated ellipse). For Earth, e ≈ 0.017 (nearly circular). For comets, e can be close to 1 (highly elongated). For Mercury, e ≈ 0.206 (most elliptical of the planets). At perihelion (closest approach), the orbiting body moves fastest; at aphelion (farthest), slowest — Kepler's second law (1609) makes this quantitative (equal areas swept in equal times). Newton (Principia, 1687) derived all three Kepler laws from his inverse-square gravitational law, showing that any closed orbit under inverse-square attraction is an ellipse (circles being a special case with e = 0). On Regents items, students recognize that orbital speed varies along an ellipse and that the central body sits at one focus, not the center.",
