      "explanation": "A central force is a force whose direction always points along the line connecting two interacting objects, with magnitude depending only on their separation. Gravitational force (F_g = G·m_1·m_2/r^2) and electrostatic force (F = k·q_1·q_2/r^2) are the two foundational central forces in physics. Central forces conserve angular momentum (a key consequence is Kepler's second law, equal areas in equal times). Newton (1687) showed that an inverse-square central force produces closed elliptical orbits with the source at one focus. Other central forces (e.g., a spring connecting two masses) produce different orbital shapes. On Regents items, central forces are usually discussed implicitly: students apply F_g = G·m_1·m_2/r^2 along the line connecting the masses and use the centripetal-force condition for circular orbits. The 1/r^2 form of Newtonian gravity is what makes elliptical orbits stable and closed; small deviations would cause orbits to precess.",
