      "correctText": "photoelectric effect",
      "topic": "Quantum Ideas",
      "explanation": "The photoelectric effect is the emission of electrons from a metal surface when light strikes it — but only if the light's frequency exceeds a threshold value (the threshold frequency f_0) characteristic of the metal. Below f_0, no electrons are emitted regardless of intensity. Above f_0, the maximum kinetic energy of emitted electrons depends on the frequency (linearly), not the intensity: KE_max = h·f - W, where W is the work function (minimum energy to extract an electron). The NYS Physics Reference Tables list this Einstein relation. Light intensity affects only the NUMBER of emitted electrons, not their KE. This behavior contradicts classical wave theory of light (which predicts that more intensity should give more KE), confirming that light interacts with electrons as discrete photons of energy E = h·f. Einstein's 1905 explanation (Nobel Prize 1921) was the first quantitative quantum-mechanical theory and launched modern physics. On Regents items, students compute KE_max = h·f - W and recognize f_0 = W/h.",
