      "correctText": "wave-particle duality",
      "topic": "Quantum Ideas",
      "explanation": "Wave-particle duality is the principle that all quantum entities — light, electrons, atoms, molecules — exhibit BOTH wave-like properties (interference, diffraction, frequency, wavelength) AND particle-like properties (discrete energy, momentum, localized impacts) depending on the experiment. For light: wave-like behavior is shown in Young's double-slit experiment (1801, interference fringes); particle-like behavior is shown in the photoelectric effect (1905, discrete photons of energy h·f) and Compton scattering. For matter: Louis de Broglie (1924, Nobel 1929) proposed that all matter has an associated wavelength lambda = h/p, confirmed by electron diffraction (Davisson-Germer 1927). Modern quantum mechanics (Heisenberg, Schrödinger 1925-1926) incorporates wave-particle duality as fundamental: quantum entities are neither classical waves nor classical particles but obey wavefunction-based probabilistic rules. On Regents items, students recognize both behaviors in different experiments and apply E = h·f or lambda = h/p as appropriate.",
