      "correctText": "scalar quantity",
      "topic": "Regents Measurement",
      "explanation": "A scalar is a physical quantity that is fully specified by its magnitude (a number with units). Examples include mass, time, temperature, distance, speed, energy, work, power, electric potential, and charge. Scalars combine by ordinary algebraic addition (5 kg + 3 kg = 8 kg). Compare to vectors, which require both magnitude and direction. On the NYS Reference Tables, scalar quantities are typeset in plain (non-bold) variables. A Regents item asking for total energy adds joules arithmetically; one asking for total displacement requires vector addition because displacement is a vector. Common student confusions: temperature is a scalar (no direction), but heat transfer can be assigned a direction in problem-setting; mass is scalar, but weight is a force vector. Distinguishing scalars from vectors is foundational for projectile, momentum, and electric-field problems.",
