      "topic": "Regents Measurement",
      "explanation": "A vector is a physical quantity specified by both magnitude and direction. Examples include displacement, velocity, acceleration, force, momentum, impulse, electric field, and magnetic field. Vectors are added geometrically using the head-to-tail or parallelogram method, or algebraically by decomposing into perpendicular components (typically x and y). On the NYS Reference Tables, vectors are typeset in bold or with arrow overlines. Two vectors of equal magnitude can sum to zero (if antiparallel) or to twice their magnitude (if parallel). On Regents items, students must use vector addition (not arithmetic) when combining forces, velocities, or fields. The magnitude of a resultant vector formed by two perpendicular components a and b is sqrt(a^2 + b^2), with direction given by arctan(b/a). Vector reasoning underlies free-body diagrams, projectile motion, and Coulomb-force superposition.",
