      "topic": "Regents Measurement",
      "explanation": "The resultant is the single vector equivalent to the sum of two or more vectors. Two perpendicular vectors of magnitudes A and B have a resultant of magnitude sqrt(A^2 + B^2) at angle arctan(B/A) from A. For non-perpendicular vectors, students decompose each into x and y components, sum the components separately (R_x = sum of A_x; R_y = sum of A_y), then combine: |R| = sqrt(R_x^2 + R_y^2). On Regents items, common resultant problems include: combining displacement vectors (a person walks 4 m east then 3 m north — resultant 5 m at 37°); finding net force on an object pulled by two ropes; computing total velocity of a boat crossing a current (velocity of boat plus velocity of water); and finding net electric field from two point charges. The resultant has the same physical effect as the sum of the individual vectors.",
