      "correctText": "free-body circular diagram",
      "topic": "Regents Traps",
      "explanation": "A free-body diagram for circular motion shows all real forces acting on the object: gravity, normal force, tension, friction, applied force, etc. The KEY constraint is that the vector sum of these real forces must point toward the center of the circle and have magnitude m·v^2/r — this provides the centripetal acceleration. Common mistake: drawing an additional 'centripetal force' arrow as if it were a separate, distinct force; it is NOT — it is the net result of the other forces already on the diagram. For a car on a banked turn: gravity (down) and normal force (perpendicular to road surface) combine to produce a net horizontal inward component supplying centripetal force, with no friction needed if the bank angle matches the speed. For an object on a vertical loop: tension/normal and gravity combine differently at top and bottom. On Regents items, students draw the FBD, identify each force, then show that the vector sum is radial-inward with magnitude m·v^2/r.",
