      "correctText": "acceleration direction",
      "topic": "Regents Traps",
      "explanation": "For an object in uniform circular motion (constant speed), acceleration is ALWAYS directed radially inward (toward the center of the circle) — this is centripetal acceleration. The direction is perpendicular to the instantaneous velocity (which is tangential). At constant speed, there is no tangential acceleration; all acceleration is centripetal. If speed is increasing or decreasing (non-uniform circular motion), there is a tangential acceleration component in addition to the centripetal component, but the centripetal component remains radial. On Regents items, students must identify acceleration as pointing toward the center, NOT in the direction of motion. A common error is drawing acceleration along the velocity vector (which would be tangential). For circular motion, velocity tangential and acceleration radial are always perpendicular. Newton's second law gives F_net = m·a_c radially inward.",
