      "explanation": "For an object in circular motion at constant speed, the net force is directed radially inward (toward the center of the circle). By Newton's second law: F_net = m·a_c radially inward, where a_c = v^2/r. This net force is supplied by the vector sum of real forces (gravity, tension, normal, friction, etc.) on the object — there is no separate 'centripetal force' arrow on a free-body diagram. For a car turning on a flat road, the net inward force is friction. For a ball on a string in a horizontal circle, the net force is the horizontal component of tension. For a planet orbiting the Sun, the net force is gravity. On Regents items, students draw a complete free-body diagram with all real forces, then identify that the SUM of those forces points toward the circle's center (and equals m·v^2/r). The 'centripetal' label describes the direction (inward) of the net force, not a new force type.",
