      "correctText": "air bag",
      "topic": "Safety Applications",
      "explanation": "An airbag is an inflatable cushion in a vehicle that deploys rapidly during a crash to reduce the peak force on the occupant by extending the collision time. The physics: the occupant has momentum p = m·v immediately before impact. To bring p to zero, the same impulse F·Δt = Δp must be delivered, regardless of mechanism. Without an airbag, the head/chest hits the steering wheel or dashboard with a very short Δt (milliseconds) and very large F. With an airbag, Δt is extended to ~100 ms and F decreases proportionally — typically by 5-10×. Combined with seatbelts (which spread force over the chest and pelvis), airbags dramatically reduce injury severity. On Regents items, this is the canonical real-world impulse-momentum theorem application: 'Explain why an airbag reduces injury' (answer: it extends collision time, reducing peak force for the same change in momentum).",
