      "explanation": "A crumple zone is a deformable structural region at the front and rear of a vehicle designed to crush in a controlled way during a collision. The physics rationale: by extending the distance and time over which the vehicle decelerates, the average force on the passenger compartment is reduced (F = Δp/Δt). Modern car bodies have a rigid 'safety cell' surrounding passengers and deliberately weak (or progressively crushable) crumple zones outside it. In a head-on collision, the crumple zones absorb energy via plastic deformation (converting KE to internal energy of bent steel), reducing both peak force and total energy transferred to occupants. On Regents items, crumple zones illustrate the impulse-momentum theorem (extend Δt to reduce F) and conservation of energy (KE dissipated as deformation work). Combined with airbags and seatbelts, crumple zones make modern vehicles dramatically safer than mid-20th-century cars.",
