      "explanation": "Stopping time is the time required to bring a moving object to rest: from F_avg·Δt = Δp = m·v (assuming final v = 0), we get Δt = m·v/F_avg. A car braking from 30 m/s under a friction force of 7000 N (m = 1000 kg): Δt = (1000)(30)/7000 ≈ 4.3 s. A skydiver opening a parachute uses drag force to extend stopping time and reduce peak force. On Regents items, stopping time problems combine momentum and impulse with safety design: increasing stopping time reduces peak force on occupants (for the same Δp). Anti-lock brakes reduce stopping distance but may not reduce stopping time as much as one might expect; the safety advantage is maintaining steering control. Stopping time is closely related to stopping distance (Δd = average velocity × Δt = (v_i/2)·Δt for uniform deceleration).",
