      "explanation": "Same current through every component is the defining property of a series circuit. By conservation of charge, the current that flows into one component must equal the current flowing out of it; in a series circuit with no branching, this means the same current flows through the entire loop. This rule applies regardless of how the components differ: a 100 Ω resistor and a 5 Ω resistor in series have the same current through them, just different voltages (V = I·R) and different powers (P = I^2·R, with the larger R consuming more power for the same I). On Regents items, students use 'same current in series' as their entry point: find total R_eq, compute I = V/R_eq, then use I in V = I·R or P = I^2·R for each individual resistor. A common Regents trap: assuming different currents in series components — this is incorrect.",
