      "explanation": "Total resistance in a series circuit is the simple algebraic sum of individual resistances: R_total = R_1 + R_2 + R_3 + ... The NYS Reference Tables list this rule. Adding resistors in series always increases the total resistance, decreasing the current the source can drive. Three 10-Ω resistors in series give R_total = 30 Ω; with a 12-V battery, the current is I = V/R_total = 12/30 = 0.4 A. Common applications: voltage dividers (two resistors in series produce intermediate voltages); current limiting (a series resistor limits the maximum current through a sensitive component); LED protection (a resistor in series with an LED limits current to safe levels). On Regents items, students compute R_total for series networks, then use it to find total current from V_source/R_total. A common error: treating series total resistance like parallel (1/R sum) — the rule for series is simple addition.",
