      "explanation": "An open circuit is a circuit with a break (gap, broken wire, burned-out component, or open switch) that prevents the flow of current. Effectively R = infinity at the break. In a series circuit, a single open anywhere stops current everywhere in the loop. This is why old-style Christmas lights were notorious: one burned-out bulb darkened the entire string. Modern bulbs include shunting elements that maintain conductivity when the filament breaks. Open circuits are intentional in switches (the open position interrupts the circuit). On Regents items, students recognize that an open circuit produces zero current and voltmeter readings across the gap equal to the source voltage (all of V_source 'drops' across the open). When troubleshooting, an open in series with other components stops all current; an open in parallel with other components removes that branch but doesn't stop overall current.",
