      "correctText": "resonance",
      "topic": "Sound",
      "explanation": "Resonance occurs when an oscillating system is driven at one of its natural (resonant) frequencies, producing large-amplitude oscillation. Energy from the driver accumulates in the system because each driving cycle reinforces the existing oscillation (constructive feedback). Examples: pushing a child on a swing in time with the swing's natural period builds large amplitude; an opera singer shattering a glass by singing at the glass's resonant frequency; the 1940 collapse of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge due to wind-driven resonance (oscillation amplified to destruction); tuning forks vibrating in sympathy; the body of a guitar amplifying string vibrations via resonant sound holes; microwave ovens heating water via 2.45 GHz resonance with water-molecule rotational modes. The sharper the resonance (less damping), the larger the amplitude buildup at the resonant frequency. On Regents items, students recognize resonance as 'driver frequency matches natural frequency,' identify examples (musical instruments, swing, bridges), and explain energy buildup.",
