      "correctText": "sound wave",
      "topic": "Sound",
      "explanation": "A sound wave is a longitudinal mechanical wave consisting of alternating compressions (high-pressure regions) and rarefactions (low-pressure regions) in a medium. Sound travels at ~343 m/s in air at 20°C, increasing with temperature (about 0.6 m/s per °C) and faster in denser/stiffer media (~1480 m/s in water, ~5000 m/s in steel). Human hearing range: 20 Hz (lowest perceptible bass) to 20 kHz (highest treble, declining with age). Below 20 Hz is infrasound (e.g., elephant calls, earthquake P-waves); above 20 kHz is ultrasound (medical imaging, bat echolocation, dog whistles). Sound cannot travel through a vacuum (no medium), unlike light. On Regents items, students recognize sound as longitudinal, compute lambda = v/f for sound, identify the human hearing range, and contrast sound (mechanical, requires medium) with light (electromagnetic, no medium needed). The speed of sound in air was first measured accurately in the 1700s by experiments timing cannon-fire flashes and bangs over known distances.",
