      "correctText": "loudness",
      "topic": "Sound",
      "explanation": "Loudness is the perceptual quality of sound corresponding to its amplitude (or, more precisely, its intensity I = P/area, with SI units of W/m^2). Because human perception of sound intensity is roughly logarithmic, the decibel (dB) scale is used: sound intensity level = 10·log_10(I/I_0), where I_0 = 10^-12 W/m^2 is the threshold of hearing. Every 10 dB increase represents a 10× increase in intensity. Reference levels: 0 dB (threshold of hearing); 60 dB (normal conversation); 80 dB (busy street); 100 dB (rock concert); 120 dB (jet engine, threshold of pain); 140 dB (immediate hearing damage). Loudness perception depends on both intensity and frequency (the human ear is most sensitive around 1-5 kHz). On Regents items, students relate loudness to amplitude (energy proportional to A^2), recognize that decibels are logarithmic, and identify dB levels for common sources. Sustained exposure above 85 dB causes hearing damage.",
