      "explanation": "Grounding is the connection of a conductor to Earth (or to a very large reservoir of charge), allowing excess charge to flow to or from the conductor to neutralize it. Earth, being so large, can absorb or supply any practical amount of charge without significantly changing its own potential. Grounding is used in: (1) Lightning rods (channel lightning current safely to Earth); (2) Electrical safety (the third prong of a US three-prong plug connects the appliance casing to ground, so any short circuit causes the breaker to trip rather than shocking the user); (3) Charging by induction (grounding removes induced like charges, leaving the conductor with opposite net charge after the ground is removed). The ground symbol in circuit diagrams is three downward-pointing horizontal lines. On Regents items, students apply grounding in induction problems and recognize its role in electrical safety. Benjamin Franklin's 1752 kite experiment led to lightning rods using grounding.",
