      "explanation": "An electroscope is a sensitive instrument for detecting and measuring static electric charge. The classic design consists of two thin metal foil leaves (usually gold) hanging from a metal rod inside a glass jar (to prevent air currents from disturbing them). The metal terminal extends through the jar's top. When charge is added to the terminal (by touching it with a charged object or by induction), the charge spreads to the leaves; the leaves carry like charges and repel each other, spreading apart. The angle of spread is proportional to the magnitude of charge — more charge means wider spread. An electroscope can determine the sign of an unknown charge: if a known-sign charged object is brought near a leaves-spread electroscope, the spread increases for same-sign and decreases (or reverses) for opposite-sign charge. On Regents items, students explain how an electroscope works, predict leaf behavior in induction and contact charging scenarios, and use it to distinguish charge signs.",
