      "explanation": "The trajectory of a projectile is the curved path it follows through space. Under gravity alone (air resistance neglected), Galileo proved in the 1638 Two New Sciences that this path is a parabola. The trajectory equation, obtained by eliminating t from x = v_x·t and y = v_iy·t - (1/2)g·t^2, is y = (tan theta)x - [g/(2 v^2 cos^2 theta)] x^2 — a quadratic in x, confirming a parabola. The shape depends on the launch angle and speed but not on the projectile's mass (a key consequence of the equivalence between gravitational and inertial mass). On Regents items, students must recognize that at any point on the trajectory, the velocity is tangent to the parabola, with horizontal component constant and vertical component changing by -g·t. Real-world trajectories (baseballs, artillery shells) deviate from parabolas due to air drag.",
