      "topic": "Two-Dimensional Motion",
      "explanation": "Range (R) is the horizontal distance traveled by a projectile from launch to landing. For a projectile launched and landing at the same height with initial speed v and angle theta on level ground (no air resistance), R = (v^2 sin(2theta))/g. This expression is maximized when sin(2theta) = 1, i.e., theta = 45°. Complementary launch angles (e.g., 30° and 60°) give equal ranges because sin(60°) = sin(120°). On Regents items, students more often compute range from t_total = 2v·sin(theta)/g, then R = v·cos(theta)·t_total. For a horizontally launched projectile (theta = 0), the simpler formula does not apply — students must use t = sqrt(2h/g) for the fall time, then R = v_x·t. Galileo's parabolic trajectory result (1638) is the foundation; Newton's mechanics generalizes it to any inverse-square gravitational field.",
