      "correctText": "minimum speed",
      "topic": "Vertical Circles",
      "explanation": "Minimum speed in a vertical loop is the lowest speed at which the object can maintain contact with the track (or maintain tension in a string) at the top of the loop. At the top: gravity and normal force both point downward (toward center), so m·g + N = m·v^2/r. For minimum speed, N = 0 (just barely maintaining contact), giving v_min^2 = g·r, so v_min = sqrt(g·r). For a roller coaster loop of radius 10 m: v_min = sqrt(9.81 × 10) ≈ 9.9 m/s at the top. Below this speed, the car would lose contact with the track and follow a parabolic path. On Regents items, students compute v_min for various loop sizes, find the minimum height to enter the loop (using energy conservation: starting height must produce v_min at the top, so h_start ≥ 2.5·r above the bottom of the loop), and recognize that at minimum speed, gravity alone supplies the centripetal force.",
