      "topic": "Vertical Circles",
      "explanation": "At the top of a vertical loop (e.g., a roller coaster, a ball on a string), the centripetal direction is downward (toward the center of the loop), so both gravity and the normal force (or tension) point downward and combine to supply the centripetal force: m·g + N = m·v^2/r. Solving: N = m·v^2/r - m·g. For the object to maintain contact with the track, N ≥ 0, so v^2 ≥ g·r, giving v_min = sqrt(g·r) at the top. At minimum speed, N = 0 and gravity alone supplies the centripetal force. On Regents items, this is the classic roller coaster loop problem: 'What is the minimum speed at the top to maintain contact?' The answer: v_min = sqrt(g·r). For a ball on a string, T + mg = mv^2/r at the top; if v_top < sqrt(gr), the string goes slack.",
