      "correctText": "apparent weight",
      "topic": "Vertical Circles",
      "explanation": "Apparent weight is the reading on a scale on which a person stands, equal to the normal force the scale exerts on the person: W_apparent = N. In an inertial (non-accelerating) frame at rest, N = m·g (true weight). When the system accelerates: in an upward-accelerating elevator, N - m·g = m·a (Newton's second law), so N = m(g + a) > m·g — apparent weight increases. In a downward-accelerating elevator (a downward): N = m(g - a) < m·g. In free fall (a = g downward): N = 0 — apparent weightlessness. At the bottom of a vertical loop: N = m(g + v^2/r). At the top of a vertical loop: N = m(v^2/r - g). On Regents items, students compute apparent weight in elevators, in loops, and on banked turns, recognizing that apparent weight reflects the normal force, not the gravitational force (which is unchanged).",
