      "explanation": "The normal force varies continuously around a vertical circle. At the top: N = m·v^2/r - m·g (smallest, can be zero or negative meaning loss of contact). At the bottom: N = m·v^2/r + m·g (largest). At the sides (horizontal positions): N must supply the full centripetal force, but gravity is tangent to the circle (not radial), so N = m·v^2/r at those points (with gravity affecting the tangential acceleration, not the radial). Speed also varies due to energy conservation in a frictionless loop: v_bottom > v_top. Combining: v_bottom^2 = v_top^2 + 4·g·r (from PE = 2·g·r between top and bottom). So N_bottom = m·v_bottom^2/r + m·g = m·(v_top^2 + 4gr)/r + m·g = m·v_top^2/r + 5·m·g. At minimum, v_top^2 = g·r, so N_bottom = m·g·(1 + 5) = 6·m·g — riders feel 6× their weight at the bottom of a minimum-speed loop.",
