      "correctText": "wavelength",
      "topic": "Wave Basics",
      "explanation": "Wavelength (lambda) is the spatial distance over which a wave repeats itself — typically measured from one crest to the next, from one trough to the next, or from any point on the wave to the next identical point one cycle later. SI unit is the meter (m). For visible light, lambda ranges from about 380 nm (violet) to 750 nm (red). For audible sound in air at room temperature, lambda ranges from 1.7 cm (20 kHz, high pitch) to 17 m (20 Hz, low pitch). The wave speed equation (NYS Physics Reference Tables) connects wavelength, frequency, and speed: v = f·lambda, so lambda = v/f. Doubling frequency at constant speed halves the wavelength. On Regents items, students compute lambda from v/f for sound, light, radio, and water waves; identify lambda on wave diagrams (crest to crest); and apply the relationship across different wave types.",
