      "topic": "Wave Basics",
      "explanation": "Frequency (f) is the number of complete wave cycles passing a fixed point per second. SI unit is the hertz (Hz), defined as 1 cycle per second (s^-1). For waves: v = f·lambda (NYS Reference Tables). Audible sound: 20 Hz - 20 kHz (human hearing range, narrows with age). Visible light: ~400-790 THz (THz = 10^12 Hz). AM radio: ~530-1700 kHz. FM radio: 88-108 MHz. WiFi: 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz. Microwaves: ~1-300 GHz. Higher frequency = more cycles per second = shorter period (T = 1/f) and shorter wavelength (lambda = v/f). On Regents items, students compute f from v/lambda or 1/T, classify electromagnetic waves by frequency (radio, microwave, IR, visible, UV, X-ray, gamma), and recognize that frequency does not change when a wave crosses a boundary (only speed and wavelength change). Heinrich Hertz (1887) generated and detected the first radio waves, confirming Maxwell's electromagnetic theory; the unit is named in his honor.",
