      "explanation": "Wave speed (v) is the rate at which a wave's pattern propagates through a medium. The fundamental wave equation: v = f·lambda (NYS Physics Reference Tables). Wave speed depends primarily on the medium, not on the wave's frequency or amplitude. Some standard speeds: sound in air at 20°C: ~343 m/s; sound in water: ~1480 m/s; sound in steel: ~5000 m/s; light in vacuum: c = 3.00 × 10^8 m/s (NYS Reference Tables); light in water: ~2.25 × 10^8 m/s (slowed by index of refraction n = 1.33). Mechanical wave speeds increase with stiffness/density of the medium. Electromagnetic wave speed in vacuum is the universal constant c. When a wave crosses from one medium to another, frequency stays the same (boundary condition) but speed and wavelength both change. On Regents items, students compute v from f·lambda, recognize speed depends on medium, and apply the rule that f is preserved across interfaces while v and lambda change.",
