      "correctText": "period",
      "topic": "Wave Basics",
      "explanation": "Period (T) is the time required for one complete wave cycle to pass a given point. SI unit is the second. T is the reciprocal of frequency: T = 1/f (NYS Reference Tables). A 60-Hz AC current has period T = 1/60 ≈ 16.7 ms. Middle-C sound (~262 Hz) has T ≈ 3.8 ms. Yellow light (~5 × 10^14 Hz) has T ≈ 2 × 10^-15 s. Period is also the time for one full cycle in any periodic phenomenon (pendulum, spring oscillation, circular motion at constant rate). On Regents items, students compute T from 1/f, recognize T as a temporal property (vs lambda as spatial), and apply the relationship to waves of all types. A high-frequency wave has a short period; a low-frequency wave has a long period. Period and wavelength are different quantities (period is time-based, wavelength is space-based); they are connected through the wave speed: v = lambda/T = f·lambda.",
