      "correctText": "refraction",
      "topic": "Wave Behaviors",
      "explanation": "Refraction is the bending of a wave as it passes from one medium into another, caused by a change in wave speed. Snell's law (Willebrord Snell, 1621): n_1·sin(theta_1) = n_2·sin(theta_2), where n is the index of refraction (n = c/v for light) and theta is measured from the normal. When a wave enters a slower medium, it bends TOWARD the normal (smaller angle); entering a faster medium, it bends AWAY from the normal. The frequency is preserved across the boundary (same cycles per second); the wavelength changes proportionally to speed: lambda = v/f. For visible light passing from air (n ≈ 1) to water (n = 1.33), light slows by ~33% and bends toward the normal. Refraction underlies prisms (dispersion separates colors), lenses (focusing), mirages (refraction in temperature-graded air), and rainbows (refraction + reflection in water droplets, separately by wavelength). On Regents items, students apply Snell's law with NYS Reference Tables values for n.",
