      "correctText": "reflection",
      "topic": "Wave Behaviors",
      "explanation": "Reflection is the bouncing back of a wave from a boundary surface. The law of reflection: angle of incidence (measured from the normal — perpendicular to the surface) equals angle of reflection. Specular (mirror-like) reflection occurs on smooth surfaces; diffuse reflection on rough surfaces scatters in many directions. Examples: light bouncing off a mirror (specular); sound echoing off a canyon wall; water waves rebounding off a sea wall. Total reflection occurs when no wave transmits through the boundary (e.g., total internal reflection in optics, when light hits a glass-air boundary above the critical angle). On Regents items, students apply the equal-angle rule to determine reflected wave directions, draw the normal first then measure both angles from it, and recognize that reflection preserves frequency, speed, and wavelength (medium unchanged). The law of reflection holds for all wave types — light, sound, water, seismic — and has been known since antiquity (Euclid's Optics, c. 300 BCE).",
