      "explanation": "An electromagnetic (EM) wave is a self-propagating oscillation of electric and magnetic fields that requires no material medium to travel. Maxwell (1864) showed that changing electric fields produce magnetic fields and vice versa, allowing EM waves to propagate through vacuum at the speed c = 3.00 × 10^8 m/s (NYS Reference Tables). Heinrich Hertz (1887) generated and detected the first radio waves, confirming Maxwell's theory. The EM spectrum spans (from low to high frequency): radio < microwave < infrared < visible (400-750 nm) < ultraviolet < X-ray < gamma. All EM waves travel at c in vacuum but slow down in materials (refractive index n = c/v). EM waves are transverse — electric and magnetic fields oscillate perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation. They carry energy and momentum (basis of radiation pressure). On Regents items, students recognize EM waves as needing no medium (unlike sound), travel at c in vacuum, and span a wide spectrum from radio to gamma.",
