      "correctText": "Liquid",
      "topic": "Particle Model",
      "explanation": "A liquid is a state of matter with definite volume but no definite shape; it takes the shape of its container. Liquid particles are still close together but can slide past each other, so liquids can flow. Examples include water, milk, and oil. When liquid water freezes at 32°F (0°C) it becomes ice; when it boils at 212°F (100°C) it becomes steam. NYS Grade 5 Standard 5-PS1-1 expects students to model that all matter, including liquids, is made of tiny particles too small to be seen with the unaided eye.",
