      "correctText": "Carbon dioxide",
      "topic": "Plants Make Food",
      "explanation": "Plants pull carbon dioxide (CO2) from the surrounding air through tiny leaf openings called stomata, then use it as the carbon source for sugar during photosynthesis. NYS 5-LS1-1 specifically asks students to support the claim that plants get materials they need for growth chiefly from air and water, not from soil. Belgian researcher Jan Baptista van Helmont's 1640s willow-tree experiment showed a plant gained 164 pounds while the soil lost only 2 ounces, which became one of the first pieces of evidence for this idea.",
