      "explanation": "Capillaries are the smallest blood vessels, about 5-10 micrometers across—just wide enough for a single red blood cell to squeeze through. Their walls are only one endothelial cell thick, allowing oxygen, glucose, CO2, and waste to diffuse rapidly. Marcello Malpighi first observed capillaries in frog lungs in 1661, confirming William Harvey's predicted link between arteries and veins. The human body has 100,000 km of capillaries. NYS Grade 6 Science (NGSS MS-LS1-3) requires students to identify capillaries as the diffusion site and Malpighi 1661 as the historic confirmation of closed circulation.",
