      "explanation": "Earth's surface gravity is approximately 9.8 m/s^2, meaning a falling object speeds up by 9.8 m/s every second of fall. Galileo Galilei established this constant by dropping objects from the Tower of Pisa (legendary) and rolling balls down inclined planes around 1604, showing all objects fall at the same rate (in vacuum) regardless of mass. The value of g varies slightly with altitude and latitude (poles ~9.83, equator ~9.78). NYS Grade 6 Science (NGSS MS-ESS1-3) requires the 9.8 m/s^2 value and Galileo's free-fall experiments.",
