      "explanation": "Troubleshooting (debugging in software) is the systematic process of finding the cause of a failure: isolate variables, check each subsystem, test hypotheses. The term 'debugging' originated in 1947 when Grace Hopper's team found an actual moth in Harvard's Mark II relay computer. Engineers use fault trees, root-cause analysis (the '5 Whys' technique, developed by Sakichi Toyoda for Toyota), and signal tracing. NGSS MS-ETS1-3 requires students to analyze test data to identify failure modes. NYS Grade 6 Science requires troubleshooting documentation.",
