      "topic": "Modern Evolution",
      "explanation": "Speciation is the evolutionary process by which one ancestral population splits into two reproductively isolated species. Ernst Mayr's 1942 biological species concept defines species as groups that can interbreed and produce fertile offspring. The four modes are allopatric (geographic isolation, like Galapagos finches), sympatric (within the same area, like apple maggot flies switching host plants), peripatric (small isolated population), and parapatric (along environmental gradient). Madeleine Beekman's 2013 work documented sympatric speciation in cichlid fish in Cameroon's Lake Barombi Mbo (11 endemic species in 1 small crater lake). NYS Grade 8 Science exams test speciation as the creator of biodiversity.",
