      "explanation": "Adaptive radiation is the rapid diversification of a single lineage into many ecologically distinct species, typically following arrival in a new environment or after a mass extinction. George Gaylord Simpson defined it in his 1944 Tempo and Mode in Evolution. Classic examples include Darwin's finches (13-18 species from one Galapagos ancestor in ~2-3 million years), Hawaiian honeycreepers (~50 species from one ancestor), Hawaiian silverswords (28 plant species), East African cichlid fish (~600 species in Lake Victoria in ~14,000 years—Erik Verheyen 2003). Mammalian radiation after the K-Pg extinction (66 mya) gave rise to bats, whales, primates from common ancestors. NYS Grade 8 Science exams test radiation with the Galapagos finches.",
