      "topic": "Modern Evolution",
      "explanation": "A mutation is a heritable change in DNA sequence, the ultimate source of genetic variation upon which natural selection acts. Hugo de Vries coined the term in 1901 from observing sudden trait changes in Oenothera evening primroses. Mutations include point mutations (single base change), insertions, deletions, and chromosomal rearrangements. Most mutations are neutral or harmful, but rare beneficial mutations drive evolution. Sickle-cell mutation (described by James Herrick, 1910) is deleterious in homozygotes but protective against malaria in heterozygotes (Anthony Allison, 1954)—a textbook balanced polymorphism. Mutation rate in humans: ~64 new mutations per child (1000 Genomes Project, 2010-15). NYS Grade 8 Science exams test mutations as the source of variation.",
