      "explanation": "The peppered moth (Biston betularia) is a textbook example of natural selection. Pre-Industrial Revolution England had light-colored bark with lichens; moths were predominantly light-colored, camouflaged from bird predators. By 1848 near Manchester, soot from coal burning blackened tree bark and killed lichens; dark-colored (carbonaria) moths became dominant—from <1% in 1848 to >95% by 1895. H.B.D. Kettlewell's 1953 mark-recapture experiments documented the predation rate. After the 1956 Clean Air Act in the UK, lichens recovered and light moths returned to dominance. Michael Majerus's 2007 study replicated Kettlewell's findings. NYS Grade 8 Science exams use peppered moths to demonstrate selection in action.",
