      "correctText": "Stabilizing selection",
      "topic": "Natural Selection",
      "explanation": "Stabilizing selection favors intermediate phenotypes and acts against extremes, narrowing the trait distribution around an optimum. The concept was formalized by Sergei Chetverikov (1926) and Theodosius Dobzhansky (1937). Classic example: human birth weight, studied by Mary Karn and Sheldon Penrose (1951) on 6,693 London births—babies of average weight (~7-8 lbs) had highest survival; very small or very large infants had higher mortality. Other examples: bird clutch size (David Lack, 1947), giraffe neck length (consistent at ~2 meters in adults). Contrasts with directional selection (one extreme favored) and disruptive selection (both extremes favored). NYS Grade 8 Science exams use birth weight as the example.",
