      "explanation": "Due process, guaranteed by the 5th and 14th Amendments, requires the government to follow fair legal procedures before depriving any person of life, liberty, or property. Procedural due process demands fair hearings and notice; substantive due process limits which rights government can restrict at all, as in Roe v. Wade (1973) and Obergefell v. Hodges (2015). Students often confuse it with equal protection—due process is about how the government acts, while equal protection is about who it treats differently.",
